// Generated by scripts/gen-l10n.mjs (gdetail n5, en) — 不要手改;改翻譯請改 scripts/out/l10n/en/ 再重 build。
window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N=window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N||{};window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N["en"]=Object.assign(window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N["en"]||{},{"n5-54":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>These three patterns put actions in <b>order</b>, and they're as easy to grasp as their English equivalents:<br><span class='gd-jp'>〜てから</span>＝\"do ~ first, then ~\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>〜たあとで</span>＝\"after ~\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>〜まえに</span>＝\"before ~\"<br>Think of telling a friend about your day: brush your teeth, then go to bed; watch TV after dinner; check the weather before heading out.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>~ first, then ~</span>Verb <b>te-form</b> + から.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>磨く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>磨いて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>磨いてから</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べてから</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>してから</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>歯を磨いてから、寝ます。</span>(I brush my teeth, then go to bed.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>手を洗ってから、食べてください。</span>(Please wash your hands first, then eat.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>after ~</span>Verb <b>ta-form</b> + あとで. The ta-form is just the te-form with 「て」 changed to 「た」 and 「で」 changed to 「だ」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見て</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見た</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見たあとで</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだあとで</span></span><br>After a noun, add 「の」 in between: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>仕事</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>仕事のあとで</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>映画を見たあとで、ご飯を食べました。</span>(After watching the movie, I ate.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>仕事のあとで、ビールを飲みます。</span>(After work, I have a beer.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>before ~</span>Verb <b>dictionary form</b> (the base form you find in a dictionary) + まえに.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寝る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寝るまえに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くまえに</span></span><br>After a noun, add 「の」 in the same way: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食事</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食事のまえに</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寝るまえに、シャワーを浴びます。</span>(I take a shower before going to bed.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>食事のまえに、手を洗います。</span>(I wash my hands before eating.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Before まえに, the verb is <b>always in dictionary form</b>, even when you're talking about the past: <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ来るまえに、英語を勉強しました。</span> (I studied English before coming to Japan.) You can't say 「来たまえに」.<br>・Before あとで, the verb is <b>always in ta-form</b>, even when the event hasn't happened yet: <span class='gd-jp'>食べたあとで、歯を磨きます。</span> (I'll brush my teeth after eating.) You can't say 「食べるあとで」.<br>・Whether the whole sentence is past or present is decided <b>by the end of the sentence</b> (磨きます／磨きました). The first half doesn't change with it.<br>・With nouns, it's easy to drop 「の」: say <span class='gd-jp'>授業のあとで</span>, not 「授業あとで」.<br>・When <span class='gd-jp'>あとで</span> stands alone with nothing before it, it means \"later\": <span class='gd-jp'>あとで電話します。</span> (I'll call you later.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>てから and あとで both mean \"B happens after A is done,\" and often either one works. The difference is in the nuance:<br><span class='gd-jp'>お風呂に入ってから、ご飯を食べます。</span> (<b>First</b> I take a bath, <b>and then</b> I eat. This stresses the order: I don't eat until I've bathed.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>お風呂に入ったあとで、ご飯を食べます。</span> (I eat after my bath. This just places the meal later in time.)<br>When you want the other person to be sure to do A first, てから sounds the most natural:<br><span class='gd-jp'>電話してから来てください。</span> (Please call first, then come over.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for \"do ~ first, then ~\" use <b>てから</b>; for simply \"after ~\" use <b>たあとで</b>; for \"before ~\" use <b>まえに</b>, always with the dictionary form in front.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>When てから is followed by a length of time, it means \"it's been ~ since ...\": <span class='gd-jp'>日本に来てから、もう三年です。</span> (It's already been three years since I came to Japan.)</div>","n5-58":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ながら」 means <b>\"while doing ~, ...\"</b>. Japanese has one habit, though: <b>the more important action goes last</b>. In \"studying while listening to music,\" studying is what you're really doing and the music is just background, so Japanese says <span class='gd-jp'>音楽を聞きながら勉強します。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in ます-form, <b>drop 「ます」</b>, and add 「ながら」. It works the same for all three verb groups.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>聞きます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>聞きながら</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べながら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見ながら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>運転します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>運転しながら</span></span><br>Then add the main action. 「ながら」 itself <b>never changes</b>. Tense, negation and politeness are all left to the final verb.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>歌を歌いながら、料理しました。</span>(I cooked while singing.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Two things at the same moment</span>You're doing this and that at the same time.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>テレビを見ながら、ご飯を食べます。</span>(I eat while watching TV.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>歩きながらスマホを見るのは危ないです。</span>(Looking at your phone while walking is dangerous.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Both going on over a period of time</span>It doesn't have to be the same second. It can also mean two things running in parallel during this stretch of your life, like working while studying.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>アルバイトをしながら、日本語を勉強しています。</span>(I'm studying Japanese while working a part-time job.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Attach it to the <b>ます-form minus ます</b>, not the dictionary form: <span class='gd-jp'>聞きながら</span> is right, and you can't say 「聞くながら」; <span class='gd-jp'>食べながら</span> is right, and you can't say 「食べるながら」.<br>・Both actions must be done by the <b>same person</b>. \"I was watching TV while Mom was cooking\" involves two people, so you can't use ながら. Use 「とき」 instead: <span class='gd-jp'>母が料理をしているとき、私はテレビを見ていました。</span><br>・Don't swap the two halves freely like you can in English. In Japanese, <b>the second action is the main one</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>勉強しながら音楽を聞きます</span> sounds like \"I'm mainly listening to music and studying on the side.\"<br>・Past tense and politeness change only the final verb. Don't put ました before ながら: you can't say 「聞きましたながら」. Say <span class='gd-jp'>聞きながら勉強しました</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Te-form linking</b> = finish one thing, <b>then</b> do the next, so there's an order.<br><b>ながら</b> = two things happen <b>at the same time</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ご飯を食べて、テレビを見ます。</span>(I eat, then watch TV.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ご飯を食べながら、テレビを見ます。</span>(I watch TV while eating.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜たり〜たりします</b> = giving examples of things you do. They aren't done at the same time.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末は本を読んだり、映画を見たりします。</span>(On weekends I do things like reading books and watching movies.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: doing both at once is ながら, finishing one and then doing the next is te-form, and listing a few example activities is たり〜たり.</div>","n5-62":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English, “the door opened” and “I opened the door” use the same verb, “open”. Japanese <b>switches to a different verb</b>: for “the door opens” you use <span class='gd-jp'>開く</span>, and for “someone opens the door” you use <span class='gd-jp'>開ける</span>. The first kind is an <b>intransitive verb</b>, the second is a <b>transitive verb</b>, and the particle in front has to change with them.</div><div class='gd-h'>Get the two names straight</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Intransitive + が</span>It only says what state something changes into, without caring who caused it. The particle before it is <b>が</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ドアが開きます。</span>(The doors are about to open: an announcement you often hear on trains)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Transitive + を</span>It says someone does something to a thing, and the thing being acted on takes <b>を</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ドアを開けます。</span>(I open the door)</div><div class='gd-sec'>A simple test: is there a doer acting on the thing? “I open the door” has a doer and an object, so use transitive + を. “The door opens” just happens by itself, with nobody acting on it, so use intransitive + が.</div><div class='gd-h'>The most common pairs</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電気がつきました。</span>(The light came on)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電気をつけてください。</span>(Please turn on the light)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>授業が始まります。</span>(Class is about to start)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>授業を始めます。</span>(Let's start the class)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>財布が落ちました。</span>(The wallet fell)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>財布を落としました。</span>(I dropped my wallet)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are a few more pairs. The left one is intransitive and the right one is transitive:<br><span class='gd-jp'>閉まる</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>閉める</span> (close)<br><span class='gd-jp'>消える</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>消す</span> (go out / turn off)<br><span class='gd-jp'>入る</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>入れる</span> (go in / put in)<br><span class='gd-jp'>出る</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>出す</span> (go out / take out)<br>No single rule tells you which is which in every case, so it's best to <b>memorize them pair by pair with example sentences</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy places to slip</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Match the particle with the verb: <span class='gd-jp'>電気を消します</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>電気が消えます</span> are both correct, but 「電気を消えます」 is wrong.<br>・When you ask someone to do something, use the transitive verb: <span class='gd-jp'>窓を閉めてください</span>. You can't say 「窓が閉まってください」.<br>・Here <span class='gd-jp'>開く</span> is read <b>あく</b>. The same kanji also has the reading 「ひらく」, which is used differently, so don't mix them up for now.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>入る</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>出る</span> often appear with places: <span class='gd-jp'>部屋に入ります</span> (enter a room) uses に. In <span class='gd-jp'>家を出ます</span> (leave the house), the を marks the “place you leave”, not a “thing being acted on”, so 出る is still intransitive.<br>・Intransitive verbs aren't only for things that “happen naturally”: <span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>寝る</span> are also intransitive, and they are actions people do themselves. What matters is <b>whether there is an object being acted on</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Tell it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Adding 「〜ています」 to each kind of verb gives very different meanings:<br><span class='gd-jp'>窓が開いています。</span> (The window is open) → just describes what you see<br><span class='gd-jp'>夏はいつも窓を開けています。</span> (In summer I always keep the window open) → talks about an action a person does, a habit</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if you're only seeing a state like “open, on, closed”, use 「が＋intransitive ています」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>A transitive verb plus 「〜てあります」 means “someone did it on purpose and left it that way”: <span class='gd-jp'>窓が開けてあります。</span> (The window has been opened on purpose)</div>","n5-15":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「は」 works like English <b>“as for ~, …”</b>. You put the thing you want to talk about at the front of the sentence, as if to say “this is what we're talking about now”, and then say what it's like. <span class='gd-jp'>私は学生です。</span> is “as for me, I'm a student”, which is just “I'm a student”.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Just add は after a noun:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>私</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>私は</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>伊藤さん</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>伊藤さんは</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>今日</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>今日は</span></span><br><b>The pronunciation matters</b>: as a particle it's written <span class='gd-jp'>は</span> but pronounced <b>wa</b>, the same sound as <span class='gd-jp'>わ</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Introducing and explaining</span>This is the most basic use: say “who / what” first, then say what it's like.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>伊藤さんは建築家です。</span>(Mr. Ito is an architect)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は暑いですね。</span>(It's hot today, isn't it?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Topic first, details after</span>Before は you put “what this sentence is about”, and another subject can appear afterward.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>象は鼻が長いです。</span>(An elephant's trunk is long)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そのケーキは姉が焼きました。</span>(That cake, my older sister baked it)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Many people get stuck on the second sentence: the one who baked is the sister, not the cake. So <b>what comes before は isn't always “the person doing the action”</b>. It only says “this is what the sentence is about”. Here the topic is the cake, and then you add “my sister baked it”.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Comparing two things</span>If you use は on both sides, it gives a contrast: “this one is like this, but that one is different”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーは好きですが、お茶はあまり飲みません。</span>(Coffee, I like, but tea, I don't drink much)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy places to slip</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The particle は is read wa. The final は in the greetings <span class='gd-jp'>こんにちは</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>こんばんは</span> is also read wa. The は in other words is still read ha, like in <span class='gd-jp'>はい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>花</span>.<br>・When a question word like 「誰」 or 「どれ」 is the subject of the question, it takes が, not は. <span class='gd-jp'>誰は来ましたか</span> is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>誰が来ましたか</span> (Who came?).<br>・Once the topic has been stated, you can leave it out afterward. English needs “I” in nearly every sentence, but in Japanese, after you've said <span class='gd-jp'>私は</span> once, you usually don't repeat it, and repeating it sounds wordy: <span class='gd-jp'>私は陳です。台湾から来ました。</span> (I'm Chen. I came from Taiwan.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Tell it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>は and が are the easiest to mix up. Look at the same scene: a new coworker arrives at the office.<br>Nobody knows you yet, so you introduce yourself: <span class='gd-jp'>私は陳です。</span> (I'm Chen) → what people want to know is the part <b>after</b>, “Chen”.<br>Someone is looking for you: <span class='gd-jp'>陳さんはどちらですか。</span> (Which one is Mr. Chen?) You raise your hand and say: <span class='gd-jp'>私が陳です。</span> (I'm Chen) → now the focus is the part <b>before</b>, “I”.<br>Also, something you've just seen or that has just happened uses が too: <span class='gd-jp'>あ、バスが来ました。</span> (Oh, the bus has come)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: with は, the focus is on <b>what comes after は</b>; with が, the focus is on <b>what comes before が</b>. Use が for the answer to “who? which one?” and for things you've just noticed.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Replace は with <b>も</b> and it becomes “also”: <span class='gd-jp'>私も学生です。</span> (I'm a student too). Note that it replaces は rather than stacking with it, so there's no such thing as 「私はも」.</div>","n5-56":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たり〜たりします」 is like English <b>\"I do things like reading, watching movies, and so on\"</b>. You pick two or three activities from a bigger set as examples, which signals \"these are just examples; I do other things too.\" It has a second use: <b>\"sometimes this, sometimes that\"</b>, like \"it's hot one minute and cold the next\" or \"on and off.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving examples</span>When someone asks what you usually do, you don't have to list everything. Just pick a few representative things.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>休みの日は、本を読んだり映画を見たりします。</span>(On days off, I do things like read books and watch movies.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末は掃除したり、買い物に行ったりします。</span>(On weekends, I clean up, go shopping, that sort of thing.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Back and forth</span>Two opposite actions or states alternate.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>最近、暑かったり寒かったりします。</span>(Lately it's been hot one day and cold the next.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は雨が降ったりやんだりしています。</span>(Today the rain keeps starting and stopping.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Three steps: <b>change the verb to the た form → add り → finish with します</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んだり</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったり</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見た</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見たり</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>したり</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来た</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来たり</span></span><br>If the た form still feels shaky, think of it as <b>swapping the 「て」 of the て form for 「た」, and 「で」 for 「だ」</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書いて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: drop the 「い」 and add 「かったり」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暑い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暑かったり</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高かったり</span></span> (<span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>よかったり</span>)<br><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: add 「だったり」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>朝ごはんはパンだったりご飯だったりします。</span>(For breakfast I have bread some days and rice on others.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Tense and politeness are all handled by <b>the final する</b>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日は友達と話したり、ゲームをしたりしました。</span>(Yesterday I chatted with friends, played games, and so on.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末は家で寝たり、テレビを見たりする。</span>(On weekends I sleep at home, watch TV, and so on. This is the casual form, for talking with friends.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Attach it to the <b>た form</b>, not the ます form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>行ったり</span>, not 行きたり; it's <span class='gd-jp'>読んだり</span>, not 読みたり。<br>・Don't forget the <span class='gd-jp'>します</span> (or <span class='gd-jp'>する</span>) at the end of the sentence. Without it, the sentence sounds unfinished.<br>・For the past tense, only the very end changes: <span class='gd-jp'>行ったりしました</span> is correct; you never say 行きましたり。<br>・With two or more actions, put たり after every one. In casual speech you'll occasionally hear the second たり dropped, but on exams and in writing, spell it out. Using it with just one action is fine too, e.g. <span class='gd-jp'>映画を見たりします</span> (I watch movies and the like).<br>・たり <b>does not indicate order</b>. To say \"do A, then B,\" use the て form.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>All of these talk about \"what I did yesterday\":<br><b>て</b> = in order, and these are exactly the things that happened: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日は本を読んで、映画を見ました。</span>(Yesterday I read a book, and then I watched a movie.)<br><b>たり</b> = a few examples, with more not mentioned, and order doesn't matter: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日は本を読んだり、映画を見たりしました。</span>(Yesterday I read a book, watched a movie, and so on.)<br><b>や</b> = also examples, but it connects <b>things (nouns)</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日は本や漫画を読みました。</span>(Yesterday I read books, manga, and so on.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: to tell what happened in order, use 「て」; to give a few <b>actions</b> as examples, use 「たり」; to give a few <b>things</b> as examples, use 「や」.</div>","n5-1":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「Noun＋です」is the English <b>\"is / am / are ~\"</b>, and 「Noun＋じゃありません」is <b>\"is not / am not / are not ~\"</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>私は学生です。</span> means \"I'm a student.\" This is the most basic Japanese sentence, and you'll use it in the very first line of a self-introduction.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's simple: <b>just add it directly</b> after the noun. The noun itself doesn't change at all.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生です</span></span> (is a student)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃありません</span></span> (is not a student)<br>The sentence pattern is 「A<span class='gd-jp'>は</span>B<span class='gd-jp'>です</span>」 = A is B.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>林です。よろしくお願いします。</span>(I'm Lin. Nice to meet you.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは私の傘です。</span>(This is my umbrella.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは先生じゃありません。</span>(Mr. Tanaka is not a teacher.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は休みじゃありません。</span>(Tomorrow is not a day off.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add <span class='gd-jp'>か</span> at the end to make a question: <span class='gd-jp'>学生ですか。</span> (Are you a student?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Ways to say \"is not\"</div><div class='gd-sec'>They all mean the same thing; the difference is the setting:<br><span class='gd-tag'>Most common</span><span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃありません</span>: everyday polite speech.<br><span class='gd-tag'>More formal</span><span class='gd-jp'>学生ではありません</span>: writing and formal situations. 「じゃ」 is just 「では」 said quickly.<br><span class='gd-tag'>More relaxed</span><span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃないです</span>: still polite, but softer in tone, and very common in everyday Japanese today.</div><div class='gd-h'>Talking with friends</div><div class='gd-sec'>With family and close friends, drop です and use <b>だ／じゃない</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生です</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生だ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃありません</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃない</span></span><br>In actual conversation, though, 「だ」 is often dropped, or 「よ」 is added to sound more natural; otherwise it can sound a bit blunt:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うん、学生だよ。</span>(Yeah, I'm a student.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The <span class='gd-jp'>は</span> in 「AはB」 is pronounced <b>wa</b>, not ha.<br>・The final 「す」 of <span class='gd-jp'>です</span> is usually very light, so it sounds like \"des.\"<br>・です doesn't always translate as \"is\": <span class='gd-jp'>今日は雨です。</span> means \"It's rainy today,\" and you wouldn't say \"Today is rain\" in English.<br>・You can't add です after a verb: <span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> is enough; you can't say 「食べるです」.<br>・You don't need to say <span class='gd-jp'>私は</span> in every sentence. When it's clear you're talking about yourself, Japanese speakers usually leave it out.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Nouns and な-adjectives both use じゃありません to negate, but <b>い-adjectives are different</b>, and that's the easiest place to get confused.<br><b>Noun</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>ここは駅じゃありません。</span>(This is not a station.)<br><b>な-adjective</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>ここは静かじゃありません。</span>(It's not quiet here.)<br><b>い-adjective</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>ここは高くないです。</span>(It's not expensive here.)<br>You can't say 「高いじゃありません」; replace い with <b>くない</b>.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: nouns and な-adjectives take 「じゃありません」 directly; for い-adjectives, replace い with 「くないです」. Note that <span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> ends in い but is actually a な-adjective, so you say <span class='gd-jp'>きれいじゃありません</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The past tense: <span class='gd-jp'>学生でした</span> (used to be a student), <span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃありませんでした</span> (wasn't a student).</div>","n5-34":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「が」 is like pointing a finger and saying <b>\"It's this one!\"</b>. When you want to single out <b>who</b> did something or <b>what</b> happened, put が right after that star of the sentence.<br>For example, someone asks \"Who came?\" and you answer \"Mr. Tanaka came.\" \"Mr. Tanaka\" is the answer you're pointing at, so you say <span class='gd-jp'>田中さんが来ました</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Just add が right after the noun. Nothing changes.<br><span class='gd-jp'>私が</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>雨が</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんが</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>誰が</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>何が</span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking who or what</span>When \"who\" or \"what\" is the subject of the sentence, use が in the question, and use が in the answer too.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰が来ましたか。</span>(Who came?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんが来ました。</span>(Mr. Tanaka came.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何が一番おいしいですか。</span>(What tastes best?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このラーメンが一番おいしいです。</span>(This ramen tastes best.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Something right in front of you</span>When you've just seen something or it has just happened, you simply describe the scene.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あ、バスが来ました。</span>(Oh, the bus is here.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雪が降っています。</span>(It's snowing.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Mentioning something for the first time</span>You bring in a person or thing the listener doesn't know about yet.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日、友達が家に来ました。</span>(A friend came to my house yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Question words like \"who,\" \"what\" and \"where\" can't be followed by は</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>誰は来ましたか</span> is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>誰が来ましたか</span>. The other question words work the same way, for example <span class='gd-jp'>どこが痛いですか</span> (Where does it hurt?).<br>・<b>If the question uses が, answer with が</b>. If someone asks <span class='gd-jp'>誰が作りましたか</span> (Who made it?), answer <span class='gd-jp'>私が作りました</span> (I made it). If you answer 「私は作りました」, it turns into \"Me? Well, I did make it,\" which sounds odd.<br>・It's tempting to put は on the subject of every sentence, since it feels like the default \"subject\" marker. But if you suddenly see rain outside the window, say <span class='gd-jp'>あ、雨が降っています</span>. For sentences that just describe what's in front of you, が sounds natural.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語が好きです</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>ピアノができます</span> also use が, but that's the \"liking something / being able to do something\" usage. It's covered in another card, 「助詞が（能力・好惡）」, so don't mix it in yet.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>は</b> means \"as for ~,\" so you set out the topic first and <b>the point comes after it</b>.<br><b>が</b> means \"it's ~ that...,\" so <b>the point is the noun right before it</b>.<br><br>Both of these are about you being Tanaka:<br>When introducing yourself, you say <span class='gd-jp'>私は田中です。</span> (I'm Tanaka.) Here everyone is listening for who you are.<br>If someone asks <span class='gd-jp'>田中さんはどの人ですか。</span> (Which one is Mr. Tanaka?), you say <span class='gd-jp'>私が田中です。</span> (I'm Tanaka.) Here you're picking Tanaka out from a group.<br><br>Both of these are about Tanaka coming:<br><span class='gd-jp'>田中さんが来ました。</span> (Mr. Tanaka came.) This simply reports who came.<br><span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来ました。</span> (Mr. Tanaka did come.) Here Tanaka is the topic, with a hint of \"as for the others, who knows.\"</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you're answering \"who is it?\" or \"what is it?\", use が. If you're saying what this person or thing is like, use は. When a question word is the subject, always use が after it.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>\"There is something somewhere\" also uses が: <span class='gd-jp'>あそこに猫がいます。</span> (There's a cat over there.) Next, try the existence expressions, plus the が pattern for \"like\" and \"can.\"</div>","n5-31":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「から」 means <b>\"because ~, so ~.\"</b> The difference is that English puts \"because\" in front of the reason, while Japanese puts 「から」 <b>at the very end of the reason</b>. You give the reason first, then the result.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>時間がないから、急ぎましょう。</span>(We're out of time, so let's hurry.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The order is: <b>reason + から, result</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put a complete clause before 「から」. It can be in plain form or in the polite です・ます form.<br><b>Verbs</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くから</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行きますから</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒いから</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒いです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒いですから</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add 「だ」 or 「です」 first. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かだから</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かですから</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: again, add 「だ」 or 「です」. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だから</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨ですから</span></span><br>It also works for past events: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寝なかった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寝なかったから</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Plain form before から and ます at the end of the sentence is very common in daily speech. If you want to sound more polite, use です・ます in both parts.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving a reason</span>Say the cause first, then the result.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は安いから、よく来ます。</span>(This shop is cheap, so I come here often.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は日曜日だから、学校は休みです。</span>(Today is Sunday, so school is closed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Requests, reminders, invitations</span>It's also natural to follow it with a request or an invitation.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>危ないですから、触らないでください。</span>(It's dangerous, so please don't touch it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving only the reason</span>When someone asks \"why?\" and the result is already obvious, just say the reason plus から.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「どうして食べないの？」「辛いから。」</span>(\"Why aren't you eating?\" \"Because it's spicy.\")</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After nouns and な-adjectives, <b>you can't attach から directly</b>. Say <span class='gd-jp'>雨だから</span>, not 「雨から」; say <span class='gd-jp'>静かだから</span>, not 「静かから」.<br>・い-adjectives are the opposite: <b>you can't add だ</b>. Say <span class='gd-jp'>高いから</span>, not 「高いだから」.<br>・Don't follow English word order and put から at the start of the sentence. \"Because it's raining\" is <span class='gd-jp'>雨だから</span>, with から at the end.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>だから</span> on its own at the start of a sentence means \"so\": <span class='gd-jp'>雨です。だから、行きません。</span> (It's raining. So I won't go.)<br>・When explaining your situation to a teacher or boss (for example, why you were late), から sounds rather direct and can come across like an excuse. In everyday conversation, から is perfectly fine.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>You've already learned 「から」 for a starting point (<b>from ~</b>). The two look exactly the same.<br><b>Starting point</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>9時から働きます。</span> (I work from 9 o'clock.)<br><b>Reason</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>雨だから、働きません。</span> (It's raining, so I won't work.)<br>The difference is what comes before it. For a starting point, から follows a time or place noun directly. For a reason, から follows the end of a full clause, such as a verb, an adjective, or 「だ」「です」.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if only a noun like <span class='gd-jp'>9時</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>駅</span> comes before から, it means \"from.\" If a complete clause like <span class='gd-jp'>雨だ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>あります</span> comes before it, it means \"because.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the reason at the end of the sentence as 「〜からです」 to answer \"why?\": <span class='gd-jp'>熱があるからです。</span> (Because I have a fever.) Later you'll also learn 「〜ので」, which also means \"because\" but sounds softer than から. It's more common when asking your boss for time off or apologizing.</div>","n5-51":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>English uses \"give\" no matter who gives to whom, but Japanese looks at <b>which direction the thing is moving</b>. When it goes <b>out</b> from my side, use <span class='gd-jp'>あげます</span>. When it comes <b>in</b> from someone else to my side, use <span class='gd-jp'>くれます</span>. When you speak from the receiver's side and say \"I <b>got</b> it,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>もらいます</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>あげます</span>I (or someone on my side) give to another person, so the thing goes outward. It's also used when one other person gives to another other person.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達に誕生日プレゼントをあげました。</span>(I gave my friend a birthday present.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは山田さんに花をあげました。</span>(Mr. Tanaka gave Ms. Yamada flowers.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>くれます</span>Someone gives to <b>me</b> or to <b>my family</b>, so the thing comes in toward my side. You usually don't need to say \"to me\" out loud, because Japanese listeners already understand it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母が時計をくれました。</span>(My mom gave me a watch.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達が妹にお菓子をくれました。</span>(A friend gave my little sister some sweets.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>もらいます</span>You speak from the receiver's side: \"I got it, I received it.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生から手紙をもらいました。</span>(I received a letter from my teacher.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three sentences are \"who, to whom, what,\" and the difference is <b>who the subject is</b>:<br><b>あげます</b>: giver <span class='gd-jp'>は</span>　receiver <span class='gd-jp'>に</span>　thing <span class='gd-jp'>を</span>　<span class='gd-jp'>あげます</span><br><b>くれます</b>: giver <span class='gd-jp'>が</span>　(me <span class='gd-jp'>に</span>)　thing <span class='gd-jp'>を</span>　<span class='gd-jp'>くれます</span><br><b>もらいます</b>: me <span class='gd-jp'>は</span>　giver <span class='gd-jp'>に</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>から</span>　thing <span class='gd-jp'>を</span>　<span class='gd-jp'>もらいます</span><br>For the past tense, just swap ます for ました: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>くれます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>くれました</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar ones apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: <b>a friend gives me a book</b>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達が本をくれました。</span>(A friend gave me a book.) The friend is the subject, and the book comes toward me.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達に本をもらいました。</span>(I got a book from a friend.) I am the subject, and I receive it.<br>These two mean the same thing, and you can say either.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達が私に本をあげました。</span>　This one is <b>wrong</b>, because あげます can't be used for \"gives to me.\"<br><br>Conversely, if <b>I give a friend a book</b>, you can only say:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達に本をあげました。</span>(I gave my friend a book.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick check: ask yourself, \"Is the thing coming toward me?\" If yes, use くれます or もらいます. If it's going outward, use あげます.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Since English just says \"give,\" many learners write <span class='gd-jp'>母があげました</span> for \"Mom gave it to me.\" That's wrong. Giving to me or to my family is always <b>くれます</b>.<br>・The subject of くれます is never \"I\": <span class='gd-jp'>私が友達にくれました</span> is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>友達にあげました</span> instead.<br>・The <span class='gd-jp'>に</span> before もらいます means \"<b>from</b>,\" not \"to\": <span class='gd-jp'>友達にもらいました</span> means \"I got it from a friend,\" not \"I gave it to a friend.\" If that confuses you, switch to <span class='gd-jp'>から</span>, which means the same thing.<br>・Family counts as \"my side,\" so when a friend gives something to your little sister, you also use くれます.<br>・When giving something to a teacher or a boss, saying あげます outright can sound a bit condescending. You'll learn more polite forms later.<br>・You may also hear <span class='gd-jp'>花に水をやります</span> (watering the flowers). やります is used for animals and plants. Saying it about people sounds rough, so as a beginner just stick with あげます.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>At N4, these three verbs attach after the て-form to mean doing something for someone: <span class='gd-jp'>友達が駅まで送ってくれました。</span>(A friend took me to the station.) The direction logic is exactly the same as here, so if you get it straight now, that step will be easy.</div>","n5-38":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「だけ」 and 「しか」 both mean \"only.\" <b>だけ</b> is like a plain \"only ~\" that just states the range. <b>しか〜ません</b> is more like \"just ~\" or \"nothing but ~,\" with a feeling of <b>\"that's so little, not enough.\"</b> Its biggest feature: <b>しか must always be followed by a negative</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>だけ</b>: put it directly after a noun or a quantity.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>水</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>水だけ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>一つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>一つだけ</span></span><br>It also attaches to verbs, in the dictionary form (the form you'd look up in a dictionary): <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見るだけ</span></span> (just looking)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>しか</b>: put it after a noun or a quantity, and the verb at the end of the sentence must become negative.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>水を飲みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>水しか飲みません</span></span> (I only drink water)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Drop the original 「を」 and 「が」</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>水を</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>水だけ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>水しか</span>. (You can keep を after だけ, but most of the time people just drop it.)<br>Particles like 「に」「で」「へ」 <b>stay</b>, and go before しか:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達にしか言いませんでした。</span>(I only told my friends.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>だけ: just stating the range</span>You don't feel it's particularly much or little. You're simply saying \"it's only this.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>サラダだけ注文します。</span>(I'll order just a salad.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>見ているだけです。</span>(I'm just looking.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second sentence is very handy when shopping in Japan. When a shop assistant comes over to greet you, you can simply reply with it.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>しか: feeling it's little, not enough</span>In your mind it's \"too little\" or \"less than I expected.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>10分しか残っていません。</span>(There are only 10 minutes left.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日は3時間しか寝ませんでした。</span>(I slept only three hours yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>しか is always followed by a negative</b>: you say <span class='gd-jp'>千円しかありません</span>, and you can't say 「千円しかあります」.<br>・<b>The negative can fool you</b>: when you see 「ません」, don't assume it means \"not.\" <span class='gd-jp'>千円しかありません</span> means \"I only have 1,000 yen,\" not \"I don't have 1,000 yen.\" <span class='gd-jp'>肉しか食べません</span> means \"I only eat meat,\" not \"I don't eat meat.\" Think of it as \"apart from 1,000 yen, I have nothing.\"<br>・<b>Don't keep を or が before しか</b>: you can't say 「水をしか」. Say <span class='gd-jp'>水しか</span>.<br>・<b>だけ doesn't mean \"enough\"</b>: だけ is just a neutral \"only\" and carries no sense of being sufficient.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar ones apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>For the same situation, だけ is a report, while しか is an exclamation of \"so little.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>パーティーに一人だけ来ました。</span>(Only one person came to the party: a plain report.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>パーティーに一人しか来ませんでした。</span>(Just one person came to the party: so few people.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語が少しだけ分かります。</span>(I understand a little Japanese: a modest way of saying you know some.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語が少ししか分かりません。</span>(I only understand a little Japanese: stressing that you don't know much, and asking the other person not to speak too difficultly.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick check: when you want to say \"just ~\" or \"only ~\" and you feel it's little, use しか＋ません. If you're only stating the range, use だけ. Remember that しか always goes with ません.</div>","n5-2":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ます」 is the <b>polite form</b> of Japanese. Use it with teachers, shop staff and people you've just met. With family and close friends you'd use the plain form without ます. To say \"don't / doesn't (do)\", swap ます for <b>ません</b>. While you're starting out, <b>ます is the safest choice for everything</b>, since it's never rude.</div><div class='gd-h'>One form for both present and future</div><div class='gd-sec'>Japanese has no separate future tense. Habits and things you're about to do both use 「〜ます」, and time words like 「毎日」「明日」「来週」 tell the listener when.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎日コーヒーを飲みます。</span>(I drink coffee every day: habit)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>来週、京都へ行きます。</span>(I'm going to Kyoto next week: future)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田村先生はお酒を飲みません。</span>(Mr. Tamura doesn't drink alcohol)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first, which group is the verb?</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the final sound to the 「い」-row sound of the same row, then add ます.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → む becomes み → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買います</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰ります</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add ます.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見ます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きます</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>します</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span>（きます）</span><br>Words made of a noun plus する, like 「勉強する」「出張する」, work the same way: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強します</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>For the negative, just swap ます for ません. It's the same for all three groups: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べません</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>入る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>走る</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>知る</span> look like Group 2 but are actually Group 1. It's <span class='gd-jp'>帰ります</span>, not 帰ます.<br>・「ません」 means \"don't / doesn't (do)\", not \"can't\". <span class='gd-jp'>お酒を飲みません</span> means \"I don't drink alcohol\", not \"I'm unable to drink\".<br>・ます attaches only to <b>verbs</b>. To say \"I'm a student\" you need <span class='gd-jp'>学生です</span>, not 学生ます.<br>・The 「す」 in ます is usually barely pronounced, so it sounds like \"mas\". Don't doubt your ears when you hear it.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>English speakers often want to use ます for \"I'm ~ing\". But ます describes a <b>habit</b> or <b>something you're about to do</b>. For \"in the middle of doing\", use <b>ています</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>今、ご飯を食べます。</span>(I'm going to eat now: I haven't started yet)<br><span class='gd-jp'>今、ご飯を食べています。</span>(I'm eating right now: already underway)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if it hasn't started yet, or you do it every day, use ます. If you're in the middle of it, use ています.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Talking about the past: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みました</span></span> (drank), <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲みません</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みませんでした</span></span> (didn't drink).<br>To invite someone, learn <span class='gd-jp'>〜ませんか</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜ましょう</span> next.</div>","n5-3":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Adjectives like <span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> (quiet), <span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> (pretty, clean) and <span class='gd-jp'>親切</span> are called <b>な-adjectives</b> in Japanese. To say \"it's quiet\" you use <span class='gd-jp'>静かです</span>, and for \"it's not quiet\" you use <span class='gd-jp'>静かじゃありません</span>. They work almost exactly like noun sentences, with just one extra rule: <b>when the adjective comes directly before a noun, you add 「な」</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>What are な-adjectives?</div><div class='gd-sec'>They're words that describe states and impressions, like \"quiet\", \"pretty\" or \"famous\". They're called 「な-adjectives」 because they gain a な before a noun. The 「語幹」 (stem) on the card is the basic form you see in the word list, such as <span class='gd-jp'>静か</span>, with nothing added.<br>Start with these common ones:<br><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> (quiet), <span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> (pretty, clean), <span class='gd-jp'>元気</span> (energetic, well), <span class='gd-jp'>有名</span> (famous), <span class='gd-jp'>便利</span> (convenient), <span class='gd-jp'>暇</span> (free, not busy), <span class='gd-jp'>大変</span> (tough), <span class='gd-jp'>好き</span> (like), <span class='gd-jp'>嫌い</span> (dislike), <span class='gd-jp'>上手</span> (good at)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to use them</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Affirmative</span>Add <span class='gd-jp'>です</span> directly to the basic form.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かです</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この喫茶店は静かです。</span>(This café is quiet)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅の近くは便利です。</span>(Near the station is convenient)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Negative</span>Add <span class='gd-jp'>じゃありません</span> directly to the basic form. The word in front doesn't change at all.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暇</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暇じゃありません</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は暇じゃありません。</span>(I'm not free today)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この町は有名じゃありません。</span>(This town isn't very famous)</div><div class='gd-sec'>There are two other ways to make the negative, with the same meaning: <span class='gd-jp'>ではありません</span> is more formal and common in textbooks and writing. <span class='gd-jp'>じゃないです</span> is more conversational but still polite.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Questions</span>Just add <span class='gd-jp'>か</span> at the end.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>仕事は大変ですか。</span>(Is work tough?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Before a noun: add な</div><div class='gd-sec'>To say \"a quiet town\" or \"a kind person\", put a <span class='gd-jp'>な</span> in between. Think of it as a small connector that links the adjective to the noun.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span>＋<span class='gd-jp'>町</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かな町</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>静かな町です。</span>(It's a quiet town)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>京都はきれいな所です。</span>(Kyoto is a beautiful place)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>親切な人ですね。</span>(What a kind person, isn't he/she?)</div><div class='gd-sec'>An easy way to remember: <b>before です, no な; before a noun, add な</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>嫌い</span> end in 「い」 and look like い-adjectives, but they're actually な-adjectives. The negative is <span class='gd-jp'>きれいじゃありません</span>, not 「きれくないです」.<br>・Don't put な before です: it's <span class='gd-jp'>静かです</span>, not 「静かなです」.<br>・Don't leave out な before a noun, and don't use の as a link either: it's <span class='gd-jp'>静かな町</span>, not 「静か町」 and not 「静かの町」.<br>・The object of <span class='gd-jp'>好き</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>嫌い</span> takes <b>が</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>猫が好きです</span> (I like cats). In English \"like cats\" looks like a verb plus an object, so many learners write 「猫を好きです」. For now, just remember to use が.<br>・Saying 「嫌いです」 outright is a bit harsh. Japanese people often say <span class='gd-jp'>あまり好きじゃありません</span> (I don't like it very much), which sounds much softer.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even when you're talking about the same \"this shop\", the negative looks different for each type of word:<br><b>い-adjectives</b>: the final い changes to くない.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は安くないです。</span>(This shop isn't cheap)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives</b>: the word itself stays the same, then add じゃありません.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店はきれいじゃありません。</span>(This shop isn't clean)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: same as な-adjectives, add じゃありません.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここは銀行じゃありません。</span>(This isn't a bank)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: when you memorize vocabulary, learn the な along with it, like <span class='gd-jp'>きれいな</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>静かな</span>. If a word takes 「～な＋noun」, its negative is simply じゃありません. Only い-adjectives change い to くない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>For the past tense, swap です for でした: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日は暇でした。</span> (I was free yesterday)</div>","n5-10":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜なります」means <b>\"become ~\"</b> or <b>\"get ~\"</b>. The weather goes from hot to cold, a room goes from messy to clean, a kid grows into an adult. Whenever something <b>changes from one state to another</b>, Japanese uses なります.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: check the word before it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: replace the final「い」with「く」, then add なります.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → replace い with く → <span class='gd-jp'>寒くなります</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大きい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大きくなります</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安くなります</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives</b>: add「に」, then なります.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>きれいになります</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>元気</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>元気になります</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>好き</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>好きになります</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: just like な-adjectives, add「に」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>春</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>春になります</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>医者</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>医者になります</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寒くなりましたね。</span>(It's gotten cold, hasn't it?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部屋がきれいになりました。</span>(The room has become clean.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本が好きになりました。</span>(I've come to like Japan.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>来月、二十歳になります。</span>(I turn twenty next month.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Choosing between なります and なりました</div><div class='gd-sec'>When you'd say \"it has gotten cold\" in English, Japanese uses <b>なりました</b>: the change has already happened, and this is how things are now.<br><b>なります</b> means \"will become ~\" or \"is about to ~\". It describes something that hasn't happened yet, or something that happens every time.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もうすぐ夏になります。</span>(It will be summer soon.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んで、よくなりました。</span>(I took medicine and got better.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> is an exception: say <span class='gd-jp'>よくなります</span>, not いくなります. Use <span class='gd-jp'>よくなりました</span> both for getting better after an illness and for the weather improving.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>きらい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>有名</span> end in an \"i\" sound, but they are な-adjectives: it's <span class='gd-jp'>きれいになります</span>, not きれくなります.<br>・Don't drop the「に」after nouns and な-adjectives: <span class='gd-jp'>先生になりました</span>, not 先生なりました.<br>・Many learners just say <span class='gd-jp'>寒いです</span> for \"it's gotten cold.\" That only means \"it's cold now\" and has no sense of change. To stress that it became cold, say <span class='gd-jp'>寒くなりました</span>.<br>・The thing that changes is marked with <span class='gd-jp'>が</span> (or <span class='gd-jp'>は</span>), not を: <span class='gd-jp'>部屋がきれいになりました</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from a look-alike</div><div class='gd-sec'>The next lesson's「〜くします・〜にします」differs by just one word, but the meaning is different. Take a room getting clean:<br><b>なります</b> = says that <b>something becomes</b> a certain way. The focus is on the result: <span class='gd-jp'>部屋がきれいになりました。</span>(The room became clean.)<br><b>します</b> = says that <b>someone makes something</b> a certain way. The focus is on who did it: <span class='gd-jp'>部屋をきれいにしました。</span>(I cleaned the room.)<br>One more pair: <span class='gd-jp'>音が大きくなりました。</span>(The sound got louder.) / <span class='gd-jp'>音を小さくしてください。</span>(Please turn the sound down.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if English says \"become ~\" or \"get ~\", use <b>が + なります</b>. If it says \"make ~ …\" or \"turn ~ …\", use <b>を + します</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs can't attach directly to なります. To say \"I've become able to ~\", use the N4 pattern「〜ようになります」: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せるようになりました。</span>(I've become able to speak Japanese.)</div>","n5-11":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜くします／〜にします」means <b>\"make ~ …\"</b> or <b>\"turn ~ …\"</b>, as in \"turn the sound down\" or \"tidy the room.\" The point is that <b>someone does something</b> to change it; it doesn't change by itself.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: drop the final「い」, replace it with「く」, then add します.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>短い</span> → replace い with く → <span class='gd-jp'>短くします</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>小さい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>小さくします</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暖かい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暖かくします</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives</b>: just add「にします」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>きれいにします</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かにします</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: also just add「にします」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子どもの部屋</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子どもの部屋にします</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>The thing being changed takes <b>を</b>, as the object of the action:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>音を小さくしてください。</span>(Please turn the sound down.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>机の上をきれいにします。</span>(I'll tidy up the desk.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋を子どもの部屋にします。</span>(We're going to make this room the kids' room.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking someone to change something</span>Add「てください」to ask the other person to make something a certain way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>静かにしてください。</span>(Please be quiet.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もう少し安くしてください。</span>(Please make it a little cheaper.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Ordering and choosing</span>Noun + にします is also very common for \"I'll go with ~\" or \"I've decided on ~\". You'll hear it all the time when ordering at restaurants.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私はコーヒーにします。</span>(I'll have coffee.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>旅行は京都にしました。</span>(I decided on Kyoto for the trip.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Always drop the「い」of い-adjectives first: it's <span class='gd-jp'>短くします</span>, not「短いくします」or「短いにします」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> ends in い but is a な-adjective: it's <span class='gd-jp'>きれいにします</span>, not「きれくします」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>よく</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>この町をよくしたいです。</span>(I want to make this town a better place.) Not「いくします」.<br>・The thing you change takes <b>を</b>, not が.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from a look-alike</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>くなります／になります</b> = the thing <b>changes by itself</b>, with <b>が</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>部屋が暖かくなりました。</span>(The room got warm; maybe the sun came out.)<br><b>くします／にします</b> = <b>someone does something</b> to make it that way, with <b>を</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>部屋を暖かくしました。</span>(I warmed up the room; for example, I turned on the heater.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can say \"make ~ …\" or \"turn ~ …\" in English, use <b>を + します</b>. If it's only \"~ gets …\" or \"~ becomes …\", use <b>が + なります</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + にします can mean \"decide on ~\". When what you decide is to do something, you'll later learn verb + ことにします: <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行くことにしました。</span>(I've decided to go to Japan.)</div>","n5-4":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Words that describe things, like \"cold,\" \"expensive,\" and \"delicious,\" are called <b>い-adjectives</b> in Japanese, because they all end in 「い」. For example: <span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>高い</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>おいしい</span>.<br>To say \"it's cold\" politely, just add <b>です</b>. To say \"it's not cold,\" change the final い to <b>く</b> and add <b>ないです</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Affirmative</span>Add です to the plain form. Leave the final い alone.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒いです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いです</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Negative</span>Change the final い to く, then add ないです.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → change い to く → <span class='gd-jp'>寒く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒くないです</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高くないです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>おいしい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>おいしくないです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大きい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大きくないです</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>In textbooks you'll also see <span class='gd-jp'>寒くありません</span>. It means the same thing but sounds a little more formal. For everyday speech, 「くないです」 is plenty.</div><div class='gd-h'>Try saying it</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このラーメンはおいしいです。</span>(This ramen is delicious.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は広くないです。</span>(This room isn't big.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日はあまり寒くないです。</span>(It's not very cold today.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English you just say \"it's cold,\" with no \"very\" needed. <span class='gd-jp'>寒いです</span> already means exactly that, so there's no need to force in とても. Only say <span class='gd-jp'>とても寒いです</span> when it's really cold and you want to stress it.<br>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>あまり</span> followed by a negative means \"<b>not very</b> ~.\" It's softer than a flat \"not ~,\" and Japanese people use it all the time.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> is an exception: its negative is <b><span class='gd-jp'>よくないです</span></b>, not いくないです. That's because いい comes from <span class='gd-jp'>よい</span>, so it uses よ when it changes form. <span class='gd-jp'>かっこいい</span> works the same way, and its negative is <span class='gd-jp'>かっこよくないです</span>.<br>・To say \"it's not cold,\" you can't say 寒いじゃないです. The negative of an い-adjective is always formed by changing い to く, never with じゃない.<br>・Don't add an extra だ in the middle: 寒いだです is wrong. To be polite, say <span class='gd-jp'>寒いです</span>. With friends, just say <span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>嫌い</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>有名</span> also sound like they end in 「い」, but they're な-adjectives. Their negative is <span class='gd-jp'>きれいじゃないです</span>, not きれくないです.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even for the same \"this room isn't ~,\" the negative is formed differently depending on the type of word before it:<br><b>い-adjective</b>: change い to く and add ないです<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は明るくないです。</span>(This room isn't bright.)<br><b>な-adjective</b>: add じゃないです<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は静かじゃないです。</span>(This room isn't quiet.)<br><b>Noun</b>: also add じゃないです<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここは私の部屋じゃないです。</span>(This isn't my room.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if the word originally ends in 「い」, change い to く and add ないです. な-adjectives and nouns just take じゃないです. Be extra careful with きれい, 嫌い, and 有名, because they're な-adjectives.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Past tense: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒いです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒かったです</span></span> (it was cold)<br>Expressing change: <span class='gd-jp'>寒くなります</span> (it will get cold). The 「く」 you used in the negative shows up again here.</div>","n5-5":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜です」 means \"is ~,\" and 「〜でした」 means <b>\"was ~.\"</b> Just like English changes \"is\" to \"was,\" <b>when you talk about the past, you swap です for でした</b>. Adding a word like \"yesterday\" isn't enough on its own.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Was</span>Add でした directly after a noun.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生です</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生でした</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休みです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休みでした</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Wasn't</span>Start with the present-tense 「じゃありません」, then add でした.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃありません</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃありませんでした</span></span><br>In writing, the more formal form is <span class='gd-jp'>ではありませんでした</span>. It means the same thing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日は雨でした。</span>(Yesterday was rainy.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先週の月曜日は祝日でした。</span>(Last Monday was a national holiday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日は休みじゃありませんでした。</span>(Yesterday wasn't a day off.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking about the past</span>Add か at the end to make it a question.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日は休みでしたか。</span>(Did you have yesterday off?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>はい、休みでした。</span>(Yes, I did.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、休みじゃありませんでした。</span>(No, I didn't.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Casual version</span>With close friends, replace でした with <b>だった</b> and じゃありませんでした with <b>じゃなかった</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨でした</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だった</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休みじゃありませんでした</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休みじゃなかった</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It's tempting to think that having 「昨日」 in the sentence is enough, and to say <span class='gd-jp'>昨日は休みです</span>. In Japanese you need to change it to <span class='gd-jp'>休みでした</span>, just as English needs \"was,\" not \"is.\"<br>・The past of \"is not\" is the easiest one to get wrong: <span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃないでした</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃでした</span> are both wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃありませんでした</span>.<br>・In everyday speech you'll also often hear <span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃなかったです</span>. It's just as polite and also correct, so don't be thrown when you hear it.<br>・でした can only follow nouns (and な-adjectives). い-adjectives like <span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> can't take でした directly: <span class='gd-jp'>寒いでした</span> is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>寒かったです</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>When you talk about \"yesterday,\" the ending changes depending on the word before it:<br><b>Noun</b> + でした: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日は雨でした。</span>(Yesterday was rainy.)<br><b>Verb</b>: change ます to ました: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日は雨が降りました。</span>(It rained yesterday.)<br><b>い-adjective</b>: change い to かったです: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日は寒かったです。</span>(It was cold yesterday.)<br><b>な-adjective</b>: add でした, just like a noun: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日は静かでした。</span>(It was quiet yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: after a noun or な-adjective, use でした. After a verb, use ました. After an い-adjective (like 寒い or 高い), use かったです.<br>Note: <span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>有名</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>嫌い</span> end in い but are な-adjectives, so they take でした, as in <span class='gd-jp'>きれいでした</span>, not きれかったです.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The past of verbs, <span class='gd-jp'>〜ました・〜ませんでした</span>, and the past of い-adjectives, <span class='gd-jp'>〜かったです</span>. Learn all three together and you won't get stuck when talking about the past.</div>","n5-6":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ました」is the <b>past form</b> of「〜ます」, and「〜ませんでした」is the past form of「〜ません」. English changes the verb itself (go → went); Japanese changes the ending: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> (go) → <span class='gd-jp'>行きました</span> (went)</span>. Like ます, it's polite speech, so it's safe to use with teachers and shop staff.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: start from the ます form</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are all four forms of \"drink\" side by side:<br>Present, does it: <span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span> (drink)<br>Present, doesn't: <span class='gd-jp'>飲みません</span> (don't drink)<br>Past, did it: <span class='gd-jp'>飲みました</span> (drank)<br>Past, didn't: <span class='gd-jp'>飲みませんでした</span> (didn't drink)<br><br>There are only two rules: <b>change ます to ました</b>; <b>add でした after ません</b>.<br>Since you always start from the ます form, the rule is exactly the same for all three verb groups:<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行きました</span></span><br><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べました</span></span><br><b>Group 3 (する・来る)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しました</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ました</span> (きました)</span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Did it</span>Talk about something that already happened.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先月、札幌へ行きました。</span> (I went to Sapporo last month.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日、映画を見ました。</span> (I watched a movie yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Didn't do it</span>Talk about something you didn't do during a past period.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今朝は朝ご飯を食べませんでした。</span> (I didn't eat breakfast this morning.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>宿題をしませんでした。</span> (I didn't do my homework.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking someone</span>Add か to make it a question.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末、何をしましたか。</span> (What did you do on the weekend?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The past negative is「ません＋<b>でした</b>」. Many learners say「食べませんました」, which is wrong.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来ました</span> is read <b>きました</b>, not こました.<br>・The「し」in「ました」is often very light, so it sounds like \"ma-shta\". Don't think you missed a sound when you hear it.<br>・Don't use ませんでした for \"not yet\". If a friend asks <span class='gd-jp'>もう食べましたか。</span> (Have you eaten yet?) and you haven't eaten but will soon, answer <span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、まだです。</span> (No, not yet.) <span class='gd-jp'>食べませんでした</span> means \"I didn't eat then\", and the moment has already passed.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>分かりました</span> means \"I understand / Got it\". It describes your state right now, not something in the past. When a shop clerk or coworker says this to you, it means \"understood\".</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>When you learn the past tense, it's easy to mix up「ました」and「でした」. Using \"yesterday's party\" as the example, here they are all at once:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日、パーティーに行きました。</span> (I went to a party yesterday: verbs take ました)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>料理は和食でした。</span> (The food was Japanese cuisine: nouns take でした)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会場はきれいでした。</span> (The venue was beautiful: な-adjectives take でした)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>とても楽しかったです。</span> (It was a lot of fun: い-adjectives take かったです)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Common mistakes:<br>「学生ました」→ say <span class='gd-jp'>学生でした</span><br>「行きでした」→ say <span class='gd-jp'>行きました</span><br>「おいしいでした」→ say <span class='gd-jp'>おいしかったです</span></div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: after a verb, use ました; after a noun or a な-adjective, use でした; after an い-adjective, use かったです.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>When talking with friends, the past tense switches to a form without ます, called the た-form / なかった-form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きました</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きませんでした</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かなかった</span></span>.</div>","n5-7":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>静かです</span> means \"it's quiet right now\". To say it <b>used to be</b> quiet, swap <span class='gd-jp'>です</span> for <span class='gd-jp'>でした</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>静かでした</span>. It's like English changing \"is\" to \"was\": Japanese changes the ending into its past form. This pattern is <b>exactly the same</b> as for nouns (<span class='gd-jp'>学生でした</span>), so learning one means learning both.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>な-adjectives are the ones that need「な」before a noun, like <span class='gd-jp'>静かな部屋</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>きれいな花</span>.<br>Step 1: Drop「な」. What's left is called the \"stem\" (the core of the word).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静かな</span> → drop な → <span class='gd-jp'>静か</span></span><br>Step 2: Attach the ending you need.<br>Present: <span class='gd-jp'>静かです</span> (it's quiet) / <span class='gd-jp'>静かじゃありません</span> (it's not quiet)<br>Past: <span class='gd-jp'>静かでした</span> (it was quiet) / <span class='gd-jp'>静かじゃありませんでした</span> (it wasn't quiet)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Past affirmative</span>It was like that back then.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日は暇でした。</span> (I was free yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>テストは簡単でした。</span> (The test was easy.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どものとき、野菜が嫌いでした。</span> (I hated vegetables when I was a kid.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Past negative</span>It wasn't like that back then. Often used with <span class='gd-jp'>あまり</span> (not very).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部屋はあまりきれいじゃありませんでした。</span> (The room wasn't very clean.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あまり有名じゃありませんでした。</span> (It wasn't very famous back then.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Other forms you'll hear</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>More formal</span>Change <span class='gd-jp'>じゃ</span> to <span class='gd-jp'>では</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>静かではありませんでした</span>, used in writing and formal situations.<br><span class='gd-tag'>Polite, spoken</span><span class='gd-jp'>静かじゃなかったです</span> means the same thing and is very common in everyday conversation.<br><span class='gd-tag'>Between friends</span>Change でした to <span class='gd-jp'>だった</span>, and use <span class='gd-jp'>じゃなかった</span> for the negative.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日、暇だった。</span> (I was free yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>全然大変じゃなかった。</span> (It wasn't hard at all.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Drop「な」: say <span class='gd-jp'>静かでした</span>, not「静かなでした」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>嫌い</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>有名</span> end in「い」, but they are <b>な-adjectives</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>きれいでした</span>, not「きれいかったです」.<br>・Don't build the past negative yourself as「静かじゃないでした」. The correct forms are <span class='gd-jp'>静かじゃありませんでした</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>静かじゃなかったです</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>あまり</span> must be followed by a negative: <span class='gd-jp'>あまり暇じゃありませんでした</span>. You can't say「あまり暇でした」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>The past of <b>い-adjectives</b> works completely differently: change い to <span class='gd-jp'>かった</span> and add です. Take the hotel you stayed at yesterday:<br><span class='gd-jp'>ホテルは静かでした。</span> (The hotel was quiet: な-adjective)<br><span class='gd-jp'>ホテルは高かったです。</span> (The hotel was expensive: い-adjective)<br>Likewise, you can't put でした after an い-adjective: you can't say「高いでした」.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>になりました</b> means \"<b>has become</b> ~\". It talks about a change, not just a past state:<br><span class='gd-jp'>町は静かでした。</span> (The town was quiet back then.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>町は静かになりました。</span> (The town has become quiet: it wasn't before.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: first check whether it's a な-adjective; if so, \"drop な + でした\". When you mean \"has become ~\", use <span class='gd-jp'>になりました</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at the い-adjective past <span class='gd-jp'>〜かったです</span>. Practice the two together and you won't mix them up anymore.</div>","n5-8":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>This is the pattern you use for \"how something was\": 「It <b>was cold</b> yesterday」「The ramen <b>was delicious</b>」. In English you just change \"is\" to \"was\". In Japanese, an い-adjective (an adjective ending in 「い」, like <span class='gd-jp'>暑い</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>おいしい</span>) has to <b>change its own form</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暑いです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暑かったです</span></span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to conjugate</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Past · affirmative</span>Drop the final 「い」 and add 「かったです」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暑い</span> → drop い → <span class='gd-jp'>暑かったです</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>おいしい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>おいしかったです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>楽しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>楽しかったです</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日は寒かったです。</span>(It was cold yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このラーメンはおいしかったです。</span>(This ramen was delicious.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Past · negative</span>Change 「い」 to 「く」 and add 「なかったです」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>難しい</span> → change い to く → <span class='gd-jp'>難しくなかったです</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>テストは難しくなかったです。</span>(The test wasn't difficult.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The alternative <span class='gd-jp'>難しくありませんでした</span> means the same thing and sounds a little more formal. Both are correct.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>The special one</span><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> (good) has to go back to <span class='gd-jp'>よい</span> before it conjugates. Just memorize these:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よかったです</span></span> (it was good), <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よくなかったです</span></span> (it wasn't good)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>天気はよくなかったです。</span>(The weather wasn't very good.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'>A friend comes back from a trip and you ask how it was. It goes like this:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>旅行はどうでしたか。</span>(How was the trip?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>とても楽しかったです。</span>(It was a lot of fun.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The question uses <span class='gd-jp'>でした</span>, but the answer is <span class='gd-jp'>楽しかったです</span>. Don't let the question pull you into saying 「楽しいでした」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>You can't say</b> <span class='gd-jp'>暑いでした</span>. The past of an い-adjective relies on 「かった」, not on adding でした.<br>・Don't say <span class='gd-jp'>暑かったでした</span> either. 「かった」 is already past. The です after it only makes the sentence polite, so it doesn't need to become でした.<br>・There's a small っ in the middle of 「かった」, so pause for one beat: あつ<b>か</b>っ<b>た</b>. Saying 「あつかた」 sounds odd.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> can't become 「いかったです」 or 「いくなかったです」. Always conjugate from <span class='gd-jp'>よ</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>有名</span> end in the sound 「い」, but they're な-adjectives. You can't say 「きれかったです」. Say <span class='gd-jp'>きれいでした</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Talking about \"last week's trip\" in three ways:<br><b>い-adjective</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>ホテルは高かったです。</span> (The hotel was expensive.)<br><b>な-adjective</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>ホテルはきれいでした。</span> (The hotel was beautiful.)<br><b>Noun</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>天気は雨でした。</span> (The weather was rainy.)<br>An い-adjective changes itself into 「かった」. な-adjectives and nouns just swap です for でした.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: look up the word in a dictionary. If it's an い-adjective ending in 「い」, use 「〜かったです」. If it isn't (like きれい, 元気, 雨), use 「〜でした」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Drop です and you get the casual way friends talk: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日、寒かった。</span> (It was so cold yesterday.) You can also go on to 「〜くなります」, which means \"become ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>寒くなりました。</span> (It's gotten cold.)</div>","n5-9":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ませんか」 is the Japanese way of saying <b>\"Would you like to ~ with me?\"</b> or \"Want to ~?\". Literally it looks like \"Won't you ~?\", and that's the idea: Japanese people use it to politely ask \"Would you be willing to ~?\" The choice is left to the other person, which makes it easy for them to decline, so it sounds courteous.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Invitations</span>Asking someone to go somewhere or do something with you.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今度の日曜日、山登りに行きませんか。</span>(Would you like to go hiking this Sunday?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一緒に昼ご飯を食べませんか。</span>(Would you like to have lunch together?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Offering food or drink, making suggestions</span>It's also very common for offering someone something to eat or drink, or for suggesting they do something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このケーキ、食べませんか。</span>(Would you like some of this cake?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>少し休みませんか。</span>(How about taking a short break?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in ます form, then swap 「ます」 for 「<b>ませんか</b>」. That's it.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行きませんか</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べませんか</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しませんか</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ませんか</span>（きませんか）</span><br>It's really just 「ません」 (the negative) plus the question marker 「か」, a bit like English \"Won't you join me?\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜でも〜ませんか</span>Adding 「でも」 after a noun means \"something like ~\", which makes the invitation more casual and puts less pressure on the other person.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーでも飲みませんか。</span>(How about a coffee or something?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>How to answer</span>Accepting: <span class='gd-jp'>いいですね。</span> (Sounds good.), <span class='gd-jp'>ぜひ。</span> (Definitely!)<br>Declining: <span class='gd-jp'>すみません、その日はちょっと…。</span> (Sorry, that day is a bit...)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't trust your English instinct here. In an invitation, <span class='gd-jp'>行きませんか</span> means \"Would you like to go?\", not \"Aren't you going?\"<br>・When declining, saying <span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、行きません。</span> (No, I won't go.) sounds blunt and can hurt. Japanese people usually say 「<span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと…</span>」 and leave the sentence unfinished. The other person understands.<br>・To offer \"Shall <b>I</b> do ~ for you?\" you can't use ませんか. <span class='gd-jp'>持ちませんか</span> asks the other person to carry something. For \"Shall I carry it for you?\" say <span class='gd-jp'>持ちましょうか</span>.<br>・With close friends you don't need to be this polite. Use the plain form with rising intonation: <span class='gd-jp'>行かない？</span> (Wanna go?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation, an invitation to the movies:<br><b>ませんか</b> = nothing is decided yet, so you ask what the other person wants: <span class='gd-jp'>一緒に映画を見ませんか。</span> (Would you like to watch a movie together?)<br><b>ましょう</b> = it's already agreed, or you both want to do it, and you give it a push: <span class='gd-jp'>いいですね。見ましょう。</span> (Sounds good. Let's watch it.)<br><b>ましょうか</b> = you offer to help with \"Shall I ~?\": <span class='gd-jp'>チケット、買いましょうか。</span> (Shall I buy the tickets?)</div><div class='gd-sec'>So the most common order is: invite with 「ませんか」 first, and once the other person says yes, decide together with 「ましょう」. Opening with 「ましょう」 feels a bit like deciding for them without asking.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: still asking \"would you like to?\" → ませんか. Already agreed on \"let's do it\" → ましょう. Offering \"let me help you\" → ましょうか.</div>","n5-12":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ましょう」 means <b>\"Let's ～.\"</b> When you say \"Let's go\" or \"Let's take a break\" to a classmate, the Japanese is <span class='gd-jp'>行きましょう</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>休みましょう</span>. It's polite, so you can use it with teachers, coworkers, and people you've just met.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First put the verb in 「〜ます」 form, then just <b>swap ます for ましょう</b>. You don't need to worry about which verb group it belongs to.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行きましょう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べましょう</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しましょう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ましょう</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Inviting someone to join you</span>Use it when everyone does something together. It often pairs with <span class='gd-jp'>一緒に</span> (together).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一緒に帰りましょう。</span>(Let's go home together.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと休みましょう。</span>(Let's take a short break.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Kicking things off</span>Teachers and meeting leaders say this a lot.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>じゃあ、始めましょう。</span>(Okay, let's begin.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Adding か: ましょうか</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>\"Shall I help you?\"</span>You offer to do something for the other person. It's a very considerate way to say it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>荷物を持ちましょうか。</span>(Shall I carry your bags?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Working it out together</span>You've already decided to do something together and now you're asking about the details. A question word like 「何」「どこ」「何時」 often comes first.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何時に会いましょうか。</span>(What time shall we meet?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・ます gets <b>replaced</b>, not followed by something extra. It's <span class='gd-jp'>行きましょう</span>, not 「行きますましょう」.<br>・Say 「ましょう」 with a long vowel, <b>ましょー</b>. Don't clip it to a short 「ましょ」.<br>・The meaning <b>includes you</b>. It doesn't tell the other person to go do it alone. To say \"please go ～\", use 「〜てください」. Public signs like <span class='gd-jp'>ゴミは持ち帰りましょう</span> (Please take your trash home) are a softer, special use. Don't use it that way in your own speech for now.<br>・When you're inviting someone for the first time and don't know if they want to come, saying 「行きましょう」 outright sounds as if you've already decided for them. It's more polite to ask with 「〜ませんか」 first.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same \"invite someone to a movie\" situation. These three often show up one after another:<br><b>ませんか</b> = first asking whether the other person wants to: <span class='gd-jp'>映画を見ませんか。</span> (Would you like to watch a movie?)<br><b>ましょう</b> = they said yes, and you move forward together: <span class='gd-jp'>ええ、見ましょう。</span> (Sure, let's watch one.)<br><b>ましょうか</b> = asking for their opinion: <span class='gd-jp'>何を見ましょうか。</span> (What shall we watch?)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if the other person hasn't agreed yet, use 「ませんか」. If it's settled and you're doing it together, use 「ましょう」. If you want their opinion or you're offering to help, use 「ましょうか」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>With close friends you don't need to be this formal. You can switch to a more casual form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きましょう</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行こう</span></span>. You'll learn this at N4 as the volitional form.</div>","n5-13":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Japanese has two ways to say the same thing. <b>Polite style</b> is the <b>polite version</b> with 「です・ます」, and <b>plain style</b> is the <b>friend version</b> with です・ます removed. The meaning is exactly the same; only the tone differs. It's like texting your boss “Certainly, I'll be there tomorrow” but just replying “Yep, be there tomorrow” to a friend.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Polite style</span>Use it with teachers, bosses, shop staff, customers, people you're meeting for the first time, and older people you don't know well. When you're unsure which to use, polite style is never rude.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Plain style</span>Use it with family, close friends, classmates, and people younger than you. It's also used for diaries, talking to yourself, and your own notes, since there's no one you need to be polite to.</div><div class='gd-h'>One sentence, two ways to say it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Say you want to ask, “Are you going tomorrow?”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、行きますか。</span>(Will you be going tomorrow? — to a teacher)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>はい、行きます。</span>(Yes, I'll go.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、行く？</span>(Going tomorrow? — to a friend)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うん、行く。</span>(Yeah, I'm going.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Notice the friend-version question: <b>the final か disappears and your voice rises at the end instead</b>. The answer 「はい」 also becomes 「うん」.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to convert: polite to plain</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: replace です with だ.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生です</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生だ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静かです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かだ</span></span><br>Negative and past forms change too:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃありません</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生でした</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生だった</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: just drop です, and <b>don't add だ</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高いです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高い</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高くないです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高くない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高かったです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高かった</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: change the ます form back to the dictionary form, the form you'd find in a dictionary.<br>Group 1 (godan verbs): change the sound before ます to the う-row sound in the same row.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> → change き to く → <span class='gd-jp'>行く</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span></span><br>Group 2 (ichidan verbs): drop ます and add る.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見る</span></span><br>Group 3 (する・来る): just memorize them.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>する</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来る</span>（くる）</span><br>Other common ones: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きません</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きました</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読んでいます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んでいる</span></span><br>The details of how negative and past forms change will be taught step by step in the lessons on the dictionary form / ない form and the た form.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't add だ to い-adjectives. Just say <span class='gd-jp'>高い</span>; 「高いだ」 is wrong.<br>・In casual chat, a bare 「<span class='gd-jp'>雨だ。</span>」 sounds blunt, like talking to yourself or announcing something. People usually add よ or ね: <span class='gd-jp'>明日、雨だよ。</span> (It's going to rain tomorrow, you know.)<br>・In plain-style questions with nouns and な-adjectives, drop だ: <span class='gd-jp'>学生？</span> (Are you a student?), not 「学生だ？」.<br>・Plain style isn't \"rude\"; it means \"close.\" Using it with a teacher is impolite. Conversely, if you keep using です・ます with a good friend, they'll find you distant, as if you were upset with them.<br>・When talking to one person, beginners should stick to one style. Don't mix ます in one sentence and plain style in the next, because it sounds confusing.<br>・In speech, <span class='gd-jp'>読んでいる</span> is often shortened to <span class='gd-jp'>読んでる</span>. When you hear it, know that it means the same thing.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Plain style isn't only for chatting. Many grammar patterns must follow plain style, even when the whole sentence is polite: <span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨だと思います。</span> (I think it will rain tomorrow.)<br>Next, you can look at the plain-form summary, which lays out the present, past, affirmative, and negative of each word type in one place.</div>","n5-17":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「の」 works like English <b>\"'s\"</b> or <b>\"of\"</b>. It links two nouns, and the first one describes the second: <span class='gd-jp'>私の本</span> = my book. The order is like \"'s\": first you say whose it is or where it's from, then the thing itself.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun A + の + Noun B = \"B of A\" or \"A's B\". B is the main noun, and A adds detail about which B you mean.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>弟</span>＋<span class='gd-jp'>車</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>弟の車</span></span> (my younger brother's car)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>数学</span>＋<span class='gd-jp'>教科書</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>数学の教科書</span></span> (math textbook)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Whose</span>Says who something belongs to.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは私の本です。</span> (This is my book.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Belonging to</span>Says which company or school a person belongs to. It's very common in self-introductions and on the phone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>さくら銀行の山田です。</span> (This is Yamada from Sakura Bank.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>What it's about</span>Says what a thing is about or what kind of thing it is.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>数学の教科書を忘れました。</span> (I forgot my math textbook.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Where from</span>Says where a thing or person comes from or is located.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>台湾のお茶はおいしいです。</span> (Taiwanese tea is delicious.)</div><div class='gd-h'>The second noun can be dropped</div><div class='gd-sec'>When everyone already knows what you're talking about, you can leave out B and just say 「A の」. <span class='gd-jp'>私の</span> simply means \"mine\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「これは誰の傘ですか。」「私のです。」</span> (\"Whose umbrella is this?\" \"It's mine.\")</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>English often has no \"'s\" or \"of\" where Japanese needs の.</b> In English you say \"my mother\", \"my friend\" and \"Japanese teacher\" with nothing linking the words. In Japanese you must say <span class='gd-jp'>私の母</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>私の友達</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>日本語の先生</span>. Saying 「私母」 or 「日本語先生」 is wrong.<br>・<b>Chains of の are fine.</b> \"My friend's car\" has only one \"'s\" in English, but Japanese needs two の: <span class='gd-jp'>私の友達の車</span>.<br>・<b>\"This book\" is <span class='gd-jp'>この本</span>, not 「これの本」.</b> <span class='gd-jp'>この</span> already means \"this\", so attach the noun directly. Don't add another の.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English, an adjective just goes in front of the noun: \"a Japanese car\", \"a new car\", \"a pretty car\". Japanese changes depending on the type of word in front:<br><b>Nouns</b> (people, things, places) take の: <span class='gd-jp'>日本の車</span> (a Japanese car, a car from Japan)<br><b>い-adjectives</b> (like <span class='gd-jp'>新しい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>おいしい</span>) attach directly, with no の: <span class='gd-jp'>新しい車</span> (a new car), not 「新しいの車」<br><b>な-adjectives</b> (like <span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>静か</span>) take な: <span class='gd-jp'>きれいな車</span> (a pretty car), not 「きれいの車」</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: use の when what comes before it is a noun (who, what thing, where). Don't use の when it's an adjective describing \"what it's like\".</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「の」 has another job. After a verb, it turns an action into \"the thing of doing it\", as in <span class='gd-jp'>本を読むのが好きです。</span> (I like reading books.) You'll meet this in the lesson on the nominalizing 「の」. It's a different use from the \"'s\" meaning here.</div>","n5-18":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「か」 is Japanese's <b>question marker</b>. Add か to the end of a sentence and a statement becomes a question: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生です</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生ですか</span></span>, \"I'm a student\" becomes \"Are you a student?\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The polite form is easy. When the sentence ends in <b>です</b> or <b>ます</b>, just add か. Nothing else changes.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>日本人です</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日本人ですか</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高いです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いですか</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静かです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かですか</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行きますか</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べました</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べましたか</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Yes/no questions</span>Questions the other person can answer with 「はい」 or 「いいえ」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは日本の本ですか。</span> (Is this a Japanese book?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このラーメン、おいしいですか。</span> (Is this ramen good?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、学校へ行きますか。</span> (Are you going to school tomorrow?)</div><div class='gd-sec'>To answer a noun question, you often say <span class='gd-jp'>はい、そうです。</span> (Yes, that's right.) or <span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、違います。</span> (No, it isn't.) For verbs and adjectives, repeat the original word: <span class='gd-jp'>はい、行きます。</span> (Yes, I'll go.) / <span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、行きません。</span> (No, I won't go.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>With question words</span>When the sentence has a word like \"what\", \"where\" or \"when\", you still add か.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>すみません、駅はどこですか。</span> (Excuse me, where is the station?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日、何を食べましたか。</span> (What did you eat yesterday?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Add か even with a question word.</b> In English, \"What is that?\" needs nothing extra beyond the question word and word order, so many learners forget か. In polite Japanese you still need it: say <span class='gd-jp'>何ですか</span>, not 「何です」.<br>・<b>Raise your intonation.</b> The か in 「ですか」 goes up. Take <span class='gd-jp'>そうですか</span>: said with a rising tone it means \"Is that so?\" With a falling tone it becomes \"Oh, I see,\" showing you understood. The meaning is different.<br>・<b>With friends, you usually drop か.</b> Use the plain form without です or ます and raise your tone at the end to make it a question: <span class='gd-jp'>明日、行く？</span> (Going tomorrow?), <span class='gd-jp'>これ、おいしい？</span> (Is this good?) Adding か directly to the plain form (like 「行くか」) sounds blunt and rough, so beginners should avoid it.<br>・With friends, a noun question is <span class='gd-jp'>学生？</span>. Don't say 「学生だか」, which isn't a question.<br>・<b>Punctuation:</b> Textbooks and formal writing usually put 「。」 after か, since か already marks the question. In LINE messages and manga you'll often see 「？」. You'll see both.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ますか</b> = simply asks whether the other person will or wants to do something:<br><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、パーティーに行きますか。</span> (Are you going to the party tomorrow?)</div><b>ませんか</b> = <b>invites</b> the other person to do something with you:<br><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、一緒にパーティーに行きませんか。</span> (Would you like to go to the party together tomorrow?)</div>The two sentences differ by only a little, but the first asks about the other person's plans, while the second invites them along.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you just want to know whether they'll do it, use 「ますか」. If you want to ask them to do it with you, use 「ませんか」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Learn question words like 「何、どこ、いつ、だれ」 and you can ask all kinds of questions. Then learn 「〜ませんか」 and you can start inviting people.</div>","n5-21":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put 「で」 after a vehicle and it means <b>\"by ~\"</b>, or \"take / ride / drive ~\". English changes the verb depending on the vehicle (take the train, ride a bike, drive a car). Japanese doesn't care what you travel in. It's always \"vehicle + で\", followed by <span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>帰ります</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Vehicle + で + 行きます／来ます／帰ります. Put the noun right in front, with no changes to its form.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車で会社に行きます。</span>(I go to the office by train.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>自転車で学校へ行きます。</span>(I go to school by bike.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>バスで帰ります。</span>(I go home by bus.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>飛行機で来ました。</span>(I came by plane.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Notice that English uses \"take,\" \"ride\" and \"drive\", but Japanese uses the same 「で」 for all of them. <span class='gd-jp'>車で行きます</span> works the same way. You might be driving yourself or riding in someone else's car, and Japanese doesn't distinguish.</div><div class='gd-h'>Asking \"How did you get here?\"</div><div class='gd-sec'>Just replace the vehicle with 「何」:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何で来ましたか。</span>(How did you come here?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>タクシーで来ました。</span>(I came by taxi.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here 「何で」 is read <b>なにで</b>. If you say なんで, it's easily heard as \"Why did you come?\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>No で for walking</b>. Don't say 「足で行きます」. Say <span class='gd-jp'>歩いて行きます</span> (I walk there). Just memorize this as a whole phrase for now.<br>・The action of getting on a bus takes 「に」: <span class='gd-jp'>バスに乗ります</span> (I get on the bus). Many learners say 「バスで乗ります」, which is wrong.<br>・The order can change: <span class='gd-jp'>電車で東京へ行きます</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>東京へ電車で行きます</span> are both correct. Just keep 「で」 right after the vehicle.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix up the look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>で (vehicle)</b> = what you \"travel\" by: <span class='gd-jp'>バスで駅へ行きます。</span> (I go to the station by bus.)<br><b>に + 乗ります</b> = which vehicle you \"get on\": <span class='gd-jp'>駅でバスに乗ります。</span> (I get on the bus at the station.)<br><b>で (place of action)</b> = where you do something: the <span class='gd-jp'>駅で</span> in the sentence above means \"at the station\". What comes before it is a place, not a vehicle.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what follows is \"go, come, return\", then で after a vehicle is right. If what follows is 「乗ります」, use に. If what comes before is a place rather than a vehicle, that で means \"at ~\".</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>This 「で」 really means \"using ~\", and it isn't limited to vehicles. Eating with chopsticks or speaking in Japanese use it too: <span class='gd-jp'>はしで食べます。</span> (I eat with chopsticks.) That's the next card, \"Particle で (means / tools)\".</div>","n5-22":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「と」 means <b>\"with ~\"</b>. To say \"I watch a movie with a friend\", add と after 「友達」: <span class='gd-jp'>友達と映画を見ます</span>. What comes before と is <b>the person doing this with you</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's simple: <b>person + と + what you do</b>. To stress \"together\", you can add <span class='gd-jp'>一緒に</span> (together) after と. The meaning is the same, but the \"together\" feeling is stronger.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>友達と</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>友達と一緒に</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達と映画を見ました。</span>(I watched a movie with a friend.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母と一緒に料理を作ります。</span>(I cook together with my mom.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎朝、犬と散歩します。</span>(I take a walk with my dog every morning.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>To ask \"with whom\", use <span class='gd-jp'>誰と</span>. You can answer simply with \"person + とです\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰と行きますか。</span>(Who are you going with?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達とです。</span>(With a friend.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>If you want to say \"I\" too, mark the main person with は and the companion with と: <span class='gd-jp'>私は友達と京都へ行きます。</span> (I'm going to Kyoto with a friend.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・When you do something alone, say <span class='gd-jp'>一人で</span>, not 一人と. Numbers of people and whole groups also take で: <span class='gd-jp'>二人で</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>家族で</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>みんなで</span>.<br>・一緒に is an optional extra. と alone already means \"together with someone\". Conversely, when you say only 一緒に without naming who, it's because everyone already knows who: <span class='gd-jp'>一緒に帰りましょう。</span> (Let's go home together.)<br>・Things that need two people, like <span class='gd-jp'>結婚する</span>, also use と, and usually without 一緒に: <span class='gd-jp'>田中さんと結婚しました。</span> (I married Mr. Tanaka.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix up the look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>と has another use: connecting two things, like \"A and B\" (a list). Compare these two sentences:<br><span class='gd-jp'>母とケーキを食べました。</span> (I ate cake with my mom.) → Mom is <b>the person eating with you</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>パンとケーキを食べました。</span> (I ate bread and cake.) → Bread and cake are both <b>things that were eaten</b></div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what comes before と is a person who does the action with you, it's \"with someone\", which is what you're learning here. If と sits between two things side by side, it's \"A and B\".</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Combine it with 「〜ませんか」 to invite someone: <span class='gd-jp'>週末、私と映画を見ませんか。</span> (Would you like to see a movie with me this weekend?)</div>","n5-14":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>There are two polite ways to say \"don't/doesn't ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>食べません</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>食べないです</span>. Both mean \"don't eat.\" The difference is a bit like wearing a suit versus a shirt: <b>ません is more formal</b> and <b>ないです is more relaxed</b>. Both are polite, so you can safely use either with a teacher or a shop clerk.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first check the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'>The <span class='gd-jp'>ません</span> version is easy. Just replace ます with ません: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みません</span></span><br>The <span class='gd-jp'>ないです</span> version takes the ない form (the casual \"don't ~\" form of a verb) and adds <span class='gd-jp'>です</span> after it:</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the あ-row sound of the same column, add ない, then add です.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → change む to ま → <span class='gd-jp'>飲まないです</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰らないです</span></span><br>If the last sound is 「う」, change it to 「わ」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買わないです</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop 「る」 and add ないです.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べないです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見ないです</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しないです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ないです</span>（こないです）</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Adjectives and nouns also have two versions</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: change the final 「い」 to 「く」, then add ないです or ありません.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒くないです</span></span>＝<span class='gd-jp'>寒くありません</span><br><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: add じゃないです or じゃありません directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かじゃないです</span></span>＝<span class='gd-jp'>静かじゃありません</span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃないです</span></span>＝<span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃありません</span><br>To make it even more formal, change じゃ to では: <span class='gd-jp'>学生ではありません</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'>The past negative (\"didn't ~\") also has two versions: <span class='gd-jp'>食べませんでした</span>＝<span class='gd-jp'>食べなかったです</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>寒くありませんでした</span>＝<span class='gd-jp'>寒くなかったです</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use which</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Formal situations</span>For emails, work, interviews, and talking to your elders, ません is the safer choice.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は会社に行きません。</span> (I'm not going to the office tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は学生ではありません。</span> (I'm not a student.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Everyday conversation</span>When chatting with shop clerks, neighbors, or classmates you don't know well, you'll hear ないです a lot. With adjectives, <span class='gd-jp'>くないです</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>じゃないです</span> are often even more common than ありません in everyday speech.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>肉はあまり食べないです。</span> (I don't eat meat much.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店、高くないですよ。</span> (This shop isn't expensive, you know.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日はそんなに寒くないですね。</span> (It's not that cold today, is it?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't mix the two: there is no <span class='gd-jp'>食べないません</span> and no <span class='gd-jp'>食べませんです</span>.<br>・The negative of <span class='gd-jp'>ある</span> is special. It becomes <span class='gd-jp'>ない</span> directly, and there is no あらない: <span class='gd-jp'>時間がありません</span>＝<span class='gd-jp'>時間がないです</span> (I have no time).<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>よくないです</span>, not いくないです.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>買わない</span>, not 買あない.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span> is read <b>こない</b>, not きない.<br>・The two forms aren't interchangeable everywhere: <span class='gd-jp'>すみません</span> is a fixed apology and never becomes すまないです. Likewise, when inviting someone, <span class='gd-jp'>一緒に行きませんか</span> (Shall we go together?) is the most natural and polite with ません.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>After learning ないです, many learners start dropping <span class='gd-jp'>です</span> in the middle of a conversation. Here are three ways to say \"I'm not going today\":<br>To a teacher: <span class='gd-jp'>今日は行きません。</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>今日は行かないです。</span> (both are fine)<br>To a friend: <span class='gd-jp'>今日は行かない。</span> (the casual version, too blunt for a teacher)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if the sentence ends with <span class='gd-jp'>ます</span> (ません) or <span class='gd-jp'>です</span>, it's polite. If it has neither, it's casual.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>To find out when you can drop です and just say <span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span>, check out the lessons \"丁寧体與普通体\" and \"辞書形・ない形\".</div>","n5-16":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「も」 means <b>\"also\" or \"too\"</b>. The position works differently from English, though. English can say \"I <b>also</b> drink\" (before the verb), but Japanese 「も」 attaches <b>directly after the thing that is \"also\"</b> (<span class='gd-jp'>私も</span> = me too).</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>は・が・を</span>These three are <b>removed and replaced by も</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>私は</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>私も</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>猫が</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>猫も</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーを</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーも</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>に・で・へ・と</span>These are <b>kept</b>, and も is added after them.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大阪に</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大阪にも</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>家で</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>家でも</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>大阪にも行きました。</span> (I went to Osaka too.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Also</span>You've already mentioned one thing, and now you add another that's the same.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>A：私は学生です。B：私も学生です。</span> (A: I'm a student. B: I'm a student too.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>A：コーヒーが好きです。B：私も。</span> (A: I like coffee. B: Me too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>AもBも</span>Both are the same: \"both A and B ~\". With a negative ending, it means \"neither A nor B\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>パンもケーキも好きです。</span> (I like both bread and cake.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>肉も魚も食べません。</span> (I eat neither meat nor fish.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Question word + も + negative</span>\"Nothing,\" \"no one,\" \"nowhere\". A negative must always follow.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今朝は何も食べませんでした。</span> (I didn't eat anything this morning.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>教室には誰もいません。</span> (There's no one in the classroom.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末はどこにも行きませんでした。</span> (I didn't go anywhere this weekend.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・There is no <span class='gd-jp'>はも</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>がも</span>, or <span class='gd-jp'>をも</span>. は, が, and を are simply replaced by も.<br>・Whatever も attaches to is the thing that is \"also,\" so the meaning can change a lot:<br><span class='gd-jp'>私もコーヒーを飲みます。</span> (Other people drink coffee, and <b>I</b> drink it too.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>私はコーヒーも飲みます。</span> (I drink tea, and I drink <b>coffee</b> too.)<br>English can say \"I drink coffee too\" for both, but in Japanese you need to decide first whether the \"also\" is the person or the coffee.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>何も</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>誰も</span> are followed by a negative. To say \"I like everything,\" you can't say 何も好きです. You need a different expression (like <span class='gd-jp'>何でも好きです</span>, which you'll learn later).<br>・To say \"didn't go anywhere,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>どこにも</span>. Don't leave out に.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>AとB</b> = simply listing two things: <span class='gd-jp'>パンとケーキが好きです。</span> (I like bread and cake.)<br><b>AもBも</b> = stressing \"<b>both</b>\": <span class='gd-jp'>パンもケーキも好きです。</span> (Bread, cake, I like both.)<br>The way you attach them is different too: と goes only once, in the middle, but も goes <b>after every item</b>.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: use と to simply list things, and use 〜も〜も when you want to stress \"both ~\".</div>","n5-19":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「に」 goes after a time word and works like English <b>at</b> 7 or <b>in</b> March. It pins an event to a specific point in time. Whenever the time has a <b>number</b>, Japanese requires に: <span class='gd-jp'>7時に起きます。</span> (I get up at 7.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Just add に right after the time word.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>7時</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>7時に</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>3月</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>3月に</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>2025年</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>2025年に</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to add it and when not to</div><div class='gd-sec'>This is the part of the grammar you really need to remember. Time words fall into three groups:</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Always add it</span>Times with a number: hour, minute, month, year, date.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>朝6時に散歩します。</span> (I go for a walk at 6 in the morning.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>3月に日本へ行きます。</span> (I'm going to Japan in March.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Clearly specific days without a number also take it, such as <span class='gd-jp'>誕生日</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>クリスマス</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>夏休み</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誕生日にケーキを食べます。</span> (I eat cake on my birthday.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Optional</span>Days of the week. Adding it is a bit clearer, so as a beginner, <b>adding it is the safest choice</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>金曜日に歓迎会があります。</span> (There's a welcome party on Friday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日曜日に休みます。</span> (I rest on Sunday.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Never add it</span>① Times measured from \"now\": <span class='gd-jp'>今日</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>明日</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>昨日</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>今週</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>来年</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>今</span>.<br>How to tell: these words point to a different day depending on when you say them. They move along with \"now\", so no に. (English works the same way: we don't say \"at tomorrow\".)<br>② \"Every ～\" times: <span class='gd-jp'>毎日</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>毎朝</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>毎週</span>. These describe a repeating pattern, how often something happens, not one point in time, so no に either.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、映画を見ます。</span> (I'm going to watch a movie tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎朝コーヒーを飲みます。</span> (I drink coffee every morning.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's also very common to combine the two kinds:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎朝7時に起きます。</span> (I get up at 7 every morning.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日の3時に会いましょう。</span> (Let's meet at 3 tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>When one sentence has both kinds, just apply the rule to each one separately. Times based on now (<span class='gd-jp'>明日</span>…) and \"every ～\" times (<span class='gd-jp'>毎朝</span>…) get no に; times with a number (<span class='gd-jp'>7時</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>3時</span>…) do.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Dropping に: \"7時起きます\" is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>7時に起きます</span>.<br>・Adding too much: \"明日に行きます\" and \"毎日に勉強します\" are both wrong. After <span class='gd-jp'>明日</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>毎日</span>, just continue the sentence.<br>・When asking about time, <span class='gd-jp'>何時</span> takes に but <span class='gd-jp'>いつ</span> doesn't: <span class='gd-jp'>何時に起きますか。</span> (What time do you get up?) and <span class='gd-jp'>いつ日本へ行きますか。</span> (When are you going to Japan?)<br>・「時」 and 「時間」 are different: <span class='gd-jp'>2時に勉強します</span> means \"I study at 2 o'clock\", while <span class='gd-jp'>2時間勉強します</span> means \"I study for two hours\". A <b>length</b> of time takes no に.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>With the same \"7 o'clock\", a different particle changes the meaning:<br><b>に</b> = at that exact point: <span class='gd-jp'>7時に起きます。</span> (I get up at 7.)<br><b>から</b> = starting from that time and continuing: <span class='gd-jp'>7時から勉強します。</span> (I study from 7.)<br><b>まで</b> = up until that time: <span class='gd-jp'>7時まで寝ます。</span> (I sleep until 7.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: English \"at 7\" is に, \"from 7\" is から, and \"until 7\" is まで.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>に does more than mark time. It can also mark where something is (n5-33, location of existence): <span class='gd-jp'>部屋に猫がいます。</span> (There's a cat in the room.) It's the same に; just check whether the word before it is a time or a place.</div>","n5-20":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「へ」 means <b>\"toward ～\"</b>. Placed after a place, it tells the listener where you're moving: <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行きます</span> is literally \"go toward Japan\", that is, \"go to Japan\". It's written へ, but as a particle it's <b>pronounced \"e\"</b>, not \"he\".</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Place + へ + a verb of movement (such as <span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>帰ります</span>).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学校</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学校へ行きます</span></span> (go to school)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、京都へ行きます。</span> (I'm going to Kyoto tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは去年日本へ来ました。</span> (Mr. Tanaka came to Japan last year.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>6時にうちへ帰ります。</span> (I go home at six.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>To ask where someone is going, swap the place for <span class='gd-jp'>どこ</span>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末、どこへ行きますか。</span> (Where are you going this weekend?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Pronunciation: the particle へ is read \"e\", just like the particle <span class='gd-jp'>は</span> is read \"wa\". But when <b>writing, always write へ</b>, never え.<br>・It must be followed by a verb of movement. \"Go to the library to study\" sounds natural in English, but the place where you do the action (\"study at the library\") takes <span class='gd-jp'>で</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>図書館で勉強します</span>. You can't say <span class='gd-jp'>図書館へ勉強します</span>.<br>・Times can't take へ. \"Go tomorrow\" is <span class='gd-jp'>明日、行きます</span>, not 明日へ.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>へ vs に</span>When talking about going, coming or returning somewhere, the two are mostly interchangeable and mean the same thing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>東京へ行きます。</span> (I'm going to Tokyo.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>東京に行きます。</span> (I'm going to Tokyo.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>If you insist on a difference: <b>へ</b> feels like \"in that direction\", while <b>に</b> feels like \"arriving at that point\". In everyday conversation に is used more. When the focus is on arrival, as with <span class='gd-jp'>着きます</span> (arrive), に is the normal choice:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅に着きました。</span> (I arrived at the station.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>へ vs で</span>へ is \"going to a place\"; で is \"doing something at a place\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>図書館へ行きます。</span> (I'm going to the library.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>図書館で勉強します。</span> (I study at the library.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if the verb is a movement verb like 「行く、来る、帰る」, either へ or に works; for \"doing something at a place\", use で. Times can't take へ: specific points like 7時 or 月曜日 take に, while words like 明日 and 来週 take no particle at all.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Pair it with <span class='gd-jp'>から</span> (from) and <span class='gd-jp'>まで</span> (to) and you can state both the starting point and the destination. Then learn \"で (means of transport)\" and you can say <span class='gd-jp'>電車で会社へ行きます</span> (I go to work by train).</div>","n5-27":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>This 「と」 works like English <b>\"and\"</b>: you list things one by one. It also carries a nuance: <b>what I listed is everything, nothing else</b>. If you tell a friend 「パンと牛乳を買いました」, you bought only those two things.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>パンと牛乳を買いました。</span>(I bought bread and milk.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + と + noun. <b>Both sides must be nouns</b>. After you finish the list, add whatever particle the sentence needs (を, が, は…) at the very end.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>鍵</span>＋<span class='gd-jp'>財布</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>鍵と財布を持ってきました</span></span> (I brought my keys and wallet.)<br>For three or more items, put と between each one:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>月曜日と水曜日と金曜日に授業があります。</span>(I have class on Monday, Wednesday and Friday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父と母は台北に住んでいます。</span>(My father and mother live in Taipei.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's also very common when ordering food:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーとケーキをお願いします。</span>(Coffee and cake, please.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>The particle goes only after the last noun</b>. Say <span class='gd-jp'>鍵と財布を持ってきました</span>, not 「鍵を財布を持ってきました」 or 「鍵をと財布を持ってきました」.<br>・と <b>can only connect nouns</b>. English \"and\" is very flexible: \"cheap and tasty\" or \"eating and watching TV\" all work. But in Japanese you can't use と to join adjectives or actions. For those you'll use the て-form, which you'll learn later.<br>・と means \"just these.\" If there are other things you haven't mentioned, use や instead.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>と vs や</span>Both describe what you brought in your bag:<br><span class='gd-jp'>鍵と財布を持ってきました。</span> (I brought my keys and wallet: just those two.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>鍵や財布を持ってきました。</span> (I brought things like my keys and wallet: there's more.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>\"and\" vs \"with\"</span>と has another use: \"doing something together with someone.\"<br><b>Listing と</b>: ties A and B into one group, and then a particle such as は, が or を follows, so the group acts as one part of the sentence.<br><span class='gd-jp'>友達と私は映画を見ました。</span> (My friend and I watched a movie: 「友達と私」 is one group, followed by は.)<br><b>\"With\" と</b>: \"person + と\" says who you did the following action with. Even if a noun comes after it, that noun is a separate element and isn't bundled with the person before と.<br><span class='gd-jp'>友達と映画を見ました。</span> (I watched a movie with my friend: 「友達と」 says who you watched with, and 「映画を」 is the thing watched. It does not mean \"friend and movie.\")<br>When you want to stress \"together,\" you'll often hear <span class='gd-jp'>友達と一緒に映画を見ました</span>.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: take the noun before と and put it in the sentence on its own. <span class='gd-jp'>鍵を持ってきました</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>財布を持ってきました</span> both make sense, so it's \"A and B.\" But if you change 「友達と映画を見ました」 to 「友達を見ました」, the meaning goes wrong, so that と means \"together with someone.\"</div>","n5-28":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「や」 is like English <b>\"things like A, B, and so on\"</b>. There are many items, you pick just a few as examples, and you let the listener know that <b>there are others and the list isn't complete</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + <b>や</b> + noun, and often 「<b>など</b>」 (etc.) at the end. Any particle the sentence needs (を, が, に…) still goes at the very end.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>牛乳</span>＋<span class='gd-jp'>卵</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>牛乳や卵</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>牛乳や卵</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>牛乳や卵など</span></span> (Adding など makes the \"and more\" feeling even clearer.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>冷蔵庫に牛乳や卵があります。</span>(There's milk, eggs and the like in the fridge.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末は掃除や洗濯をします。</span>(On weekends I do things like cleaning and laundry.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本で寿司やラーメンなどを食べました。</span>(In Japan I ate sushi, ramen and so on.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>You can also give three examples, with や between each one:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>机の上にペンやノートや辞書などがあります。</span>(On the desk there are pens, notebooks, dictionaries and so on.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>「と」 also connects nouns, but the meaning is quite different. Both describe what's in a bag:<br><b>と</b> = <b>that's everything</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>かばんに財布と鍵があります。</span> (There's a wallet and keys in the bag, just those two.)<br><b>や</b> = <b>examples, there's more</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>かばんに財布や鍵があります。</span> (There's a wallet, keys and the like in the bag, plus other things.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can say \"just these,\" use と. If you're only giving a few examples and there's an \"…and stuff\" in your mind, use や.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・や <b>can only connect nouns</b>. To give examples of actions you can't say 「食べるや飲む」. Use 「〜たり〜たりします」: <span class='gd-jp'>食べたり飲んだりします</span> (I eat and drink, among other things).<br>・<b>Don't put や after the last noun</b>. You can't end with 「ペンやノートや」. To express \"etc.\", add など: <span class='gd-jp'>ペンやノートなど</span>.<br>・English \"and\" can also mean \"together with someone\" (\"I'm going with a friend\"), but や can't. \"I'm going with a friend\" is <span class='gd-jp'>友達と行きます</span>; you can't say 友達や行きます.<br>・You don't need a long list to use や. Even two examples are perfectly fine. What matters is the feeling that \"the list isn't complete.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The version for actions is 「〜たり〜たりします」: <span class='gd-jp'>休みの日は映画を見たり、買い物をしたりします。</span> (On days off I do things like watch movies and go shopping.)</div>","n5-23":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>「を」 is a little tag you attach after a noun. It means <b>\"this is the thing the action is done to.\"</b> In English, word order tells you what's being eaten in \"eat bread.\" Japanese puts the verb at the very end of the sentence, so 「を」 has to mark <b>what you eat, what you watch</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + を + verb. Japanese order is \"the thing first, the action last\":<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>パン</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>パンを食べます</span></span> (eat bread)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>水を飲みます。</span> (I drink water.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>映画を見ます。</span> (I watch a movie.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎日日本語を勉強します。</span> (I study Japanese every day.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Object of an action</span>This is the main, most common use: what you eat, drink, buy, do. As a beginner, get comfortable with this one first.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>コンビニで傘を買いました。</span> (I bought an umbrella at the convenience store.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Place you pass through</span>Used with movement verbs like \"walk, stroll, cross (a bridge, a road).\" It means <b>moving along or through that place</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>公園を散歩します。</span> (I take a walk in the park.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>橋を渡ります。</span> (I cross the bridge.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Place you leave</span>Used with verbs like \"leave, get off (a vehicle).\" It means <b>departing from there</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>8時に家を出ます。</span> (I leave home at 8.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>次の駅で電車を降ります。</span> (I get off the train at the next station.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・「を」 is pronounced <b>o</b>, just like 「お」. Don't say \"wo.\" But when typing on a computer or phone, you do need to type <b>wo</b> to get it.<br>・Even though they sound the same, you still have to write 「を」 and can't write 「お」 instead.<br>・\"Like, understand, want\" don't take を. The standard textbook phrasing is <span class='gd-jp'>猫が好きです</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>英語がわかります</span>, not 猫を好きです.<br>・In English you \"meet friends\" and \"take a train\" with no little word in between, but Japanese needs に: <span class='gd-jp'>友達に会います</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>電車に乗ります</span>. Getting off is <span class='gd-jp'>電車を降ります</span>, though. Getting on uses に and getting off uses を, so memorize them as a pair.<br>・<b>Don't put two を on the same verb.</b> <span class='gd-jp'>日本語を勉強をします</span> sounds unnatural (勉強します can only have one を). Say <span class='gd-jp'>日本語を勉強します</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>日本語の勉強をします</span>. Different verbs can each take their own を without a problem, for example <span class='gd-jp'>本を読んで、ご飯を食べます。</span> (I read a book, then eat.)<br>・Friends chatting often drop を: <span class='gd-jp'>ご飯、食べた？</span> (Did you eat?). That's casual speech. When you write your own sentences, put the を in first.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar ones apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>を vs が</b>: Both appear in Japanese sentences, but actions take を, while feelings and abilities take が.<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本語を勉強します。</span> (I study Japanese: an action)<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本語が好きです。</span> (I like Japanese: a feeling)<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本語がわかります。</span> (I understand Japanese: an ability)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>を vs で</b>: Take the same bridge. \"Crossing the bridge\" uses を, while \"doing something else on the bridge\" uses で.<br><span class='gd-jp'>橋を渡ります。</span> (I cross the bridge: walking across it)<br><span class='gd-jp'>橋で写真を撮ります。</span> (I take a photo on the bridge: the bridge is just where it happens, and the thing being shot, 「写真」, is what takes を)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: ask yourself, \"Is the action done directly to this thing?\" If yes, use を. \"Like, understand, want\" use が. If you're just saying \"where something happens,\" use で.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Some verbs come in pairs, one taking を and the other taking が: <span class='gd-jp'>ドアを開けます</span> (I open the door), <span class='gd-jp'>ドアが開きます</span> (the door opens). These are \"intransitive and transitive verbs,\" and they'll come easily once you've got を down.</div>","n5-24":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>「で」 is the <b>\"at/in (a place), doing (something)\"</b> of Japanese. Think of the place as a stage. 「で」 tells the listener that this action is being performed on that stage.<br><span class='gd-jp'>カフェで仕事をします。</span> (I work at a café.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add 「で」 right after the place, then the action you do there.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>図書館</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>図書館で勉強します</span></span> (study at the library)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>家</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>家で晩ご飯を食べます</span></span> (eat dinner at home)<br>The place can be big or small: a country, a city, a shop, a room, even \"here.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Doing something somewhere</span>Eating, drinking, buying, watching, studying, working, playing, waiting for someone… for any action you \"do,\" put で after the place.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅で友達を待ちます。</span> (I wait for my friend at the station.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>デパートで靴を買いました。</span> (I bought shoes at the department store.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここで写真を撮ってもいいですか。</span> (May I take a photo here?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking where</span>Use 「どこで」 to ask where something is done.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そのかばん、どこで買いましたか。</span> (Where did you buy that bag?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どこで昼ご飯を食べますか。</span> (Where will you eat lunch?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar ones apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>「で」 vs 「に」: same place, look at the verb</b><br>English says \"at the library\" either way, but Japanese cares whether you're \"staying there\" or \"doing something there.\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>図書館にいます。</span> (I'm at the library: you're simply there, so に)<br><span class='gd-jp'>図書館で勉強します。</span> (I study at the library: you're doing something there, so で)<br>If the verb is <b>いる or ある</b> (to be, to exist), use に. If it's <b>an action</b>, use で.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>「で」 vs 「へ」: going there, or already there</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>図書館へ行きます。</span> (I go to the library: moving toward it, so へ)<br><span class='gd-jp'>図書館で本を読みます。</span> (I read a book at the library: you've arrived and do something there, so で)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>The other 「で」: vehicles and tools</b><br>「で」 has another job, where what comes before it is a thing, not a place.<br><span class='gd-jp'>バスで行きます。</span> (I go by bus: a vehicle)<br><span class='gd-jp'>公園で遊びます。</span> (I play at the park: a place)<br>Just check whether the word before it is a \"place\" or a \"tool.\"</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: <b>a person or thing \"is\" there → に; \"going\" there → へ; \"doing\" something there → で.</b></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・English \"at/in\" can't always become で. \"I'm at home\" is <span class='gd-jp'>家にいます</span>, not 家でいます.<br>・\"Live in Tokyo\" takes に: <span class='gd-jp'>東京に住んでいます</span>, not 東京で住んでいます. Living somewhere means being based there, not doing a single action there. This is one of the most common mistakes among learners.<br>・\"Work\" with で is fine: <span class='gd-jp'>銀行で働いています</span> (I work at a bank). Don't switch 働く to に just because 住む uses に.<br>・With movement verbs like 行く, 来る, 帰る, the destination doesn't take で. Say <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行きます</span>, not 日本で行きます.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put 「で」 and 「を」 together and you can say a complete sentence: <b>place + で + thing + を + action</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>コンビニでお茶を買いました。</span> (I bought tea at the convenience store.)<br>Next, look at 「に」 (location of existence) and compare the two together, and you won't mix up に and で anymore.</div>","n5-32":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English you say \"I like dogs\" or \"I understand Korean.\" Dogs and Korean are the things being liked or understood, so your instinct says to use <span class='gd-jp'>を</span>. But in Japanese, the thing in front of <b>like, dislike, good at, bad at, understand, can do, and want</b> takes <b>が</b>. Just memorize these as fixed pairings.</div><div class='gd-h'>Which words take が</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Like・dislike</span><span class='gd-jp'>好き</span> (like), <span class='gd-jp'>嫌い</span> (dislike)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>チョコレートが好きです。</span> (I like chocolate.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は野菜が嫌いです。</span> (I dislike vegetables.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Good at・bad at</span><span class='gd-jp'>上手</span> (good at, skilled), <span class='gd-jp'>下手</span> (bad at, not skilled)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは歌が上手です。</span> (Mr. Tanaka is good at singing.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Understand・can do</span><span class='gd-jp'>わかります</span> (understand), <span class='gd-jp'>できます</span> (can do)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>韓国語がわかります。</span> (I understand Korean.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>テニスができます。</span> (I can play tennis.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Want</span><span class='gd-jp'>ほしい</span> (want)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>新しいかばんがほしいです。</span> (I want a new bag.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Thing (noun) + が + one of the words above.<br>To say who likes it, put \"person + は\" at the very front: <span class='gd-jp'>私は犬が好きです。</span> (I like dogs.)<br>In this sentence, <span class='gd-jp'>は</span> says \"we're talking about me,\" and <span class='gd-jp'>が</span> says \"the thing liked is dogs.\" Each one does its own job.<br>To ask what someone likes, just swap the thing for <span class='gd-jp'>何</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>何が好きですか。</span> (What do you like?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't follow your English instinct and use を. Say <span class='gd-jp'>韓国語がわかります</span>, not 韓国語をわかります. It's the same for liking: just memorize <span class='gd-jp'>犬が好きです</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>好き</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>嫌い</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>上手</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>下手</span> are not verbs. They are followed by <span class='gd-jp'>です</span>, and you can't add ます.<br>・When saying \"don't like,\" Japanese speakers very often swap が for は: <span class='gd-jp'>魚は好きじゃありません。</span> (As for fish, I don't like it.) It carries a feeling of \"other things are okay, but not this one,\" and sounds perfectly natural.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>上手</span> is used to compliment other people. Saying \"I'm good at...\" about yourself sounds like bragging, so Japanese speakers usually don't use it that way.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>The easiest one to confuse it with is <b>を</b> (the object marker). Look at the same thing placed before different words:<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本語を勉強します。</span> (I study Japanese.): studying is an action you do, so use を.<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本語がわかります。</span> (I understand Japanese.): understanding is your own ability, so use が.<br><span class='gd-jp'>料理を作ります。</span> (I cook.): an action, so use を.<br><span class='gd-jp'>料理が上手です。</span> (I'm good at cooking.): a skill, so use が.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if it's an action you \"go and do\" (eat, read, make, buy), use を. If it's a feeling inside you or an ability you have (like, dislike, good at, understand, can do, want), use が.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>For the <span class='gd-jp'>ほしい</span> pattern, move on to \"<span class='gd-jp'>〜が欲しいです・〜たいです</span>\" to learn how to say \"I want ~\" and \"I want to do ~.\"</div>","n5-33":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>This 「に」 works like the English <b>\"in / at\"</b>: it tells the listener <b>where</b> a person or thing is. In English you say \"there's milk in the fridge.\" In Japanese, you put に after \"the fridge\" and finish with あります or います.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Pattern: <b>place + に + person/thing + が + あります／います</b></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>冷蔵庫に牛乳があります。</span> (There's milk in the fridge.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>教室に先生がいます。</span> (There's a teacher in the classroom.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>あります or います?</b> Ask whether the thing can move on its own: people and animals take <span class='gd-jp'>います</span>; objects and plants take <span class='gd-jp'>あります</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to say positions</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English you say \"on the desk,\" \"in the box,\" \"in front of the station.\" In Japanese, you attach the position word with 「の」 and then add に:<br><span class='gd-jp'>机の上に</span> (on the desk), <span class='gd-jp'>箱の中に</span> (in the box), <span class='gd-jp'>駅の前に</span> (in front of the station), <span class='gd-jp'>机の下に</span> (under the desk)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅の前にコンビニがあります。</span> (There's a convenience store in front of the station.)</div><div class='gd-h'>The two patterns you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>What's there</span>Put the place first, and mark the thing you're describing with が.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>公園に子供がいます。</span> (There are children in the park.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Where it is</span>To say where a particular thing is, put that thing first with は. に still follows the place.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>猫は机の下にいます。</span> (The cat is under the desk.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>トイレはどこにありますか。</span> (Where is the restroom?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Here あります／います mean that a person or thing \"exists\" in that place. When あります means an event or activity \"takes place\" (e.g. <span class='gd-jp'>パーティーがあります</span>), the place takes で: <span class='gd-jp'>明日、学校で試験があります。</span> (There's an exam at school tomorrow.)<br>・English uses \"in\" both for \"there are kids in the park\" and \"play in the park,\" but Japanese separates them. Playing is an action, so use で: <span class='gd-jp'>公園で遊びます</span>, not 公園に遊びます.<br>・You can't use あります for people and animals: it's <span class='gd-jp'>先生がいます</span>, not 先生があります.<br>・The place where someone lives also takes に: <span class='gd-jp'>姉は大阪に住んでいます。</span> (My older sister lives in Osaka.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>The one most easily confused with it is <b>で (place of action)</b>. Even with the same park, look at what the sentence is saying:<br><span class='gd-jp'>公園に子供がいます。</span> (There are children in the park: it only says they are there.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>公園で子供が遊んでいます。</span> (Children are playing in the park: an action happening there.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>公園でお祭りがあります。</span> (There's a festival in the park: an event held there.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: check the end of the sentence. あります／います meaning a person or thing \"is there\" (and also 住んでいます) takes に. Actions like eating, playing, buying, and working, or an あります meaning an event is \"held\" (e.g. <span class='gd-jp'>ホテルでパーティーがあります</span>), take で.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The \"existence expressions\" lesson teaches how to say \"there's nothing\" and \"there's nobody\": <span class='gd-jp'>部屋に誰もいません。</span> (There's nobody in the room.)</div>","n5-35":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put 「が」 at the end of a clause and it works like English <b>“…, but …”</b>. You say one thing, then add something that goes against what you'd expect. For “This restaurant is expensive, but it's delicious,” Japanese says <span class='gd-jp'>この店は高いですが、おいしいです。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The first clause must be a <b>complete sentence</b>. At N5 that usually ends in 「です」 or 「ます」. Add が, put a comma, then continue with the second clause.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高いです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いですが、</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨です</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨ですが、</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きたいです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行きたいですが、</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強しました</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強しましたが、</span></span><br>If the first half uses 「です・ます」, the second half should too, so the whole sentence stays at the same politeness level.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Contrast</span>The two parts point in opposite directions, or the result isn't what you'd expect.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語は難しいですが、おもしろいです。</span>(Japanese is hard, but it's fun.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日は雨でしたが、出かけました。</span>(It rained yesterday, but I went out anyway.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲みましたが、まだ頭が痛いです。</span>(I took medicine, but my head still hurts.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Polite opener</span>Before asking someone for help or asking a question, you can cushion it with 「すみませんが」 or 「失礼ですが」. Here が doesn't mean “but.” It just softens the tone, much like English “Excuse me, could I ask…”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>すみませんが、駅はどこですか。</span>(Excuse me, where is the station?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>失礼ですが、お名前は？</span>(Excuse me, may I ask your name?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・English “although” goes at the <b>very start</b> of the sentence, but Japanese が goes at the <b>end of the first half</b>. Don't look for a word at the start of the sentence to match “although.”<br>・You may see two が in one sentence: <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っていますが、行きます。</span> (Although it's raining, I'm going anyway.) The first が follows the noun 「雨」 and marks the subject. The second follows the complete clause 「降っています」, and that one means “but.” The test is simple: is the word before が <b>a single noun</b>, or <b>a clause that's already complete</b>?<br>・Once you use が, you don't need to add でも. <span class='gd-jp'>高いですが、おいしいです。</span> is enough.<br>・When chatting with friends, saying 「高いが、おいしい」 (が attached directly without です・ます) sounds stiff, like written text. Among friends, けど is more natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar words apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>There are three common ways to say “It's expensive, but it's delicious”:<br><b>が</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>高いですが、おいしいです。</span> This is fairly formal, good for customers, people older than you, or letters.<br><b>けど</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>高いけど、おいしい。</span> This is the spoken version you'll hear most in everyday chat. You can also add です: <span class='gd-jp'>高いですけど、おいしいです。</span><br><b>でも</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>高いです。でも、おいしいです。</span> The first sentence ends with a period, and でも starts the next one.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: attached to the end of a clause and you want to sound a bit formal, use が. Chatting with friends, use けど. If the previous sentence is already finished and you're starting a new one with “but,” use でも.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at 「けど」. It means the same as が but is more casual, and you'll use it almost every time you talk with friends.</div>","n5-36":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「けど」 is roughly English “…, but…” or “…, though.” It has two uses. One is <b>contrast</b>, as in “It's small, but it's easy to use.” The other is <b>setting the scene</b>: you bring up the topic first, like English “Excuse me, I wanted to ask…”. Japanese people use it constantly in everyday speech.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Contrast</span>You say one thing, then follow with something that contradicts it or is unexpected.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>小さいけど、使いやすいです。</span>(It's small, but it's easy to use.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は安いけど、おいしいです。</span>(This restaurant is cheap, but it's delicious.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Setting the scene</span>You give a little background first, then ask a question, make an invitation, or make a request. Here けど <b>doesn't mean “but”</b>. It's closer to “So, here's the thing…”. The second part is often left unsaid so the other person can respond.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日映画を見に行くけど、一緒に行かない？</span>(I'm going to see a movie tomorrow. Want to come along?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>すみません、駅に行きたいんですけど…</span>(Excuse me, I'd like to get to the station…)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もしもし、田中ですけど…</span>(Hello, this is Tanaka…)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>けど attaches to the <b>end of the previous clause</b>, and what comes before it must be a complete sentence.<br><b>With friends (no です・ます)</b>:<br>Verbs and い-adjectives take it directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くけど</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いけど</span></span><br>Nouns and な-adjectives need 「だ」 first: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生だけど</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>きれいだけど</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Polite style</b>: use です・ます before it, then add けど.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行きますけど</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高いです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いですけど</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生です</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生ですけど</span></span><br>Memorize 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜たいんですけど…</span>」 as a set phrase. It's useful for asking directions, making requests, and speaking up at a counter.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Forgetting だ with nouns and な-adjectives: <span class='gd-jp'>学生けど</span>× should be <span class='gd-jp'>学生だけど</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>静かけど</span>× should be <span class='gd-jp'>静かだけど</span>. Conversely, い-adjectives <b>don't</b> take だ: <span class='gd-jp'>高いだけど</span>× should simply be <span class='gd-jp'>高いけど</span>.<br>・English “but” can start the second sentence, but Japanese けど sticks to the <b>end of the first sentence</b>. To start the second sentence with “But…”, use <span class='gd-jp'>でも</span> (compared below).<br>・A sentence that stops at 「〜けど…」 isn't unfinished. It's a <b>polite, softer way</b> of not spelling everything out. When translating into English, don't force a “but” into it.<br>・けど is spoken language. It's natural in chat and when talking to shop staff, but for reports and formal situations 「が」 is safer.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>けれど</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>けれども</span> mean the same thing. The longer the form, the more formal it is.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar words apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: the clothes are expensive, but you bought them anyway.<br><b>けど</b> = everyday speech: <span class='gd-jp'>高いけど、買いました。</span><br><b>が</b> = more formal, for writing or formal situations: <span class='gd-jp'>高いですが、買いました。</span><br><b>でも</b> = finish the first sentence, then start the second with “but”: <span class='gd-jp'>高いです。でも、買いました。</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>The scene-setting use works the same way. With a boss or someone older, swap けど for が to be more polite: <span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと聞きたいんですが…</span> (I'd like to ask you something…)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: use けど in everyday speech; use が in formal speech and writing; use でも when you split into two sentences and start the second with “but.”</div>","n5-37":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>This 「に」 explains <b>why you go / come / return</b>. In English you say \"go to buy something\" or \"come to hang out.\" In Japanese, you put the thing you're going to do first, then add 「に行きます」 or 「に来ます」: <span class='gd-jp'>買いに行きます</span> (go to buy), <span class='gd-jp'>遊びに来ます</span> (come to hang out).</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verbs</span>First put the verb in its ます form, drop 「ます」, then add 「に」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買います</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>買いに行きます</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べに行きます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見に行きます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会います</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会いに行きます</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お土産を買いに行きます。</span>(I'm going to buy souvenirs.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末、友達に会いに行きます。</span>(I'm going to see a friend this weekend.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昼ごはんを食べに家へ帰ります。</span>(I'm going home to eat lunch.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>書類を届けに来ました。</span>(I came to deliver some documents.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Nouns</span>Nouns that already mean \"an action,\" such as <span class='gd-jp'>買い物</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>散歩</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>旅行</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>食事</span>, can take 「に」 directly.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>デパートへ買い物に行きます。</span>(I'm going shopping at the department store.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>公園へ散歩に行きませんか。</span>(Shall we go for a walk in the park?)</div><div class='gd-h'>When a place and a purpose are in the same sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>The usual order is: <b>place + へ　→　purpose + に　→　行きます</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>図書館へ本を借りに行きます。</span>(I'm going to the library to borrow a book.)<br>Using 「へ」 after the place keeps you from stacking two に in a row with the purpose 「に」, so it sounds clearer.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Before に, use the <b>ます form minus ます</b>, not the dictionary form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>買いに行きます</span>, not 「買うに行きます」.<br>・After it, you can only use a <b>verb of movement</b>, most commonly <span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>帰ります</span>. For sentences that have nothing to do with movement, like \"I read books in order to learn Japanese,\" you can't use this に.<br>・English says \"go + buy,\" but Japanese says 「買い＋に行く」, so <b>the order is reversed from English</b>. Don't say 「行きに買います」.<br>・The 「を」 that normally goes with the verb stays: <span class='gd-jp'>お土産を買いに行きます</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>遊びに来てください。</span> (Come hang out!) is a very common invitation in Japan, so it's worth memorizing as a whole sentence.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even with the same 「に行きます」, <b>what comes before に</b> decides the meaning:<br><b>Place + に</b> = go <b>where</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>デパートに行きます。</span> (I'm going to the department store.)<br><b>Action + に</b> = go to <b>do what</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>買い物に行きます。</span> (I'm going shopping.)<br>Put them together: <span class='gd-jp'>デパートへ買い物に行きます。</span> (I'm going shopping at the department store.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what comes before に is a place, it means where you're going. If it's a verb with ます dropped, or an action noun like 買い物 or 散歩, it means what you're going to do.</div>","n5-39":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「ね」 is like English <b>\"…, right?\" or \"…, isn't it?\"</b>: you're talking about something <b>the other person also knows or feels</b>, so you share the feeling or check with them.<br>「よ」 is like <b>\"…, you know!\" or \"…!\" as a heads-up</b>: you're talking about something <b>the other person doesn't know yet</b>, and you're telling or reminding them.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use them</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ね: sharing a feeling</span>You both see, taste or feel the same thing, and you say it to connect.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いい天気ですね。</span>(Nice weather, isn't it?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このケーキ、おいしいですね。</span>(This cake is delicious, isn't it?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ね: checking</span>You mostly know, but want to confirm you've got it right. Staff confirming your order use it the same way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は休みですね。</span>(Tomorrow's a day off, right?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>コーヒー二つですね。</span>(Two coffees, correct?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>よ: telling</span>Something you know and the other person doesn't, like a recommendation or a piece of news.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店、安いですよ。</span>(This shop is cheap, you know.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この番組、面白いですよ。</span>(This show is fun, you know.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>よ: reminding</span>Something the other person hasn't noticed, so you point it out.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もう始まりますよ。</span>(It's about to start!)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車が来ましたよ。</span>(The train's here!)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach them</div><div class='gd-sec'>Very simple: finish the sentence and <b>put it right at the end</b>.<br>Polite form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>おいしいです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>おいしいですね</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>始まります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>始まりますよ</span></span><br>Casual speech works the same way, just add it directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>いいね</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くよ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だね</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Don't add ね to things the other person can't possibly know</b>. If you introduce yourself with 「私は台湾人ですね」, it sounds like you're asking them, \"I'm Taiwanese, right?\", which is odd. Just say <span class='gd-jp'>私は台湾人です。</span>.<br>・<b>よ is stronger</b>. If you add よ when correcting a teacher or boss, as in 「それは違いますよ」, it can sound like you're lecturing them. With people above you, it's safer to use よ sparingly.<br>・Intonation of ね: <b>rising</b> checks (\"right?\"); <b>flat or falling</b> expresses a feeling (\"it really is…\").<br>・Learners often want to add a particle to every sentence, the way English speakers sprinkle in \"you know\" or \"right?\", but Japanese doesn't need it every time. When you're simply describing something, adding nothing is perfectly natural, and ね or よ on every sentence gets noisy.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take \"it's raining outside\". Choose by whether the other person knows:<br><b>ね</b> = they see it too, and you share the moment: <span class='gd-jp'>雨ですね。</span> (It's raining, isn't it?)<br><b>よ</b> = they're indoors and haven't noticed, so you tell them: <span class='gd-jp'>雨ですよ。</span> (It's raining out there!)<br><b>か</b> = you have no idea yourself and you're asking: <span class='gd-jp'>雨ですか。</span> (Is it raining?)</div><div class='gd-sec'>「ね」 and 「か」 can both make a question. The difference is whether you already have an answer in mind:<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日は休みですか。</span> (Is tomorrow a day off? You don't know and are asking.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日は休みですね。</span> (Tomorrow's a day off, right? You pretty much know and are confirming.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: the other person knows too → ね; they don't know and you're telling them → よ; you don't know yourself → か.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The two can be combined into 「よね」. The order is fixed, with よ first, so you can't say 「ねよ」. It means \"I remember it being this way, right?\": <span class='gd-jp'>テストは明日ですよね。</span> (The test is tomorrow, right?)</div>","n5-40":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Japanese particles work a lot like English prepositions such as \"in,\" \"on,\" and \"at\": <b>one word often has several meanings</b>. You've already learned the basic uses of を, に, で, は, and か. This lesson gathers their <b>other common uses</b> in one place. When you see a particle, don't memorize just one meaning. Look at the words around it to decide which one it is.</div><div class='gd-h'>を: leaving and passing through</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Place you leave</span>When you come out of a place, get off a vehicle, or graduate, the place takes を. It often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>出る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>降りる</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>卒業する</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>7時に家を出ます。</span>(I leave the house at 7.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>次の駅で電車を降ります。</span>(I get off the train at the next station.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Place you pass through</span>With movement verbs like walking, running, strolling, or crossing a bridge, the route or area you move through takes を.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎朝、公園を散歩します。</span>(I take a walk in the park every morning.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この橋を渡ってください。</span>(Please cross this bridge.)</div><div class='gd-h'>に: frequency, where something lands, becoming</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Frequency</span>Time period + に + number of times = how many times per period.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>1日に3回、薬を飲みます。</span>(I take medicine three times a day.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週に1回、プールへ行きます。</span>(I go to the pool once a week.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Where something lands</span>When you write on something, sit on something, or put something into something, the spot where the thing ends up \"touching or staying\" takes に.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここに名前を書いてください。</span>(Please write your name here.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>椅子に座ります。</span>(I sit on the chair.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Becoming</span>Noun + になる = become ~.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>将来、先生になりたいです。</span>(I want to become a teacher in the future.)</div><div class='gd-h'>で: range and total</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Range</span>For \"the most ~ out of/in ~,\" the range takes で.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>兄はクラスでいちばん背が高いです。</span>(My older brother is the tallest in the class.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Total</span>Quantity + で = added up, in total. Very common when shopping.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>全部でいくらですか。</span>(How much is it in total?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>りんごは3つで500円です。</span>(The apples are 500 yen for three.)</div><div class='gd-h'>は: contrast</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Contrast</span>When you set two things side by side and give each its own は, it means \"A is like this, but B isn't.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寿司は好きですが、刺身は苦手です。</span>(I like sushi, but sashimi isn't really my thing.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even when you mention only one side, it can carry a hint of \"other things are a different story\":</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お酒は飲みません。</span>(Alcohol, I don't drink.)</div><div class='gd-h'>か: choosing one of two</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Choice</span>AかB = A or B, pick one of them.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>月曜日か火曜日に来てください。</span>(Please come on Monday or Tuesday.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Use に for getting on and を for getting off: <span class='gd-jp'>電車に乗ります</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>電車を降ります</span>. Many learners use the same particle for both.<br>・The \"passing through\" を needs a movement verb. For things you do while staying in one place, like playing in a park or eating a boxed lunch, use で: <span class='gd-jp'>公園で遊びます</span>.<br>・It's tempting to think of \"write here\" as doing something at a place and reach for で. But when you write on paper or sit on a chair, the thing \"lands\" there, so use に: <span class='gd-jp'>ここに書いてください</span>.<br>・The contrast は is still pronounced wa, not ha.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>か, と, and や can all link two things, but the meanings are quite different. Take buying drinks:<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーと紅茶を買いました。</span>(I bought coffee and tea: both.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーや紅茶を買いました。</span>(I bought things like coffee and tea: there was more.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーか紅茶を買います。</span>(I'll buy coffee or tea: just one.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: use と for everything on the list, や for a few examples, and か when you pick only one.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The range で lets you ask \"which is the most ~?\": <span class='gd-jp'>果物の中で何がいちばん好きですか。</span>(What do you like best among fruits?)</div>","n5-41":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The 「ます form」 is a verb wearing its <b>polite coat</b>. The dictionary lists <span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span>, but to a teacher, a shop clerk, or someone you've just met, you say <span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span>. This lesson drills one thing: <b>take any verb and turn it into its 〜ます form</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to convert: first check the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Step 1</span><b>Pick out Group 3 (する・来る) first.</b> There are only two, so just memorize them:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>します</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span>(きます)</span><br>「Noun + する」 verbs belong here too: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強します</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Step 2</span><b>For the rest, decide whether it's Group 1 or Group 2:</b><br>・If the last kana is <b>not る</b> (like <span class='gd-jp'>書く</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>会う</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span>): it's always Group 1.<br>・If it ends in る and the <b>sound before る ends in a, u, or o</b> (like か in <span class='gd-jp'>分かる</span>, く in <span class='gd-jp'>作る</span>, の in <span class='gd-jp'>乗る</span>): it's always Group 1. (する and 来る were already removed in Step 1, so they don't count here.)<br>・If it ends in る and the <b>sound before る ends in i or e</b> (like み in <span class='gd-jp'>見る</span>, べ in <span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span>, き in <span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span>): it's mostly Group 2. A few exceptions are actually Group 1, so memorize them: <span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>入る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>走る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>知る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>切る</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the 「い」-row sound of the same consonant row, then add ます.<br>On the gojūon chart, move from the う-column one step over to the い-column (one step up in a vertical chart, one step left in a horizontal chart): う→い, く→き, ぐ→ぎ, す→し, つ→ち, ぬ→に, ぶ→び, む→み, る→り.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → change く to き → <span class='gd-jp'>書きます</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会います</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待ちます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話します</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泳ぎます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遊ぶ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遊びます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰ります</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add ます.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見ます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>着る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>着ます</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'>Daily habits and things you'll do tomorrow both use the same ます form.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎朝パンを食べます。</span>(I eat bread every morning.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎日、日本語を勉強します。</span>(I study Japanese every day.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、友達が家に来ます。</span>(A friend is coming to my house tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You can't just stick ます onto the dictionary form: <span class='gd-jp'>飲むます</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>行くます</span> are both wrong. Change the last sound first.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>入る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>走る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>知る</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>切る</span> look like Group 2 but are really Group 1: it's <span class='gd-jp'>帰ります</span>, not 帰ます.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span> is read <b>きます</b>, not くます, and not 来ります. Likewise, <span class='gd-jp'>する</span> does not become すります.<br>・The 「す」 in 「ます」 is often barely voiced, so it sounds like \"mas.\" That's normal pronunciation, not your ears playing tricks.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The part left after you remove ます (<span class='gd-jp'>飲み</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>食べ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>し</span>) comes up constantly. Swap in a different ending and you get a new pattern:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みました</span></span>(drank)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みましょう</span></span>(let's drink)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みたいです</span></span>(I want to drink)</div>","n5-42":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The te-form is the verb's <b>\"connector\"</b>, like the stud on a LEGO brick that lets you snap something else on. In English you'd say \"get up <b>and</b> wash your face.\" Japanese turns 「起きる」 into its te-form and attaches the next action after it. Many important patterns, such as \"please do ~\" and \"be doing ~\", only work once the verb is in te-form. That's why it's called <b>the most important conjugation</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Chaining actions</span>Link several actions together in order.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>朝起きて、顔を洗います。</span>（I get up in the morning, then wash my face）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Attaching to patterns</span>Add other words after it to make a new meaning.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここに名前を書いてください。</span>（Please write your name here）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今、ご飯を食べています。</span>（I'm eating right now）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Casual requests</span>With friends, the te-form alone is a casual request: \"do ~ for me.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと待って。</span>（Wait a moment）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first, which group is the verb?</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: the easiest. Drop 「る」 and add 「て」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見て</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きて</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>して</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来て</span>（きて）</span><br>\"Noun + する\" verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> work the same way: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強して</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: look at the <b>last kana</b>. There are five groups.<br><b>う・つ・る → って</b><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買って</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待って</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰って</span></span><br><b>む・ぶ・ぬ → んで</b> (note the で with the two small dots)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んで</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遊ぶ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遊んで</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>死ぬ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>死んで</span></span><br><b>く → いて</b><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いて</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>聞く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>聞いて</span></span><br><b>ぐ → いで</b><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>急ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>急いで</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泳いで</span></span><br><b>す → して</b><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話して</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>貸す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>貸して</span></span><br><b>The one exception</b>: by the rule, <span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> should become 「行いて」, but it's actually <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行って</span></span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'>A little chant, just repeat it until it sticks: <b>うつる って、むぶぬ んで、く いて、ぐ いで、す して、行く は 行って</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>入る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>走る</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>知る</span> look like Group 2 but are actually Group 1. It's <span class='gd-jp'>帰って</span>, not 帰て.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>行って</span> is an exception, but other verbs ending in く, like <span class='gd-jp'>書いて</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>聞いて</span>, still follow the rule. Don't change them all to って.<br>・ぐ becomes <b>いで</b> and む・ぶ・ぬ become <b>んで</b>. People often write 泳いて or 飲んて, so don't forget the dots (dakuten).<br>・The small 「っ」 holds for one beat: <span class='gd-jp'>来て</span> (きて, come) and <span class='gd-jp'>切って</span> (きって, cut) differ only by that beat, but mean completely different things.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来て</span> is read <b>きて</b>, not こて or くて.<br>・The te-form itself has no past or present tense. The <b>last verb</b> decides: <span class='gd-jp'>シャワーを浴びて、寝ました。</span>（I took a shower, then went to bed）</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Once the te-form feels natural, the <b>た-form (the plain past tense) comes almost free</b>. The rules are exactly the same, you just swap て for た and で for だ.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書いて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだ</span></span><br>After that, you can learn 「～てください」 (please ~), 「～ています」 (be ~ing) and 「～てから」 (after ~). They all rely on the conjugation on this card.</div>","n5-43":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The dictionary form is the <b>casual version</b> of 「〜ます」, and it's also the form you find in a dictionary. The ない-form is the <b>casual version</b> of 「〜ません」. With a teacher you say <span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>行きません</span>, and with friends you say <span class='gd-jp'>行く</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span>. The meaning is the same, just more relaxed.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Chatting with friends and family</span>Just like ます, it covers present habits and things that haven't happened yet. To ask a question, just raise your intonation at the end.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、何時に起きる？</span>（What time are you getting up tomorrow?）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>7時に起きる。</span>（I get up at 7）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は学校に行かない。</span>（I'm not going to school today）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私はお酒を飲まない。</span>（I don't drink alcohol）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first, which group is the verb?</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>First, ます-form → dictionary form</b><br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the sound before ます to the same row's 「う」-column sound.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span> → み becomes む → <span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書く</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>歌います</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>歌う</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰ります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span></span><br><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: replace ます with る.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見る</span></span><br><b>Group 3 (only two, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>する</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来る</span>（くる）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Then, dictionary form → ない-form</b><br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the same row's 「あ」-column sound, then add ない.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → く becomes か → <span class='gd-jp'>書かない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲まない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待たない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰らない</span></span><br>If the verb ends in 「う」, <b>change it to 「わ」</b>, not 「あ」:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → う becomes わ → <span class='gd-jp'>買わない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>歌う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>歌わない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会わない</span></span><br><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop る and add ない.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見ない</span></span><br><b>Group 3 (just memorize)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span>（こない）</span><br>\"Noun + する\" verbs work the same way: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>電話する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>電話しない</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Verbs ending in 「う」 can't become 「あない」. It's <span class='gd-jp'>買わない</span>, not 買あない.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>入る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>走る</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>知る</span> look like Group 2 but are actually Group 1. It's <span class='gd-jp'>帰らない</span>, not 帰ない.<br>・Keep the three readings of 来る apart: <span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> is read くる, <span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span> is read きます, and <span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span> is read こない.<br>・The negative of <span class='gd-jp'>ある</span> (to exist, to be) is <span class='gd-jp'>ない</span>, not あらない: <span class='gd-jp'>お金がない。</span>（I have no money）<br>・The ない-form means \"don't (do)\", not \"can't\". <span class='gd-jp'>食べない</span> means \"I don't eat,\" not \"I can't eat.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same sentence, \"I'm not coming tomorrow.\" Which form you use depends on who you're talking to:<br>To a teacher or boss: <span class='gd-jp'>明日は来ません。</span><br>To a friend or family: <span class='gd-jp'>明日は来ない。</span><br>The meaning is exactly the same, only the politeness differs. Using <span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span> with a senior can sound disrespectful, while sticking to <span class='gd-jp'>来ません</span> with a close friend can sound distant.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: <span class='gd-jp'>ます</span> pairs with the dictionary form, and <span class='gd-jp'>ません</span> pairs with the ない-form. Think about who you're talking to first, then pick the set.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Many more grammar patterns attach to the dictionary form and ない-form, for example asking someone not to do something: <span class='gd-jp'>ここで写真を撮らないでください。</span>（Please don't take photos here）. Next, you can learn the past forms: <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>行かなかった</span>.</div>","n5-46":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English, an adjective just sits in front of the noun: \"tall mountain\", \"pretty flower\". Japanese splits into two patterns: <b>い-adjectives attach directly before the noun</b>, while <b>な-adjectives need a 「な」 in between</b>. Think of that 「な」 as a small connector that only な-adjectives need.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b> (the ones ending in い): change nothing, just put them before the noun.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span>＋<span class='gd-jp'>山</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高い山</span></span> (a tall mountain)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>新しい</span>＋<span class='gd-jp'>靴</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>新しい靴</span></span> (new shoes)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives</b>: add 「な」 first, then the noun.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span>＋な＋<span class='gd-jp'>花</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>きれいな花</span></span> (a pretty flower)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span>＋な＋<span class='gd-jp'>部屋</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かな部屋</span></span> (a quiet room)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most often</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>新しい靴を買いました。</span> (I bought new shoes.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これはおいしいケーキですね。</span> (This is a tasty cake, isn't it?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>静かな部屋がいいです。</span> (I'd like a quiet room.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>好きな食べ物は何ですか。</span> (What's your favorite food?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>きらい</span> end in い, but they are actually <b>な-adjectives</b>. Say <span class='gd-jp'>きれいな花</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>きらいな食べ物</span>, not きれい花.<br>・Some learners are tempted to squeeze a 「の」 in between: 「高いの山」 and 「静かの部屋」 are both wrong. い-adjectives take nothing extra; な-adjectives take 「な」.<br>・\"Many people\" can't be said as 「多い人」. Say <span class='gd-jp'>たくさんの人</span>.<br>・You may also see <span class='gd-jp'>大きな家</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>小さな店</span>. These aren't typos: <span class='gd-jp'>大きい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>小さい</span> have an extra form that can only go before a noun. <span class='gd-jp'>大きい家</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>大きな家</span> are both correct.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>End of sentence vs before a noun</span>The 「な」 of a な-adjective <b>only appears when a noun follows</b>. At the end of a sentence, it goes straight into です.<br><span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は静かです。</span> (This room is quiet.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>静かな部屋です。</span> (It's a quiet room.)<br>You can't say 「この部屋は静かなです」.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun + の</span>If what comes before is a <b>noun</b>, the connector is 「の」, not 「な」.<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本の車</span> (a Japanese car: noun + の)<br><span class='gd-jp'>新しい車</span> (a new car: い-adjective, attached directly)<br><span class='gd-jp'>きれいな車</span> (a pretty car: な-adjective + な)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: ask yourself what the word in front is. い-adjective: attach directly. な-adjective: add な. Noun: add の.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Adjectives can also modify verbs, as in <span class='gd-jp'>早く起きます</span> (get up early). This is the adverb use of adjectives.</div>","n5-47":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English, you describe <i>how</i> an action is done with an adverb: \"get up early\", \"clean thoroughly\", \"dance well\". Japanese does this too: you just <b>change the ending</b> of the adjective, and it can go in front of the verb to say how the action is done.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to change it: first check which type of adjective it is</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives (adjectives ending in 「い」)</b>: replace the final 「い」 with 「く」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>早い</span> → change い to く → <span class='gd-jp'>早く</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大きい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大きく</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>楽しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>楽しく</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は早く起きます。</span> (I'll get up early tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>名前を大きく書いてください。</span> (Please write your name big.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives (adjectives that take 「な」 before a noun)</b>: just add 「に」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → add に → <span class='gd-jp'>静かに</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>きれいに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>上手</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>上手に</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部屋をきれいに掃除しました。</span> (I cleaned the room until it was spotless.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>図書館では静かに話してください。</span> (Please speak quietly in the library.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>An exception to memorize</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よく</span></span>, not いく. Words ending in いい work the same way, for example <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>かっこいい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>かっこよく</span></span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日はよく寝ました。</span> (I slept well yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>かっこよく踊りたいです。</span> (I want to dance in a cool way.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Word order: say the \"how\" first</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English the adverb often comes <b>after</b> the verb: \"dances well\", \"writes beautifully\". Japanese puts it the other way around: <b>say how first, then the action</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは上手に踊ります。</span> (Mr. Tanaka dances well.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>字をきれいに書きます。</span> (I write characters beautifully.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>有名</span> look like they end in い, but they are actually な-adjectives: say <span class='gd-jp'>きれいに</span>, not きれく.<br>・You can't put the plain form of an adjective directly before a verb: not 早い起きます but <span class='gd-jp'>早く起きます</span>; not 静かな話します but <span class='gd-jp'>静かに話します</span>.<br>・Besides \"well, properly\", <span class='gd-jp'>よく</span> is also very often used to mean \"often\": <span class='gd-jp'>よく行きます</span> means \"I often go.\" Use the context to tell which one it is.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>早く</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>速く</span> are both read はやく, but 「早く」 means early in time and 「速く」 means fast in speed: <span class='gd-jp'>早く起きます</span> (get up early), <span class='gd-jp'>速く走ります</span> (run fast).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's easy to mix this lesson up with the previous one, <b>adjectives modifying nouns</b>. The only difference is <b>what comes after</b>:<br>Before a noun (a person or thing): い-adjectives stay the same, な-adjectives add 「な」.<br>Before a verb (an action): い-adjectives change to 「く」, な-adjectives add 「に」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>静かな人です。</span> (He/She is a quiet person.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>静かに話します。</span> (I speak quietly.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>早い電車に乗ります。</span> (I'll take an earlier train.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>早く来てください。</span> (Please come early.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: before a \"thing\", use 「〜い／〜な」. Before an \"action\", use 「〜く／〜に」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The same く／に followed by <span class='gd-jp'>なります</span> means \"become ~\", and followed by <span class='gd-jp'>します</span> means \"make it ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>寒くなりました。</span> (It got cold.), <span class='gd-jp'>静かにしてください。</span> (Please be quiet.)</div>","n5-44":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The た-form is the <b>casual version</b> of 「〜ました」, and the なかった-form is the <b>casual version</b> of 「〜ませんでした」. To your teacher you say 「<span class='gd-jp'>昨日、映画を見ました</span>」, and to a friend 「<span class='gd-jp'>昨日、映画を見た</span>」. Both mean “I watched a movie yesterday”; the second is just more relaxed.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Talking about the past with people you know</span>Friends and family, when you talk about something that already happened.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日、渋谷で友達に会った。</span>(I met a friend in Shibuya yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今朝は何も食べなかった。</span>(I didn't eat anything this morning.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Talking to yourself, writing a diary</span>When there's no one you need to be polite to.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あ、傘を忘れた。</span>(Oh, I forgot my umbrella.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Connecting to other grammar</span>Many later grammar points need the verb in た-form first, so you really do need this one.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to make the た-form: first, check which type the verb is</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>If you already know the て-form, this is the easy way</b>: just change the 「て」 of the て-form to 「た」 and 「で」 to 「だ」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書いて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: look at the last sound and change it like this.<br>Ending in う・つ・る → った：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会った</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待った</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰った</span></span><br>Ending in ぬ・む・ぶ → んだ：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>死ぬ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>死んだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遊ぶ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遊んだ</span></span><br>Ending in く → いた：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いた</span></span><br>Ending in ぐ → いだ：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泳いだ</span></span><br>Ending in す → した：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話した</span></span><br>The one exception: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span></span> (it ends in く, but it is not 行いた)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add 「た」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見た</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きた</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>した</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来た</span>（きた）</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to make the なかった-form</div><div class='gd-sec'>First make the ない-form, then change 「ない」 to 「なかった」. The same trick works for all three groups.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲まない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲まなかった</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会わない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会わなかった</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べなかった</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しなかった</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span>（こない） → <span class='gd-jp'>来なかった</span>（こなかった）</span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日はどこにも行かなかった。</span>(I didn't go anywhere yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The た-form of <span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span>, not 行いた.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>走る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>入る</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>知る</span> look like Group 2 but are actually Group 1: they become <span class='gd-jp'>帰った</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>走った</span>, not 帰た or 走た.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来た</span> is read <b>きた</b>, but <span class='gd-jp'>来なかった</span> is read <b>こなかった</b>. The same kanji is read differently.<br>・The negative of <span class='gd-jp'>ある</span> is simply <span class='gd-jp'>ない</span> (there is no 「あらない」), so the past is <span class='gd-jp'>なかった</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>時間がなかった。</span>(I didn't have time.)<br>・English “I haven't eaten yet” is not <span class='gd-jp'>食べなかった</span>. なかった means “didn't do it back then”, about something that's over. For “still haven't done it as of now”, say <span class='gd-jp'>まだ食べていない</span>.<br>・The た-form and なかった-form are for talking with friends. When you talk about the past to someone older or senior, always end the sentence with 「〜ました」 or 「〜ませんでした」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>た-form vs ました</b>: exactly the same meaning; the difference is who you're talking to.<br><span class='gd-jp'>昨日、映画を見ました。</span>(To your teacher: I watched a movie yesterday.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>昨日、映画を見た。</span>(To a friend: I watched a movie yesterday.)<br>The negative works the same way: <span class='gd-jp'>行きませんでした</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>行かなかった</span> both mean “didn't go”. The first is polite, the second more casual.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>た vs て</b>: only one sound apart, but completely different jobs. 「た」 ends the sentence and says it was done. 「て」 means the sentence isn't finished and something follows, or it's used for making requests.<br><span class='gd-jp'>ご飯を食べた。</span>(I ate.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>ご飯を食べて、すぐ寝た。</span>(I ate and went straight to bed.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: the sentence ends here and it already happened → た; more is coming after → て; talking to someone older or a customer → go back to ました or ませんでした.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add 「り」 after the た-form to get 「〜たり〜たりします」 (do things like this and that): <span class='gd-jp'>週末は映画を見たり、買い物をしたりします。</span>(On weekends I do things like watch movies and go shopping.)<br>た-form + 「あとで」 = after doing ~: <span class='gd-jp'>ご飯を食べたあとで、散歩します。</span>(After eating, I'll go for a walk.)</div>","n5-45":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The plain form is the <b>casual way of speaking</b>, made by dropping 「です・ます」, and it's also the basic form that many sentence patterns attach to. Every word has four shapes, just like English “go / don't go / went / didn't go”: <b>present affirmative, present negative, past affirmative, past negative</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Talking with people you know</span>Among family and friends you don't use ます; you just use the plain form.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日、映画を見た。</span>(I watched a movie yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Connecting to patterns</span>Even in a very polite sentence, you often need to switch to the plain form in the middle before attaching the next pattern.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨が降ると思います。</span>(I think it will rain tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first, check which type the verb is</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b><br>Negative: change the last sound to the あ-row sound of the same row, then add ない. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → change く to か → <span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span></span><br>Past: formed the same way as the て-form, but with た instead of て. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだ</span></span><br>Past negative: change the 「い」 of ない to 「かった」. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かなかった</span></span><br>The whole set at a glance: <span class='gd-jp'>買う／買わない／買った／買わなかった</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: the easiest. Drop 「る」 and add ない, た, or なかった.<br><span class='gd-jp'>食べる／食べない／食べた／食べなかった</span><br><span class='gd-jp'>見る／見ない／見た／見なかった</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>する／しない／した／しなかった</span><br><span class='gd-jp'>来る／来ない／来た／来なかった</span>（くる／こない／きた／こなかった）<br>“Noun + する” verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> change just like する: <span class='gd-jp'>勉強しない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>勉強した</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>勉強しなかった</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How adjectives and nouns change</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: for the negative, change the final い to くない; for the past, change い to かった.<br><span class='gd-jp'>高い／高くない／高かった／高くなかった</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives</b>: add だ, じゃない, だった, or じゃなかった.<br><span class='gd-jp'>静かだ／静かじゃない／静かだった／静かじゃなかった</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: they change exactly like な-adjectives.<br><span class='gd-jp'>学生だ／学生じゃない／学生だった／学生じゃなかった</span></div><div class='gd-warn'>A handy pattern: whether it's a verb, adjective or noun, the negative always ends in 「ない」, and it always becomes past the same way: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>なかった</span></span>. Remember this and you barely need to memorize the fourth form (past negative) separately.</div><div class='gd-h'>Compare with ます・です</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行く</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きません</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きました</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きませんでした</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かなかった</span></span><br>い-adjectives are the easiest: just drop です. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高かったです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高かった</span></span><br>For な-adjectives and nouns, replace です with だ. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生です</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生だ</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・For Group 1 verbs ending in う, the negative uses 「わ」: <span class='gd-jp'>買わない</span>, not 買あない.<br>・The negative of <span class='gd-jp'>ある</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>ない</span>, not あらない.<br>・The past of <span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span>, not 行いた.<br>・As soon as <span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> conjugates, it switches to a よ- stem: <span class='gd-jp'>よくない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>よかった</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>よくなかった</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>きれい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>有名</span> look like い-adjectives but are actually な-adjectives: say <span class='gd-jp'>きれいじゃない</span>, not きれくない.<br>・You can't add だ after an い-adjective: just say <span class='gd-jp'>高い</span>, not 高いだ.<br>・Ending a noun or な-adjective with 「だ」 sounds a bit blunt. In chat with friends, people often drop だ or add よ, for example <span class='gd-jp'>学生だよ</span>.<br>・When you ask a friend a question in the plain form, you usually don't add か; just raise your intonation at the end: <span class='gd-jp'>明日、来る？</span>(Are you coming tomorrow?)</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>When the plain form attaches to a pattern, pay special attention to the 「だ」 of nouns and な-adjectives: sometimes it stays, sometimes it's dropped, and sometimes it becomes な.<br><span class='gd-jp'>静かだと思う。</span>(I think it's quiet: だ stays)<br><span class='gd-jp'>静かでしょう。</span>(It's probably quiet: だ is dropped)<br><span class='gd-jp'>学生なの？</span>(You're a student?: だ becomes な)<br>We'll go through these one by one in the lessons on 「～でしょう」 and the nominalizer 「の」.</div>","n5-50":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>English says \"there is\" for everything: there's a book on the desk, there's a cat in the room. Japanese adds one step: use <span class='gd-jp'>あります</span> for <b>things and plants</b>, and <span class='gd-jp'>います</span> for <b>people and animals</b>. Ask yourself whether it's alive and can move on its own, and you'll know which one to use.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Place + <span class='gd-jp'>に</span> + thing or person + <span class='gd-jp'>が</span> + <span class='gd-jp'>あります</span> / <span class='gd-jp'>います</span><br><span class='gd-jp'>に</span> marks the location, like \"in\" or \"at\"; <span class='gd-jp'>が</span> marks what is there.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>引き出しにハサミがあります。</span>(There are scissors in the drawer.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>教室に生徒がいます。</span>(There are students in the classroom.)</div><div class='gd-h'>あります or います?</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>あります</span> Things, plants, buildings. A tree is alive, but it doesn't move around, so it takes <span class='gd-jp'>あります</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>机の上に本があります。</span>(There's a book on the desk.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>庭に大きい木があります。</span>(There's a big tree in the yard.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>います</span> People and animals. Fish and insects count too.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部屋に猫がいます。</span>(There's a cat in the room.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>川に魚がいます。</span>(There are fish in the river.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Patterns you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Where something is</span> To say where something is, put it first with <span class='gd-jp'>は</span>. The location still takes <span class='gd-jp'>に</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ハサミは引き出しにあります。</span>(The scissors are in the drawer.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>トイレはどこにありますか。</span>(Where is the restroom?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Not there</span> Change ます to ません: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>あります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ありません</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>います</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>いません</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>教室に誰もいません。</span>(There's no one in the classroom.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>I have ~</span> For \"having\" family or time, Japanese also uses these same two words.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>姉が一人います。</span>(I have one older sister.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、時間がありますか。</span>(Are you free tomorrow?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・People always take <span class='gd-jp'>います</span>: \"There's a teacher\" is <span class='gd-jp'>先生がいます</span>; saying 「先生があります」 is wrong. Family works the same way: <span class='gd-jp'>妹がいます</span>.<br>・Plants take <span class='gd-jp'>あります</span>; fish and insects, however tiny, take <span class='gd-jp'>います</span> because they're alive.<br>・For things, people and animals \"being somewhere\", the location takes <span class='gd-jp'>に</span>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>部屋に猫がいます</span>, not 部屋で猫がいます.<br>・<b>Exception</b>: when what \"exists\" is an activity or event (<span class='gd-jp'>テスト</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>パーティー</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>会議</span>…), it means \"is held there\", and the location takes <span class='gd-jp'>で</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>あした学校でテストがあります。</span>(There's a test at school tomorrow.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>何も</span> (\"nothing\") and <span class='gd-jp'>誰も</span> (\"no one\") must always be followed by a negative: <span class='gd-jp'>ありません</span> / <span class='gd-jp'>いません</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same park, but look at what comes after:<br><b>Existing</b> takes <span class='gd-jp'>に</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>公園に犬がいます。</span>(There's a dog in the park.)<br><b>Doing an action</b> takes <span class='gd-jp'>で</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>公園で犬と遊びます。</span>(I play with the dog in the park.)<br><b>An event being held</b> takes <span class='gd-jp'>で</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>公園でお祭りがあります。</span>(There's a festival at the park.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if it ends in <span class='gd-jp'>あります</span> / <span class='gd-jp'>います</span> and a thing, person or animal simply exists there, use <span class='gd-jp'>に</span>. If it ends in an action verb, or what \"exists\" is an activity or event, use <span class='gd-jp'>で</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add a count: <span class='gd-jp'>猫が二匹います。</span>(There are two cats.)<br>Also, the <span class='gd-jp'>います</span> inside <span class='gd-jp'>～ています</span> (\"is ~ing\") is the very same word you learned today.</div>","n5-52":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ています」 covers roughly <b>\"is ~ing,\" \"is in the state of ~,\" \"has already ~ed (and still is that way),\" and \"has been ~ing lately.\"</b> The key point is that it doesn't describe a single moment but <b>an ongoing state</b>: an action in progress, a result that remains, or something you've been doing these days.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to its て-form, then add <b>います</b>. If you need a refresher on forming the て-form, go back to the \"て-form\" lesson.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べています</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んでいます</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>して</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しています</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来て</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ています</span>（きています）</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Three meanings</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In progress</span> The action is happening right now, often together with \"今\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今、洗濯しています。</span>(I'm doing laundry right now.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>弟はテレビを見ています。</span>(My younger brother is watching TV.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Result remains</span> Some actions are <b>over in an instant</b>, such as getting married, a door or window opening (<span class='gd-jp'>開く</span>), or moving to live somewhere. With these verbs, ています doesn't mean \"in the middle of\"; it means \"it happened, and it's still in that state.\" To describe how something is now, use an intransitive verb like <span class='gd-jp'>開く</span>: say <span class='gd-jp'>窓が開いています</span>, not <span class='gd-jp'>窓を開けています</span> (which sounds more like \"someone is opening the window\").</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>窓が開いています。</span>(The window is open.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>京都に住んでいます。</span>(I live in Kyoto.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>姉は結婚しています。</span>(My older sister is married.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Lately / these days</span> Something you've kept doing recently or for the current period, like a job, your current situation, or a recently formed habit.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎朝走っています。</span>(I've been running every morning.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父は銀行で働いています。</span>(My father works at a bank.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>結婚しています</span> means \"is married,\" not \"is having a wedding right now.\" Likewise, <span class='gd-jp'>住んでいます</span> means \"live in\"; use it whenever you say where you live now. <span class='gd-jp'>京都に住みます</span> would instead mean \"I'm going to live in Kyoto (in the future).\"<br>・\"I know\" is <span class='gd-jp'>知っています</span>, but \"I don't know\" is <span class='gd-jp'>知りません</span>, not 知っていません.<br>・If someone asks <span class='gd-jp'>もう食べましたか</span>, the \"not yet\" answer is <span class='gd-jp'>まだ食べていません</span>, not まだ食べませんでした.<br>・The 来 in <span class='gd-jp'>来ています</span> is read <b>き</b>.<br>・Among friends, the い is often dropped: <span class='gd-jp'>食べています</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>食べてる</span>. Don't be thrown when you hear it.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ます</b> and <b>ています</b> are the pair people confuse most. Compare the same action:<br><span class='gd-jp'>本を読みます。</span>(I read books: a regular habit, or I'm about to read.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>本を読んでいます。</span>(I'm reading a book.)<br><br><span class='gd-jp'>来月結婚します。</span>(I'm getting married next month.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>もう結婚しています。</span>(I'm already married.)<br><br>Both can express habits, but the feel is different:<br><span class='gd-jp'>毎日コーヒーを飲みます。</span>(I drink coffee every day: a general habit or fact.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>最近、毎朝走っています。</span>(I've been running every morning lately: something ongoing these days.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: for something happening right now, a state that remains (\"already ~ed\" / \"is in the state of ~\"), or \"I've been doing this lately,\" use ています. For a general habit or something you're about to do, use ます.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>When you study intransitive and transitive verbs, you'll often see forms like <span class='gd-jp'>ドアが閉まっています</span> (the door is closed), the \"result remains\" use. It will make more sense when you compare it with what you learned here.</div>","n5-48":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English we say “the book <b>I borrowed last week</b>” or “the school <b>my younger brother goes to</b>”. The describing clause comes <b>after</b> the noun, joined by “that” or “which”. Japanese puts the whole clause <b>before the noun</b>, and there is <b>no “that” or “which” to add</b>. Just put the clause straight in front of the noun.<br><span class='gd-jp'>先週借りた本</span> (the book I borrowed last week)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The clause that describes the noun uses the <b>plain form</b>, the way you'd talk with friends, without ます or です. Then you attach it directly to the noun.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>本を借りました</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>借りた</span></span> → <span class='gd-jp'>先週借りた本</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verbs</span>The form you pick changes the meaning:<br><span class='gd-jp'>見る映画</span> (a movie I'm going to watch)<br><span class='gd-jp'>見ない映画</span> (a movie I don't watch)<br><span class='gd-jp'>見た映画</span> (a movie I watched)<br><span class='gd-jp'>見なかった映画</span> (a movie I didn't watch)<br><span class='gd-jp'>見ている映画</span> (a movie I'm watching)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>い-adjective endings</span>Attach directly.<br><span class='gd-jp'>髪が長い人</span> (a person with long hair)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>な-adjective endings</span>In the present affirmative, change だ to <b>な</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>交通が便利だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>交通が便利な町</span></span> (a town with convenient transport)<br>It is not 「便利だ町」.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun endings</span>In the present affirmative, change だ to <b>の</b>, not な.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>母が日本人だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>母が日本人の友達</span></span> (a friend whose mother is Japanese)<br>It is not 「日本人だ友達」, and it is not 「日本人な友達」 either.</div><div class='gd-sec'>In the past or negative, nothing changes. Just attach directly:<br><span class='gd-jp'>静かじゃない部屋</span> (a room that isn't quiet)<br><span class='gd-jp'>昔、先生だった人</span> (a person who used to be a teacher)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'>The whole “noun + clause” chunk works as one big noun inside a sentence. To be polite, <b>only the end of the sentence needs です・ます</b>. The clause before the noun stays in plain form.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは母が作ったケーキです。</span> (This is a cake my mother made.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日見た映画はおもしろかったです。</span> (The movie I watched yesterday was interesting.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本で行きたい所はどこですか。</span> (Where is the place you want to go in Japan?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅の近くにある店で会いましょう。</span> (Let's meet at the shop near the station.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Where people slip up</div><div class='gd-warn'>・English needs “that” or “of” in the middle, so it's tempting to add の. 「借りたの本」 is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>借りた本</span>.<br>・Don't use ます inside the clause. 「買いました本」 is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>買った本</span>. Leave the politeness to the です・ます at the end of the sentence.<br>・The person doing the action inside the clause takes <b>が</b>, not は. 「私は作った料理」 is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>私が作った料理</span> (the food I made). You may also see <span class='gd-jp'>私の作った料理</span>, where が is swapped for の. The meaning is the same.<br>・な-adjectives change to <b>な</b> (<span class='gd-jp'>静かな部屋</span>) and nouns change to <b>の</b> (<span class='gd-jp'>医者の父</span>, a father who is a doctor). Don't mix them up.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>見た映画</span> means “a movie I watched”, while <span class='gd-jp'>見る映画</span> means “a movie I'm going to watch or usually watch”. Pick the right tense.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even when you're talking about the same book, the word before it decides whether you need の:<br><span class='gd-jp'>私の本</span> (my book): a noun comes before, so add の<br><span class='gd-jp'>私が買った本</span> (the book I bought): a verb comes before, so attach directly<br><span class='gd-jp'>新しい本</span> (a new book): an い-adjective comes before, so attach directly</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: look at the word right in front of the noun. If it's a noun, add の. If it's a verb or an い-adjective, attach directly. Don't worry about the “that” or “of” you'd say in English.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜とき」 (when ~) uses exactly the same connections: <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行くとき</span> (when I go to Japan), <span class='gd-jp'>子どものとき</span> (when I was a child).</div>","n5-49":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「の」 can <b>wrap an action up into “a thing”</b>, much like the English “-ing” in “<b>taking</b> photos”. English lets you say “I like swimming”, because “swimming” already works as a noun. In Japanese, a bare verb can't do that. You first add 「の」 after the verb, which turns it into “swimming / the act of swimming”. Only then can <span class='gd-jp'>は</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>が</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>を</span> follow.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Use the verb in its <b>plain form</b> (the dictionary form, like <span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>撮る</span>, not the ます-stem <span class='gd-jp'>泳ぎ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>撮り</span>), then add 「の」 directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐの</span></span> (swimming)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>写真を撮る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>写真を撮るの</span></span> (taking photos)<br>To say “not doing something”, use the ない form plus の: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べないの</span></span> (not eating)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>のが好き・上手</span>Talk about whether you like or are good at doing something. When what follows is <span class='gd-jp'>好き</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>嫌い</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>上手</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>下手</span>, use 「が」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>小説を読むのが好きです。</span> (I like reading novels.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母は料理を作るのが上手です。</span> (My mother is good at cooking.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は歌うのが下手です。</span> (I'm bad at singing.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>のは＋impression</span>Name an activity first, then say what you think of it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>写真を撮るのは楽しいです。</span> (Taking photos is fun.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語を話すのは難しいです。</span> (Speaking Japanese is difficult.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>朝ご飯を食べないのは体によくないです。</span> (Not eating breakfast is bad for your health.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>のを＋action</span>Make “that thing” the object of an action.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>宿題を出すのを忘れました。</span> (I forgot to hand in my homework.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんが来るのを待っています。</span> (I'm waiting for Tanaka to come.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Where people slip up</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Dropping the verb in as-is, the way English does with “-ing”: <span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐが好きです</span> is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐのが好きです</span>.<br>・You can't use the ます form before の. 「泳ぎますのが好きです」 is wrong. Keep the politeness in <span class='gd-jp'>です</span> at the end of the sentence.<br>・If the word is already a noun, don't add の: <span class='gd-jp'>音楽が好きです</span> (I like music). Don't say 「音楽のが好きです」.<br>・Before <span class='gd-jp'>好き</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>上手</span>, use 「が」. Don't switch to 「を」 just because “swimming” feels like the object of “like”.<br>・This 「の」 is different from the one in <span class='gd-jp'>私の本</span> (my book). There, a noun comes before it and it means “'s / of”. Here, a verb comes before it and it means “the act of”.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>「こと」 can also turn an action into “a thing”, and in many cases either one works:<br><span class='gd-jp'>本を読むのが好きです。</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>本を読むことが好きです。</span> (Both mean “I like reading books”; こと sounds a bit more formal.)<br><br>But some places allow only one of them:<br><b>For scenes you see or hear, only の works</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>田中さんが走っているのを見ました。</span> (I saw Tanaka running.)<br><b>For a sentence ending like “A is ~”, use こと</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>私の趣味は写真を撮ることです。</span> (My hobby is taking photos.) You can't say 「撮るのです」 here.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: use の for things you see or hear with your own eyes and ears. Use こと when “is doing ~” sits at the end of the sentence. For likes and for “fun or not”, both work, and の sounds the most natural in everyday speech.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Once you're comfortable with 「こと」, the next step is <span class='gd-jp'>〜ことができる</span> = can do ~ / be able to do ~: <span class='gd-jp'>私は泳ぐことができます。</span> (I can swim.)</div>","n5-57":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>English uses \"want\" for two different things: wanting a <b>thing</b> (I want a new phone) and wanting to <b>do</b> something (I want to go to Japan). Japanese keeps these apart. Use <b>「欲しい」</b> for things and <b>「〜たい」</b> for actions.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Wanting a thing</span>Noun + が欲しいです</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>新しいスマホが欲しいです。</span>(I want a new phone.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誕生日に時計が欲しいです。</span>(For my birthday, I want a watch.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Wanting to do something</span>First put the verb in ます form, drop 「ます」, then add 「たいです」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>行きたい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みたい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べたい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見たい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>したい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来たい</span>（きたい）</span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行きたいです。</span>(I want to go to Japan.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>温泉に入りたいです。</span>(I want to soak in a hot spring.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Watch the particles</div><div class='gd-sec'>Before <span class='gd-jp'>欲しい</span>, use <b>が</b>.<br>With <span class='gd-jp'>〜たい</span>, wherever the verb would normally take 「を」, you can keep 「を」 or switch to 「が」. Both are correct:<br><span class='gd-jp'>水を飲みたいです。</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>水が飲みたいです。</span>(I want to drink water.)<br>Other particles can't be swapped; they stay as they were: <span class='gd-jp'>友達に会いたいです。</span>(I want to see my friend.) Here 「に」 can't be changed to が.</div><div class='gd-h'>Negative and past: they conjugate like い-adjectives</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>欲しい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜たい</span> both end in 「い」, so they change exactly like い-adjectives:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>欲しいです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>欲しくないです</span></span> (don't want) <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>欲しかったです</span></span> (wanted, back then)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きたいです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行きたくないです</span></span> (don't want to go) <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きたかったです</span></span> (wanted to go, back then)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は何もしたくないです。</span>(I don't want to do anything today.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・たい attaches to the ます stem, not the dictionary form. 「行くたい」 is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>行きたい</span>.<br>・たい is already an adjective, so you can't add ます after it. Say <span class='gd-jp'>行きたいです</span>, not 「行きたいます」. For the negative, say <span class='gd-jp'>行きたくないです</span>, not 「行きたいません」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来たい</span> is read <b>きたい</b>.<br>・Both of these express <b>your own</b> feelings. Saying 「田中さんは車が欲しいです」 directly sounds odd to Japanese ears, because you can't feel what someone else feels. When talking about other people, say <span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは車が欲しいと言っていました。</span>(Tanaka said he wants a car.) Asking the other person in a question is fine: <span class='gd-jp'>何が欲しいですか。</span>(What do you want?)<br>・Asking a teacher, boss, or customer 「〜たいですか」 directly sounds like you're prying into what they want to do, which is not very polite. To offer someone a drink, <span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーはいかがですか。</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーを飲みませんか。</span> is more appropriate.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar-looking ones apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take one cup of coffee:<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーが欲しいです。</span> (I want coffee: what you want is the thing, coffee.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーが飲みたいです。</span> (I want to drink coffee: what you want to do is the action, drinking.)<br>English speakers most often get stuck on \"I want to go to Japan.\" The word \"want\" is right there, but what you really want to do is the action \"go\", so say <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行きたいです。</span> You can't use 欲しい.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: take out \"want\" and look at what follows. If it's a <b>noun</b> (a phone, coffee), use 欲しい. If it's \"to\" + a <b>verb</b> (to go, to eat, to see), use たい.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>It combines with 「に (purpose)」: <span class='gd-jp'>映画を見に行きたいです。</span>(I want to go see a movie.) Later you'll also learn how to say what someone else wants: <span class='gd-jp'>欲しがっている</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜たがっている</span>.</div>","n5-59":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜とき」 means <b>\"when ~\" / \"at the time of ~\"</b>. The difference from English is that Japanese puts 「とき」 at the very end, and everything before it describes that \"time\", for example <span class='gd-jp'>暇なとき</span> (when I'm free) and <span class='gd-jp'>子どものとき</span> (when I was a child).</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>「とき」 is itself a noun, so what comes before it attaches exactly the way you've learned for describing a noun.<br><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くとき</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないとき</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったとき</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忙しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忙しいとき</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒いとき</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add 「な」<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暇</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暇なとき</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>元気</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>元気なとき</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add 「の」<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子ども</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子どものとき</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休み</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休みのとき</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>暇なとき、何をしますか。</span>(What do you do when you're free?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どものとき、よくこの公園で遊びました。</span>(When I was a child, I often played in this park.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>わからないとき、先生に聞きます。</span>(When I don't understand, I ask my teacher.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>時間がないとき、コンビニでご飯を買います。</span>(When I don't have time, I buy a meal at a convenience store.)</div><div class='gd-h'>The most important point: 行くとき vs 行ったとき</div><div class='gd-sec'>Whether the verb before 「とき」 is in dictionary form or た form <b>doesn't depend on whether the whole sentence is present or past</b>. It depends on whether the action is <b>finished</b> at that point in time.<br><span class='gd-tag'>Dictionary form + とき</span>The action isn't finished yet: you're about to do it, or you're in the middle of it.<br><span class='gd-tag'>た form + とき</span>The action is already finished.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>出かけるとき、「いってきます」と言います。</span>(When you leave the house, you say 「いってきます」.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家に帰ったとき、「ただいま」と言います。</span>(When you get back home, you say 「ただいま」.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here's another pair. Both end in the past tense, but the meanings differ a lot:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に行くとき、辞書を買いました。</span>(When I was going to Japan, I bought a dictionary: before leaving or on the way.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に行ったとき、辞書を買いました。</span>(When I went to Japan, I bought a dictionary: after arriving in Japan.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The first sentence is clearly about the past, yet it still uses <span class='gd-jp'>行く</span>, because at the moment of buying the dictionary, you hadn't arrived in Japan yet.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Nouns need の: <span class='gd-jp'>子どものとき</span>, not 子どもとき.<br>・な-adjectives need な, and you can't use だ: <span class='gd-jp'>暇なとき</span>, not 暇とき, and not 暇だとき. Only in the past can you say <span class='gd-jp'>暇だったとき</span>.<br>・Do <b>not</b> add の after verbs and い-adjectives. Many learners, thinking of the noun pattern, say 「行くのとき」, which is wrong.<br>・Don't use ます or です before 「とき」: <span class='gd-jp'>行くとき、〜ます</span> is already polite. Don't say 「行きますとき」. Politeness is decided at the end of the sentence.<br>・When talking about habits or conditions, は is often added to make 「ときは」, as in <span class='gd-jp'>寒いときは、温かいお茶を飲みます。</span>(When it's cold, I drink hot tea.) It sounds very natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar-looking ones apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>The <span class='gd-jp'>〜まえに</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜たあとで</span> you've learned also involve time, and they're easy to mix up with 「とき」.<br><b>とき</b> = at that very time, during that process: <span class='gd-jp'>家に帰るとき、パンを買いました。</span>(I bought bread on my way home.)<br><b>まえに</b> = just \"before that\", no matter how long before: <span class='gd-jp'>家に帰るまえに、パンを買いました。</span>(I bought bread before going home.)<br><b>たとき</b> = the point when something is finished: <span class='gd-jp'>家に帰ったとき、母はいませんでした。</span>(When I got home, my mother wasn't there.)<br><b>たあとで</b> = after finishing, then doing the next thing: <span class='gd-jp'>家に帰ったあとで、宿題をしました。</span>(After I got home, I did my homework.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: to say \"at that moment\", use とき. To talk about order, use まえに or あとで.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>When the action before 「とき」 means \"when it's already done\", you use the た form, so make sure you're comfortable with <span class='gd-jp'>た形</span> conjugation. Later, at N4, you'll learn <span class='gd-jp'>〜たら</span> (if ~, after ~), which you'll often compare with 「とき」.</div>","n5-53":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English, listing a series of actions is easy: “I get up, brush my teeth, and leave,” with just commas and “and.” Japanese can't chain verbs like that. Every verb before the last one has to change to <b>て形</b>, which works like “...and then...”. Only <b>the last verb</b> decides whether the sentence is present or past, and whether it is polite or casual.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: just use the て form (see the 「て形」 lesson for how to form it).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きて</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行って</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んで</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: replace the final 「い」 with 「くて」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → replace い with くて → <span class='gd-jp'>安くて</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よくて</span></span> (memorize this exception)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: just add 「で」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かで</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生で</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In sequence</span>You finish one thing, then do the next, and you list them in the order they happen.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>朝起きて、歯を磨いて、出かけます。</span> (I get up in the morning, brush my teeth, then head out.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日は家に帰って、すぐ寝ました。</span> (Yesterday I got home and went straight to bed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Listing several features</span>When describing a person or thing, you give two features at once, like “both ... and ...” in English.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は安くて、おいしいです。</span> (This restaurant is both cheap and tasty.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は台湾人で、大学生です。</span> (I'm Taiwanese, and I'm a university student.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Manner or means</span>The first action is the “way” or “state” in which the second action happens.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>歩いて駅へ行きます。</span> (I go to the station on foot.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>傘を持って出かけます。</span> (I go out with an umbrella.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Reason</span>The first part is the cause, and the second part is usually a feeling, a state, or “can't do ~”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ニュースを聞いて、びっくりしました。</span> (I heard the news and was shocked.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>頭が痛くて、何もできませんでした。</span> (My head hurt, so I couldn't do anything.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Tense and politeness are decided <b>only by the last verb</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日、デパートへ行って、靴を買いました。</span> (Yesterday I went to the department store and bought shoes.) The earlier <span class='gd-jp'>行って</span> doesn't change to past; the past tense is left to the final <span class='gd-jp'>買いました</span>.<br>・The い-adjective form is <span class='gd-jp'>安くて</span>, not 「安いて」; and <span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>よくて</span>. The な-adjective form is <span class='gd-jp'>静かで</span>, not 「静かくて」.<br>・When you list two features, they should ideally point the same way (both good or both bad). For one good and one bad, like “cheap but not tasty,” use <span class='gd-jp'>が</span> instead: <span class='gd-jp'>この店は安いですが、おいしくないです。</span> (This restaurant is cheap, but the food isn't good.)<br>・When て expresses a reason, what follows <b>can't</b> be “let's ~,” “please ~,” or “I want to ~.” To say “It's raining, so let's take a taxi,” switch to <span class='gd-jp'>から</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っているから、タクシーで行きましょう。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with these</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three talk about what you did on the weekend:<br><span class='gd-jp'>映画を見て、ご飯を食べました。</span> (I watched a movie, then ate: simply telling things in order)<br><span class='gd-jp'>映画を見てから、ご飯を食べました。</span> (I ate <b>only after</b> the movie ended: the order is emphasized)<br><span class='gd-jp'>映画を見たり、ご飯を食べたりしました。</span> (I watched a movie, ate, and so on: just examples, order doesn't matter, and I may have done other things too)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use 「て」 to tell things one by one in order, 「てから」 to stress “only after finishing A, do B,” and 「たり～たり」 to give a few examples at random.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Many other common patterns attach to the て form, for example “please ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと待ってください。</span> (Please wait a moment.) and “be ~ing”: <span class='gd-jp'>今、ご飯を食べています。</span> (I'm eating right now.)</div>","n5-55":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜てください」 is the Japanese way to say <b>“please do ~,”</b> and 「〜ないでください」 means <b>“please don't ~.”</b> When a friend says “Wait a moment, please,” that's <span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと待ってください</span>, and when a sign says “No photos, please,” that's <span class='gd-jp'>写真を撮らないでください</span>. Both use these two patterns.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking someone for a favor</span>You ask the other person to do something for you.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もう一度言ってください。</span> (Please say it once more.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Instructions</span>You'll hear this a lot from teachers in class, from doctors giving advice, and when steps are being explained.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここに名前を書いてください。</span> (Please write your name here.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この薬を毎日飲んでください。</span> (Please take this medicine every day.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking someone not to do something</span>It works for reminders, prohibitions, and reassurance.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>心配しないでください。</span> (Please don't worry.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>教室で食べないでください。</span> (Please don't eat in the classroom.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: please do ~</div><div class='gd-sec'>First change the verb to its <b>て form</b> (the form from the 「て形」 lesson), then add <span class='gd-jp'>ください</span> directly.<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: the last kana decides how it changes.<br>・Verbs ending in う, つ, る → って: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待ってください</span></span><br>・Verbs ending in む, ぶ, ぬ → んで: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んでください</span></span><br>・Verbs ending in く → いて: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いてください</span></span><br>・Verbs ending in ぐ → いで: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>急ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>急いでください</span></span><br>・Verbs ending in す → して: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話してください</span></span><br>・Exception: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ってください</span></span> (not 行いて)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop 「る」 and add 「て」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べてください</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見てください</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (する・来る)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>してください</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来てください</span> (きてください)</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: please don't do ~</div><div class='gd-sec'>First change the verb to its <b>ない form</b>, then add <span class='gd-jp'>でください</span>.<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the matching あ-row sound and add ない.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>撮る</span> → change る to ら → <span class='gd-jp'>撮らない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>撮らないでください</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書かないでください</span></span><br>Verbs ending in う change to 「わ」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>吸う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>吸わないでください</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop 「る」 and add ない.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないでください</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (する・来る)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しないでください</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ないでください</span> (こないでください)</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・“Please don't” is ない followed by <b>で</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>見ないでください</span>, not 「見ないてください」, and there is no such form as 「見ませんでください」.<br>・Verbs ending in う change to 「わ」 in the ない form: <span class='gd-jp'>吸わない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>買わない</span>, not 「吸あない」.<br>・来る is read two different ways: <span class='gd-jp'>来てください</span> is read <b>きて</b>, while <span class='gd-jp'>来ないでください</span> is read <b>こないで</b>.<br>・Although it's translated as “please,” it is really <b>telling the other person to do something</b>. When asking a teacher or a boss for a favor, <span class='gd-jp'>〜てくださいませんか</span> (could you please ~?) sounds more polite.<br>・Among friends, ください is often dropped: <span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと待って。</span> (Wait a sec.) <span class='gd-jp'>見ないで。</span> (Don't look.)<br>・Shop staff often say <span class='gd-jp'>少々お待ちください</span> (please wait a moment) to customers. That's a more polite form of a different kind, not the て form from this lesson, so just learn to recognize it when you hear it.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with these</div><div class='gd-sec'>When <span class='gd-jp'>ください</span> comes after a <b>thing</b>, it means “please give me”; when it comes after a <b>verb in て form</b>, it means “please do.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>水をください。</span> (Please give me some water.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>水を飲んでください。</span> (Please drink some water.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: “noun + をください” asks someone for an item, while “て form + ください” asks someone to do an action.</div>","n5-65":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English we say \"a <b>sheet</b> of paper\" or \"a <b>piece</b> of cake\", with a little word between the number and the thing. Japanese does this too, and these words are called <b>counters</b>. The choice is based on shape, much like in English: flat, thin things take <span class='gd-jp'>枚</span>, and long, thin things take <span class='gd-jp'>本</span>. To say \"about,\" add <span class='gd-jp'>くらい</span> after the amount.</div><div class='gd-h'>Learn these counters first</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>つ</span>Works for most things. It's the all-purpose choice when you don't know which counter to use, and it's very common when ordering food. Learn 1 to 10 separately: <span class='gd-jp'>ひとつ、ふたつ、みっつ、よっつ、いつつ、むっつ、ななつ、やっつ、ここのつ、とお</span>. Past 10, you don't use つ anymore.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>りんごを3つ買いました。</span>(I bought 3 apples.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>人</span>For counting people. 1 and 2 people are special: <span class='gd-jp'>ひとり、ふたり</span>. From 3 on it's <span class='gd-jp'>さんにん</span>, and 4 people is <span class='gd-jp'>よにん</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家族は4人です。</span>(There are 4 people in my family.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>本</span>Long, thin things: pens, bottles, umbrellas, bananas.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>鉛筆を1本貸してください。</span>(Please lend me a pencil.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>枚</span>Thin, flat things: paper, stamps, shirts, plates.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>切手を5枚ください。</span>(Five stamps, please.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>匹</span>Small animals like cats, dogs and fish.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うちに猫が2匹います。</span>(I have 2 cats at home.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Other common ones: <span class='gd-jp'>冊</span> for books, <span class='gd-jp'>台</span> for machines like cars and TVs, and <span class='gd-jp'>杯</span> for cups or glasses of drinks.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to put them in a sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>The most natural order is: <b>thing + を (or が) + amount + verb</b>. The amount goes right before the verb, and you don't add another particle after it.<br>In English you say \"buy three apples,\" but in Japanese it's <span class='gd-jp'>りんごを3つ買います</span>: the thing comes first, then the amount.<br>To ask \"how many,\" replace the number with <span class='gd-jp'>何</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>何人</span> (なんにん), <span class='gd-jp'>何本</span> (なんぼん), <span class='gd-jp'>何枚</span> (なんまい). For things counted with つ, ask <span class='gd-jp'>いくつ</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>兄弟は何人いますか。</span>(How many siblings do you have?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling time</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>時</span> means \"o'clock,\" and <span class='gd-jp'>時間</span> means \"hours (duration)\": <span class='gd-jp'>2時</span> is two o'clock, and <span class='gd-jp'>2時間</span> is two hours.<br>The reading of <span class='gd-jp'>分</span> (minutes) changes, so memorize it: <span class='gd-jp'>いっぷん、にふん、さんぷん、よんぷん、ごふん、ろっぷん、ななふん、はっぷん、きゅうふん、じゅっぷん</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎日7時間寝ます。</span>(I sleep 7 hours every day.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅まで歩いて10分くらいです。</span>(It's about a 10-minute walk to the station.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>1時間くらいかかります。</span>(It takes about an hour.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>くらい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>ぐらい</span> mean the same thing, so you can use either one.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>The position of \"about\" is the opposite of English</b>: in English \"about an hour\" puts \"about\" first, but in Japanese it's <span class='gd-jp'>1時間くらい</span>, with くらい <b>after</b> the amount.<br>・Don't add another を after the amount: it's <span class='gd-jp'>りんごを3つ買いました</span>, not 「りんごを3つを買いました」.<br>・The readings of <span class='gd-jp'>本</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>匹</span> change: <span class='gd-jp'>1本</span>いっぽん, <span class='gd-jp'>3本</span>さんぼん, <span class='gd-jp'>6本</span>ろっぽん; <span class='gd-jp'>1匹</span>いっぴき, <span class='gd-jp'>3匹</span>さんびき.<br>・1 and 2 people are ひとり and ふたり, not 「いちにん」 or 「ににん」.<br>・4 o'clock is <span class='gd-jp'>4時</span> (よじ) and 9 o'clock is <span class='gd-jp'>9時</span> (くじ). Don't read them as よんじ or きゅうじ.<br>・Always use <span class='gd-jp'>人</span> to count people, never つ.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar words apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>English uses \"about\" for everything, but Japanese depends on what comes before it:<br><b>くらい</b> follows <b>how long or how much</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>3時間くらい待ちました。</span> (I waited about 3 hours.)<br><b>ごろ</b> follows a <b>point in time</b>, like a time of day or a date: <span class='gd-jp'>3時ごろ着きます。</span> (I'll arrive around 3 o'clock.)<br>In casual speech you'll also hear 「3時くらい」, but the reverse, 「3時間ごろ」, is wrong.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: use くらい for \"about how long / how much,\" and ごろ for \"around what time.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add <span class='gd-jp'>だけ</span> after an amount to mean \"only this much\": <span class='gd-jp'>1つだけください。</span> (Just one, please.)</div>","n5-66":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>Adverbs are little words placed before a verb or adjective to add <b>\"how much,\" \"how many,\" or \"how often\"</b>. The \"very\" in \"very tasty,\" the \"often\" in \"I often go,\" and the \"not much\" in \"I don't drink much\" are all adverbs in Japanese too. This card covers three groups: <b>degree</b>, <b>quantity</b>, and <b>frequency</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use them</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Degree: very</span><span class='gd-jp'>とても</span> = very, extremely. It goes before an adjective.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>とても楽しかったです。</span>(It was really fun.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このラーメンはとてもおいしいです。</span>(This ramen is very tasty.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Quantity: a lot</span><span class='gd-jp'>たくさん</span> = a lot, many. It's about \"amount.\" Put it before a verb, or use 「たくさんの＋noun」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日はたくさん食べました。</span>(I ate a lot yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅にたくさんの人がいます。</span>(There are a lot of people at the station.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Frequency: how often</span>From most often to never, they line up like this:<br><b>いつも＞よく＞ときどき＞たまに＞あまり～ない＞全然～ない</b></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いつも7時に起きます。</span>(I always get up at 7.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>よくこの店に来ます。</span>(I often come to this shop.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ときどき料理をします。</span>(I sometimes cook.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>たまに映画を見ます。</span>(I occasionally watch movies.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>テレビはあまり見ません。</span>(I don't watch TV much.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お酒は全然飲みません。</span>(I don't drink alcohol at all.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>The other meaning of よく</span>Besides \"often,\" <span class='gd-jp'>よく</span> can also mean \"well, thoroughly.\" The verb after it tells you which meaning it is.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日はよく寝ました。</span>(I slept well yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>すみません、よく分かりません。</span>(Sorry, I don't quite understand.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あまり</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>全然</span> must be followed by a <b>negative</b>: 「<span class='gd-jp'>あまり好きです</span>」 is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>あまり好きじゃないです</span> (I don't like it much).<br>・To say \"not very tasty,\" don't just put a negative after 「とても」. Use <span class='gd-jp'>あまりおいしくないです</span>.<br>・English \"very\" and \"a lot\" are close, but Japanese keeps them apart: use とても for \"very tasty\" and たくさん for \"a lot of people.\" Saying 「とても人がいます」 is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>人がたくさんいます</span>.<br>・You may hear young Japanese people say 「<span class='gd-jp'>全然大丈夫</span>」 (totally fine). That's casual speech. For textbooks and exams, stick to 「全然＋negative」.<br>・An adverb usually goes right before the verb or adjective it modifies: <span class='gd-jp'>よく行きます</span>. Don't put よく at the end of the sentence.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar words apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>あまり～ない</b> = not very much, but a little still happens: <span class='gd-jp'>お酒はあまり飲みません。</span> (I don't drink much; I have a little now and then.)<br><b>全然～ない</b> = not at all, none whatsoever: <span class='gd-jp'>お酒は全然飲みません。</span> (I never drink alcohol.)<br>If someone asks <span class='gd-jp'>よく行きますか。</span> (Do you go often?), answer <span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、あまり行きません。</span> if you go once in a while, and <span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、全然行きません。</span> only if you never go.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: use あまり for \"not much\" and 全然 for \"not at all.\" Both need a negative after them.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Adjectives can also become adverbs, for example <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>早い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>早く</span></span>: <span class='gd-jp'>早く起きます。</span> (I get up early.) Check out the lesson on using adjectives as adverbs next.</div>","n5-60":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜でしょう」works like English “probably…” or a tag question like “…, right?” With falling intonation, you're making a guess: “It'll probably rain tomorrow.” With rising intonation, you're checking with the listener: “It's delicious, isn't it?” It's the same phrase, but <b>intonation decides which of the two meanings you get</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Guess (falling ↓)</span>You're not 100% sure, just inferring. It's often paired with <span class='gd-jp'>たぶん</span> (probably), and weather forecasts love it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日はたぶん雨でしょう。</span>(It'll probably rain tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんはもう帰ったでしょう。</span>(Mr. Tanaka has probably already gone home.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Confirmation (rising ↑)</span>You think the other person probably feels the same way, so you check and wait for a nod.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このケーキ、おいしいでしょう？</span>(This cake is delicious, isn't it?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ほら、言ったでしょう？</span>(See? Didn't I tell you?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach it directly. Negative and past forms attach directly too.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降るでしょう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降らない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降らないでしょう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降ったでしょう</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒いでしょう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒くない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒くないでしょう</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: drop 「だ」 first, then attach.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静かだ</span> → drop だ → <span class='gd-jp'>静かでしょう</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生でしょう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨でしょう</span></span><br>The past 「だった」 stays: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だったでしょう</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After a noun or な-adjective, you can't keep 「だ」: <span class='gd-jp'>学生でしょう</span> is correct, and 「学生だでしょう」 is wrong.<br>・Don't put です before it: <span class='gd-jp'>寒いでしょう</span> is enough, and 「寒いですでしょう」 is wrong. でしょう is already the “guessing version” of です, so it's polite as it is.<br>・Don't use it for <b>your own plans</b>. 「私は明日行くでしょう」 sounds odd. For your own plans, just say <span class='gd-jp'>明日行きます</span>.<br>・The confirming 「でしょう？」 carries a “you surely know this, right?” feel, so it can sound a bit blunt toward a teacher or boss. To ask politely, use <span class='gd-jp'>〜でしょうか</span> (“could you tell me…?”), for example <span class='gd-jp'>駅はどこでしょうか。</span> (Where is the station, please?)<br>・Among friends it's often shortened to <span class='gd-jp'>でしょ？</span>. Don't be thrown when you hear it; the meaning is the same.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ましょう</b> and <b>でしょう</b> both end in 「しょう」, but they mean completely different things:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>一緒に行きましょう。</span> (Let's go together: an invitation)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんも行くでしょう。</span> (Mr. Tanaka will probably go too: a guess)<br>They attach differently too: ましょう goes after the ます stem (<span class='gd-jp'>行き</span>＋ましょう), while でしょう goes after plain forms such as the dictionary form (<span class='gd-jp'>行く</span>＋でしょう).</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>でしょう？</b> and <b>ね</b> can both be rendered as “…, right?”, and the difference is whether the other person is feeling it with you:<br>・Two people walking outside together: <span class='gd-jp'>寒いですね。</span> (It's cold, isn't it?: you both feel it right now, and you're just chiming in)<br>・A friend has just come in from outside and you're indoors: <span class='gd-jp'>外、寒いでしょう？</span> (It's cold outside, isn't it?: you weren't out there, you're guessing they felt cold and want them to agree)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: “Let's do ~ together” is ましょう. “Probably ~” or “~, right?” is でしょう. When you both feel it right now and are just agreeing, use ね.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>To express even less certainty, “might ~”, use <span class='gd-jp'>〜かもしれません</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨かもしれません。</span> (It might rain tomorrow.)</div>","n5-61":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜からです」means <b>“because…”</b>. When someone asks <span class='gd-jp'>どうして</span> (why), just state the reason and add 「からです」 to answer.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Answering “why”</span>The other person asks “why” first, and you give only the reason. There's no need to repeat what happened before.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どうして休んだんですか。</span>(Why did you take the day off?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>熱があったからです。</span>(Because I had a fever.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どうして日本語を勉強しているんですか。</span>(Why are you studying Japanese?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本のアニメが好きだからです。</span>(Because I like Japanese anime.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Adding the reason yourself</span>State one thing first, then add the reason in the next sentence.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日はパーティーに行きません。忙しいからです。</span>(I'm not going to the party today. It's because I'm busy.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Before 「から」, use the <b>form without ます or です</b> (the plain form you'd use with friends), and let 「です」 at the end handle the politeness.<br><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くからです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったからです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないからです</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いからです</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: add 「だ」 in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>好き</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>好きだからです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だからです</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Forgetting 「だ」 after な-adjectives and nouns: <span class='gd-jp'>好きからです</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>雨からです</span> are both wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>好きだからです</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>雨だからです</span>.<br>・The other way around, you <b>can't</b> add 「だ」 to い-adjectives: say <span class='gd-jp'>高いからです</span>, not 高いだからです.<br>・Don't put ます before 「から」: <span class='gd-jp'>熱がありましたからです</span> is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>熱があったからです</span>.<br>・English puts “because” at the start, before the reason, but Japanese 「から」 goes <b>after</b> the reason. Don't say から熱があった.<br>・With friends, just drop です: <span class='gd-jp'>熱があったから。</span> (Because I had a fever.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>You've already learned the particle 「から」 (cause/reason). It means the same as 「からです」, and the difference is <b>how the sentence is arranged</b>. For the same event:<br><span class='gd-tag'>〜から、〜</span>The reason comes first and the result second, all in one sentence.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>熱があったから、休みました。</span>(I had a fever, so I took the day off.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜からです</span>You state the result first and add the reason as a separate sentence, or you answer only the reason when asked “why.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>休みました。熱があったからです。</span>(I took the day off. It's because I had a fever.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if the reason is followed by “so, …”, use 「〜から、」. If the reason itself ends the sentence, use 「〜からです」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the result first and add 「のは」 to make the reason even clearer: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日休んだのは、熱があったからです。</span> (The reason I took yesterday off is that I had a fever.)</div>","n5-67":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>This card covers three kinds of small words that make Japanese sound more like <b>a real person talking</b>:<br><b>Connectives</b> = words like \"and then, also, but, so.\" They go at the start of the second sentence and link two sentences together.<br><b>Response words</b> = the little sounds you make when answering or starting to speak: \"yes, no, um…, uh…\"<br><b>Onomatopoeia and mimetic words</b> = words that imitate a sound or a look, like \"woof woof, splish-splash, beaming.\" Japanese uses these far more than English does.</div><div class='gd-h'>Connectives: linking two sentences</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's simple: finish the first sentence, put a connective at the start of the second, and usually add a comma「、」after it.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>そして</span>And, and then. Adds something in the same direction.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は安いです。そして、おいしいです。</span>(This shop is cheap, and the food is good too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>それから</span>And then, and also. Used for what happens next, or to add \"and also…\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>パンを買いました。それから、牛乳も買いました。</span>(I bought bread. Then I bought milk too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>でも</span>But. What follows goes against what came before.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語は難しいです。でも、おもしろいです。</span>(Japanese is hard, but it's fun.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>だから</span>So. The first sentence is the reason, the second is the result.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りました。だから、行きませんでした。</span>(It rained, so I didn't go.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Response words: answering and starting to speak</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>はい・いいえ</span>Yes / no.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「学生ですか。」「はい、そうです。」</span>(\"Are you a student?\" \"Yes, I am.\")</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>あのう</span>Used to get someone's attention or to start asking something, with a feel of \"sorry to bother you.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あのう、すみません。駅はどこですか。</span>(Um… excuse me, where is the station?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ええと</span>Used while you think as you speak, like English \"um…\" or \"let's see…\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ええと、三つください。</span>(Um… three, please.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Onomatopoeia and mimetic words: imitating sounds and appearances</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Onomatopoeia</b> imitates real sounds. <b>Mimetic words</b> use sound to describe a look or a feeling. They are usually two syllables repeated once.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨がザーザー降っています。</span>(The rain is pouring down: onomatopoeia)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>犬がワンワン鳴いています。</span>(The dog is barking woof woof: onomatopoeia)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お腹がぺこぺこです。</span>(I'm starving: mimetic)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Words like <span class='gd-jp'>ゆっくり</span> (slowly) and <span class='gd-jp'>はっきり</span> (clearly) are adverbs describing how an action is done. They don't imitate sounds, but they're just as common:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ゆっくり話してください。</span>(Please speak slowly.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・A connective goes at the <b>start of the second sentence</b>. You can't stick it in the middle: <span class='gd-jp'>高いです。でも、買います。</span> is fine, but you can't say 「高いでも買います」.<br>・Starting with <span class='gd-jp'>だから</span> and then 「言ったでしょう」, as in 「だから、言ったでしょう」, sounds like an impatient \"I told you so!\" Don't use it with people senior to you.<br>・When you just mean \"so,\" in formal situations or with seniors, you can swap <span class='gd-jp'>だから</span> for the more polite <span class='gd-jp'>ですから</span>.<br>・The response word <span class='gd-jp'>あのう</span> (also written <span class='gd-jp'>あの</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>あのー</span>) shares its origin with the demonstrative <span class='gd-jp'>あの</span> (that + noun), but they're used differently. To get someone's attention, 「あの、すみません」 or a slightly drawn-out 「あのう…」 both work. The drawn-out version adds a bit more hesitation and a \"sorry to bother you\" feel.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>はい</span> isn't only \"yes.\" When your name is called, answering <span class='gd-jp'>はい</span> means \"here\" or \"present.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>The same situation, \"It's expensive, but I'll buy it,\" can be said two ways:<br><span class='gd-jp'>高いです。でも、買います。</span> (two sentences, with <b>でも</b> opening the second)<br><span class='gd-jp'>高いですが、買います。</span> / <span class='gd-jp'>高いけど、買う。</span> (one sentence, with <b>が・けど</b> attached to the end of the first half)<br><br>\"It rained, so I didn't go\" works the same way:<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りました。だから、行きませんでした。</span> (two sentences, with <b>だから</b>)<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ったから、行きませんでした。</span> (one sentence, with <b>から</b> attached after the reason)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: <b>でも・だから</b> stand at the start of a new sentence; <b>が・けど・から</b> attach to the end of the previous clause. Check whether there's a period before it and you'll know which one to use.</div>","n5-25":"<div class='gd-h'>The short version</div><div class='gd-sec'>「から」means <b>“from”</b>. Use it for a starting time, a starting place, or the person something comes from. It often appears with 「まで」(until / to), and <span class='gd-jp'>AからBまで</span> means “from A to B.”</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Easy: <b>just add から right after the noun</b>. Nothing changes.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>9時</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>9時から</span></span>(from 9 o'clock)<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>駅</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>駅から</span></span>(from the station)<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>友達</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>友達から</span></span>(from a friend)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Starting point in time</span>Something starts at a certain time.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>仕事は9時からです。</span>(Work starts at 9.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日から夏休みです。</span>(Summer vacation starts tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Starting point in place</span>Where you set out from or come from.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どこから来ましたか。</span>(Where are you from? / Where did you come from?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>台湾から来ました。</span>(I'm from Taiwan.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>From a person</span>Who gave you something or who the news came from.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達から電話がありました。</span>(I got a call from a friend.)</div><div class='gd-h'>What you'll hear most: AからBまで</div><div class='gd-sec'>When you talk about a range, 「から」and 「まで」usually go together, for both time and place.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>月曜日から金曜日まで働きます。</span>(I work from Monday to Friday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家から駅まで歩きます。</span>(I walk from home to the station.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何時から何時までですか。</span>(From what time to what time?)</div><div class='gd-sec'>That last one is super handy for asking about a shop's opening hours or an event's schedule, and you'll definitely use it in Japan. When you don't need a range, 「から」on its own is perfectly fine too.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>The position is the opposite of English</b>: English puts “from” before the noun, but Japanese puts 「から」<b>after</b> the noun. “From the station” is <span class='gd-jp'>駅から</span>, not から駅.<br>・In English you might say “starting tomorrow.” In Japanese, <span class='gd-jp'>明日から夏休みです</span> is enough. 「から」already carries the meaning “starting from,” so don't try to translate “start” separately.<br>・To say “at what time you do something,” use 「に」, not 「から」. More on that below.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>① から (starting point) vs に (point in time)</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>9時に起きます。</span>(I get up at 9: just that one moment)<br><span class='gd-jp'>9時から勉強します。</span>(I study from 9: it continues for a while afterward)<br>“Getting up” is an instant action, so saying 9時から起きます would sound odd.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if you only say “at what time” something happens, use に. If you mean “starting then and continuing after,” use から.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>② から (starting point) vs から (reason)</b><br>At N5 you'll also learn another 「から」meaning “because.” They look exactly the same, so check <b>what comes before it</b>.<br>Starting point: <b>a noun plus から directly</b>, such as <span class='gd-jp'>駅から</span> (from the station).<br>Reason: it follows <b>a whole clause</b>. A noun needs だ or です first (<span class='gd-jp'>雨だから</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>雨ですから</span>), or it's a verb or い-adjective in plain or polite form (<span class='gd-jp'>行くから</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>行きますから</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>高いから</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>高いですから</span>).<br>Compare them with the same “tomorrow's test”:<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日からテストです。</span>(The tests start tomorrow.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日テストだから、勉強します。</span>(I have a test tomorrow, so I'll study.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if only a noun like a time, place, or person comes before から, it means “from.” If it follows だ／です, or a verb or adjective in plain or polite form (<span class='gd-jp'>行くから</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>行きますから</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>高いから</span>), it means “because.” (“Verb て-form + から” is a different grammar point meaning “after doing ~.” Don't mix them up.)</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Its partner 「まで」(until / to) is a good one to study alongside. Later you'll also learn “verb て-form + から” = “after doing ~, then ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>ご飯を食べてから、出かけます。</span>(I'll go out after eating.)</div>","n5-26":"<div class='gd-h'>The short version</div><div class='gd-sec'>「まで」means <b>“until ~” or “up to ~”</b>. It marks <b>where something ends</b>. The endpoint can be a time (until 5 o'clock) or a place (as far as the station). It often pairs with 「から」(from ~): <span class='gd-jp'>から</span> is the starting point and <span class='gd-jp'>まで</span> is the endpoint.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Time</span>You keep doing something until that point in time, and then you stop.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>5時まで待ちます。</span>(I'll wait until 5.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日は11時まで寝ました。</span>(Yesterday I slept until 11.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Place</span>How far you go.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅まで歩きます。</span>(I'll walk to the station.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>空港までバスで行きます。</span>(I'll take the bus to the airport.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Easy: just add まで right after the noun. Nothing changes.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>6時</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>6時まで</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>金曜日</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>金曜日まで</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>東京</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>東京まで</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>What you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>から～まで</span>From where to where, or from what time to what time. It states the whole range at once.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>9時から5時まで働きます。</span>(I work from 9 to 5.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>東京から大阪まで新幹線で行きます。</span>(I'll take the Shinkansen from Tokyo to Osaka.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>～までです</span>You can follow it directly with です. This is very common for opening hours and class times.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は夜10時までです。</span>(This shop is open until 10 p.m.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>いつまで・どこまで</span>Asking “until when” and “how far.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いつまで日本にいますか。</span>(How long will you be in Japan? / Until when will you be in Japan?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・まで means the action <b>keeps going</b> up to that point. So you can't translate “Be here by 5” as 「5時まで来てください」. That would come across as “keep coming until 5,” which is very odd. To say “come at 5,” use <span class='gd-jp'>5時に来てください</span>.<br>・「金曜日まで休みです」usually <b>includes</b> Friday, so it means “off through Friday.”<br>・まで only marks the endpoint. It doesn't carry the idea of “going” by itself, so you still need a verb after it, as in <span class='gd-jp'>駅まで歩きます</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>駅まで行きます</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に (point in time)</b> = at that time, something <b>happens once</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>11時に寝ました。</span>(I went to bed at 11.)<br><b>まで</b> = <b>keep doing something until</b> that time: <span class='gd-jp'>11時まで寝ました。</span>(I slept until 11.)<br>One small particle makes the difference: in one you go to bed at 11, in the other you don't get up until 11.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>までに</b> = <b>finish before</b> that time (a deadline): <span class='gd-jp'>5時までに帰ります。</span>(I'll be back by 5.)<br><b>まで</b> = keep going until that time: <span class='gd-jp'>5時まで待ちます。</span>(I'll wait until 5.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: “keep doing something until X” takes まで. “Get it done by X” takes までに. “Do it at X” takes に.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add に after まで and you get <span class='gd-jp'>までに</span>, which expresses a deadline: <span class='gd-jp'>金曜日までに出してください。</span>(Please hand it in by Friday.)</div>","n5-68":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AというB」means <b>\"a B called A\"</b>. Say you tell a friend, \"There's a shop called Shunsuidō in Taichung.\" In Japanese you use「という」to link the shop's name to the word for \"shop.\" Use it when the listener <b>may not know</b> the name.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Name (noun A) + という + what kind of thing it is (noun B)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>村田</span> ＋ <span class='gd-jp'>人</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>村田という人</span></span> (a person named Murata)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>さくら</span> ＋ <span class='gd-jp'>店</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>さくらという店</span></span> (a shop called Sakura)<br>B is usually a word like「人、店、町、食べ物、ドラマ、本」, which tells the listener what kind of thing the name refers to.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Introducing a name</span>Use it for a person, shop, work or food the listener may not have heard of.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅の前に「さくら」という喫茶店があります。</span> (There's a café called \"Sakura\" in front of the station.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>山田という人から電話がありました。</span> (There was a phone call from someone named Yamada.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>小籠包という食べ物を知っていますか。</span> (Do you know a food called xiaolongbao?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking a name</span>When you don't know what something is called, ask with <span class='gd-jp'>何という</span>. Here 「何」 is read <b>なん</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは何という花ですか。</span> (What is this flower called?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・という<b>must be followed by a noun</b>. You can't say「さくらというです」. Say the whole thing: <span class='gd-jp'>さくらという店です</span>.<br>・Don't use it for things everyone knows. Saying「富士山という山」to a Japanese person sounds like \"there's a mountain called Mt. Fuji, you've probably never heard of it,\" which is odd.<br>・「いう」is often pronounced <b>ゆう</b>, but you still write「いう」. Don't write「とゆう」.<br>・This「いう」is normally written in hiragana, not with the kanji「言う」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>という</b> = called / named ～: <span class='gd-jp'>村田という人</span> (a person whose name is Murata)<br><b>の</b> = belonging to ～ / from ～: <span class='gd-jp'>村田さんの友達</span> (Mr. Murata's friend)<br>In English, \"a person named Murata\" and \"Murata's friend\" are clearly different phrases, but learners often grab「の」for both because it feels like the easy \"link\" word. 「村田の人」would mean \"someone from the Murata family / Murata's side,\" not \"someone called Murata.\"</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, the <span class='gd-jp'>林といいます。</span> (My name is Hayashi.) you hear in self-introductions puts \"called\" at the <b>end of the sentence</b>, with no noun after it.<br><span class='gd-jp'>林といいます。</span> (My name is Hayashi.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>林という人がいます。</span> (There's a person named Hayashi.)<br>When you give your own name, <b>don't add さん</b>. 「林さんといいます」is wrong.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if English would say \"<b>called / named</b> ～\", use という. If it's just \"whose\" or \"from where,\" use の.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Asking \"How do you say this in Japanese?\": <span class='gd-jp'>これは日本語で何といいますか。</span> (What is this called in Japanese?)</div>","n5-30":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「に」 goes after a <b>person</b> and shows <b>who the action is directed at</b>. English changes the wording every time: \"give <b>to</b> mom,\" \"call a friend,\" \"ask the teacher,\" \"tell mom.\" Japanese is simpler: you just add 「に」 after the person.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The basic order is: <b>person + に</b>　<b>thing + を</b>　<b>verb</b>.<br>Say who first, then what, and put the action last:<br><span class='gd-jp'>母に</span> (to mom) + <span class='gd-jp'>花を</span> (flowers) + <span class='gd-jp'>あげます</span> (give) → <span class='gd-jp'>母に花をあげます。</span><br>If there is no \"thing,\" go straight to \"person + に + verb\": <span class='gd-jp'>友達に電話します。</span> (I call a friend.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Patterns you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving something to someone</span>Giving, lending, showing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母に花をあげます。</span> (I give flowers to my mom.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>妹に本を貸しました。</span> (I lent a book to my younger sister.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達に写真を見せます。</span> (I show photos to a friend.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Speaking to / contacting someone</span>Calling, writing letters, asking questions.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達に電話します。</span> (I call a friend.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生に聞きます。</span> (I ask the teacher.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんに手紙を書きました。</span> (I wrote a letter to Mr. Tanaka.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Teaching someone</span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>弟に英語を教えます。</span> (I teach English to my younger brother.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・In English you \"ask the teacher\" and \"meet a friend,\" with the person right after the verb as a direct object, so many learners reach for を. <span class='gd-jp'>先生を聞きます</span> is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>先生に聞きます</span>. <span class='gd-jp'>友達を会います</span> is also wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>友達に会います</span>. Some verbs do take を with a person, though, for example <span class='gd-jp'>友達を待きます</span>. Learn each verb together with an example sentence instead of guessing from English.<br>・The person after に is <b>not always the one who receives something</b>. With actions like \"borrow\" and \"learn,\" the に person is the source: <span class='gd-jp'>友達に本を借りました。</span> (I borrowed a book from a friend.) <span class='gd-jp'>先生に日本語を習います。</span> (I learn Japanese from my teacher.)<br>・「に」 has other uses too, such as time (<span class='gd-jp'>7時に</span>) and destination (<span class='gd-jp'>東京に</span>). Look at what comes before it: if it's a <b>person</b>, it's usually the usage on this card.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>English \"talk to\" and \"talk with\" are a good way to feel the difference between <b>に</b> and <b>と</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>友達に話します。</span> (I tell my friend something: I speak, they listen. It's <b>one-way</b>.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>友達と話します。</span> (I chat with my friend: you go back and forth, doing it <b>together</b>.)<br>Both are \"talking to a friend,\" but with に it's me addressing them, and with と it's the two of us chatting together.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can say \"with ～\" or \"together with ～\" in English, use と; if it can only be \"to,\" \"for,\" or \"at,\" use に.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Expressions for \"who gives what to whom,\" such as <span class='gd-jp'>あげます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>もらいます</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>くれます</span>, rely on this 「に」 all the time, so giving-and-receiving verbs are a good next step.</div>","n5-64":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>English has \"this\" and \"that\". Japanese has four series: <b>こ</b> = this (near me), <b>そ</b> = that (near you), <b>あ</b> = that over there (far from both of us), <b>ど</b> = which. Memorize these four starting sounds, then swap in different endings to point to things, places, and directions.</div><div class='gd-h'>Picture it first</div><div class='gd-sec'>You and a friend are standing on the street. You're holding a phone, your friend is holding a cup of coffee, and far away across the road there's a big building.<br>The phone is near you, so use <b>こ</b>. The coffee is near your friend, so use <b>そ</b>. The building is far from both of you, so use <b>あ</b>. If you don't know which one, use <b>ど</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>All four series together</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Pointing to things</span><span class='gd-jp'>これ・それ・あれ・どれ</span><br>These words can stand alone as the subject, followed directly by 「は」 or 「が」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは私の傘です。</span>(This is my umbrella.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>それ、何ですか。</span>(What's that you're holding?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あれは東京タワーです。</span>(That's Tokyo Tower.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Followed by a noun</span><span class='gd-jp'>この・その・あの・どの</span><br>These words can't be used alone. A noun must always follow.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この本、おもしろいですよ。</span>(This book is really good, you know.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そのかばん、かわいいですね。</span>(That bag of yours is so cute.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの人はだれですか。</span>(Who is that person?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Pointing to places</span><span class='gd-jp'>ここ・そこ・あそこ・どこ</span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>トイレはどこですか。</span>(Where is the restroom?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あそこにコンビニがあります。</span>(There's a convenience store over there.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Polite version</span><span class='gd-jp'>こちら・そちら・あちら・どちら</span><br>These can point to directions or places, and can also politely introduce people. Shop staff and receptionists use them a lot. When talking with friends, they become <span class='gd-jp'>こっち・そっち・あっち・どっち</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>受付はあちらです。</span>(The reception desk is over there.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>こちらは田中さんです。</span>(This is Mr. Tanaka.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この・その・あの・どの</span> can't be used alone. \"This is mine\" is <span class='gd-jp'>これは私のです</span>, not このは私のです.<br>・The word for \"over there\" (place) is <b>あそこ</b>, not あこ. It doesn't follow the pattern of the others in its set, so memorize it specially.<br>・English uses \"that\" for both, but Japanese keeps them apart: if the thing is next to the other person or in their hands, use <b>それ</b>. Only when it's far from both of you do you use <b>あれ</b>.<br>・When you change position, the word changes too. If the other person points at something in their own hand and asks <span class='gd-jp'>これは何ですか</span>, you answer <span class='gd-jp'>それは～です</span>, because the thing is on their side.<br>・Something the other person just mentioned is also referred to with a 「そ」 word: <span class='gd-jp'>それ、いいですね。</span>(That sounds good!)<br>・Use <span class='gd-jp'>こちら</span> to introduce people. Pointing at a person with <span class='gd-jp'>これ</span> treats them like an object, which is very rude.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar words apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>これ</b> and <b>この</b>: both mean \"this\"; the difference is what follows.<br><span class='gd-jp'>これは私の本です。</span>(This is my book.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>この本は私のです。</span>(This book is mine.)<br>In the first sentence, <span class='gd-jp'>これ</span> is followed by 「は」. In the second, <span class='gd-jp'>この</span> is followed by 「本」.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>どれ</b> and <b>どちら</b>: both mean \"which one\"; the difference is how many choices there are.<br><span class='gd-jp'>この三つの中で、どれがいいですか。</span>(Of these three, which is best?)<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーと紅茶、どちらがいいですか。</span>(Coffee or tea, which would you like?)<br>Use どれ when choosing among three or more things; use どちら when choosing between just two.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: if a noun comes right after, use この; if 「は」 or 「が」 follows, use これ. Pick one of three or more: どれ. Pick one of two: どちら.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The 「ど」 series is really a set of question words, so a good next step is \"question words\". Learn <span class='gd-jp'>だれ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>なに</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>いつ</span> together.</div>","n5-29":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>This 「で」 is the English <b>\"with / by / in\"</b> for tools, languages and methods. Eat with chopsticks, speak in Japanese, get in touch by phone: in Japanese you add 「で」 after the thing you use: <span class='gd-jp'>はしで食べます</span> (I eat with chopsticks).</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Tool</span>Something you hold or handle to do the action.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>はしで食べます。</span> (I eat with chopsticks.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ボールペンで書いてください。</span> (Please write with a ballpoint pen.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Language</span>The language you speak or write in.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語で話しましょう。</span> (Let's talk in Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは英語で何と言いますか。</span> (How do you say this in English?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Method</span>The means by which you do something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母と電話で話しました。</span> (I talked with my mom by phone.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ネットでチケットを買いました。</span> (I bought a ticket online.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Material</span>What something is made from, when you can still see the original material in the finished product.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>紙で花を作りました。</span> (I made a flower out of paper.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Easy: <b>just add 「で」 right after the noun</b>. Nothing changes.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>スプーン</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>スプーンで</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>英語</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>英語で</span></span><br>The thing being done still takes 「を」 before the verb, as usual: <span class='gd-jp'>スプーンでスープを飲みます。</span> (I drink soup with a spoon.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・English puts \"with\" <i>before</i> the noun, but Japanese puts 「で」 <b>after</b> it. English says \"with chopsticks\"; Japanese says 「はしで」. The order is reversed.<br>・Beginners often treat the tool as the object and say 「はしを食べます」, which means \"I eat the chopsticks.\" After a tool, use <b>で</b>.<br>・To ask \"with what?\", say <span class='gd-jp'>何で</span>, read <b>なにで</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>何で書きますか。</span> (What will you write with?) If you read it as なんで, in casual speech it is often heard as \"why?\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>で (place)</b>: where you do something: <span class='gd-jp'>教室で書きます。</span> (I write in the classroom.)<br><b>で (tool)</b>: what you do it with: <span class='gd-jp'>ペンで書きます。</span> (I write with a pen.)<br>You can put both in one sentence: <span class='gd-jp'>教室でペンで書きます。</span> (I write with a pen in the classroom.)<br>By the way, the 「で」 in <span class='gd-jp'>バスで行きます</span> (I go by bus) also means \"by what means,\" so it's the same idea as this card.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what comes before is a place, it means \"at / in ～\"; if it's a thing you use, a language or a method, it means \"with / by ～\".</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>日本語を話します</b>: you can speak Japanese. Japanese is something you know.<br><b>日本語で話します</b>: you speak using Japanese. Japanese is the means of communication.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は日本語を話します。</span> (I speak Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>店員と日本語で話しました。</span> (I talked with the shop clerk in Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: to say which language you speak, use を; to say which language you used this time to communicate, use で.</div>","n5-63":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Question words are the words you use to ask things: \"what, who, where, when, which, how much, how.\" They work much like English: <b>put the question word where the answer would go, and add か at the end</b>. Also, adding 「も」 or 「か」 after a question word changes the meaning completely. That's the key point of this lesson.</div><div class='gd-h'>The seven most common ones</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは何ですか。</span> (What is this?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの人は誰ですか。</span> (Who is that person?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>トイレはどこですか。</span> (Where is the restroom?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誕生日はいつですか。</span> (When is your birthday?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どれがいいですか。</span> (Which one is good?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これはいくらですか。</span> (How much is this?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本はどうでしたか。</span> (How was Japan?)</div><div class='gd-sec'>To be more polite with customers or elders: <span class='gd-jp'>誰</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>どなた</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>どう</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>いかが</span>. Shop staff often say <span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーはいかがですか。</span> (Would you like some coffee?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Is 「何」 read なに or なん?</div><div class='gd-sec'>Before <span class='gd-jp'>です</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>の</span>, or before a counter such as \"o'clock\" or \"people,\" it's read <b>なん</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>何ですか</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>何の本</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>何時</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>何人</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>何曜日</span>.<br>Before <span class='gd-jp'>を</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>が</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>も</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>か</span>, it's read <b>なに</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>何を食べますか</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>何も</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>何か</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Adding 「も」: none at all</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Question word + も + negative</span>This means \"not anything, not anyone, not anywhere.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何も飲みませんでした。</span> (I didn't drink anything.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰も来ませんでした。</span> (Nobody came.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日曜日はどこへも行きませんでした。</span> (I didn't go anywhere on Sunday.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Watch the particles: the original <span class='gd-jp'>を</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>が</span> are replaced by も (<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>何を</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>何も</span></span>), but <span class='gd-jp'>へ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>に</span> stay, placed before も: <span class='gd-jp'>どこへも</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>どこにも</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>誰にも</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Adding 「か」: some, unspecified</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Question word + か</span>This means \"something, someone, somewhere, some day.\" There is one, but you can't say which.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何か食べたいです。</span> (I want to eat something.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰かいますか。</span> (Is anyone there?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どこか静かな場所へ行きたいです。</span> (I want to go somewhere quiet.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いつか日本に住みたいです。</span> (Someday I want to live in Japan.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・When the question word is the subject, use <b>が</b>, not は: <span class='gd-jp'>誰が来ましたか。</span> You can't say 誰は来ましたか.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>いつも</span> does not mean \"never.\" It means \"always.\" It can take a positive or a negative: <span class='gd-jp'>いつも朝ご飯を食べます。</span> (I always eat breakfast.) / <span class='gd-jp'>いつも朝ご飯を食べません。</span> (I never eat breakfast. Literally, \"I always don't eat it.\")<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>何も</span> is read なにも, not なんも.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>どれ</span> is for choosing one out of three or more; for just two, use <span class='gd-jp'>どちら</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Say you're asking a friend what they drank. Changing one syllable changes the meaning:<br><b>何を</b> = asks \"what,\" so the answer is a thing: <span class='gd-jp'>何を飲みましたか。</span> (What did you drink?) → <span class='gd-jp'>お茶です。</span><br><b>何か</b> = asks \"did you or didn't you,\" so the answer starts with はい or いいえ: <span class='gd-jp'>何か飲みましたか。</span> (Did you drink anything?) → <span class='gd-jp'>はい、お茶を飲みました。</span><br><b>何も</b> + negative = \"nothing at all\": <span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、何も飲みませんでした。</span> (No, I didn't drink anything.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: to find out \"what,\" use 何を; to find out \"whether there was any,\" use 何か; to say \"none at all,\" use 何も + negative.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>\"This, that, which\" is easier to remember as a set, so go on to the demonstratives (こそあど): <span class='gd-jp'>どの本ですか。</span> (Which book is it?)</div>"});
